ADDART({id:"machupicchu",t:"Machu Picchu",c:"Countries and Places",i:"⛰️",w:"Machu Picchu",r:["perucountry","andes","aztec","maya"],
p:[["City in the clouds","Machu Picchu is a 15th-century Inca citadel built on a mountain ridge about 2,430 meters above sea level in the Andes of Peru."],["Masterful stonework","Its walls are made of stones cut so precisely that they fit together without mortar."],["Rediscovered","Little known outside the region for centuries, it became world famous after a visit by Hiram Bingham in 1911."]],
l:"Machu Picchu is an Inca site high in the Andes Mountains of southern Peru, above the Urubamba River valley. It was probably built around 1450 as a royal estate for the Inca emperor Pachacuti and was abandoned about a century later, around the time of the Spanish conquest. The Spanish never found it, so it survived largely intact. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the most famous archaeological sites in the world.",
s:[["The Inca Empire","The Inca built the largest empire in the Americas, stretching along the Andes from present-day Ecuador to Chile, with its capital at Cusco. They had no writing system as we know it, but they kept records with knotted cords called quipu and built roads of more than 30,000 kilometers to connect their lands."],["A royal estate","Most archaeologists think Machu Picchu was a retreat built for Pachacuti, the emperor who expanded the Inca state in the 1400s. Up to about 750 people may have lived there, including nobles, priests and servants. The site has temples, palaces, houses, plazas, fountains and storehouses."],["Building on a mountain","The Inca shaped the steep ridge with stone terraces for farming, which also stopped the soil from sliding. Underground drainage channels carried rainwater away. The finest buildings use ashlar masonry, stones cut so exactly that a knife blade cannot fit between them, which helps them survive earthquakes."],["Abandoned and forgotten","Machu Picchu was abandoned around the 1500s, perhaps because of the Spanish conquest, civil war or disease such as smallpox. Local farmers still knew about it, but the outside world did not. In 1911 the American explorer Hiram Bingham was guided there by local people and published photographs that made it famous."]],
k:[["Location","Cusco Region, Peru"],["Altitude","About 2,430 m"],["Built","c. 1450"],["Built by","The Inca, probably for Emperor Pachacuti"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1983"]],
d:[["c. 1450","Machu Picchu is built"],["1532","Spanish conquistadors arrive in the Inca Empire"],["c. 1570s","The site is abandoned by about this time"],["1911","Hiram Bingham publicizes the site"],["1983","Named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2007","Voted one of the New Seven Wonders of the World"]],
s2:[["The Temple of the Sun","The curved Temple of the Sun, or Torreón, is built over a rock cave. Its windows line up with the rising Sun at the June solstice. Nearby, the Intihuatana stone, a carved rock pillar, may have been used to track the Sun’s movement through the year."],["The Inca Trail","Many visitors hike the Inca Trail, a stone path built by the Inca, for four days through mountains and cloud forest, passing other ruins along the way. They arrive at the Sun Gate, Inti Punku, with a view over the whole site. Others take a train to the town below and a bus up the mountain."],["Huayna Picchu","The steep peak rising behind the ruins is Huayna Picchu, Young Mountain. The Inca built terraces and a temple even on its narrow summit. Climbing its stone steps is popular but dizzying, and only a limited number of hikers are allowed each day."],["Return of the objects","Bingham’s expeditions took thousands of objects, such as pottery and bones, to Yale University in the United States. After years of requests from Peru, Yale returned them between 2011 and 2012, and they are now displayed in Cusco."],["Protecting the site","Machu Picchu receives more than a million visitors in many years. To protect it from wear, Peru limits the number of tickets each day and requires visitors to follow set routes with guides. Landslides and heavy rains also threaten the site and the railway that serves it."],["Living Inca heritage","Quechua, the language of the Inca, is still spoken by millions of people in the Andes. Farming terraces, weaving and festivals such as Inti Raymi, the Inca Festival of the Sun held in Cusco every June, keep traditions of the Inca world alive."]],
sp:["The New Seven Wonders","In 2007 a worldwide public vote organized by a Swiss foundation chose the New Seven Wonders of the World: Machu Picchu, the Great Wall of China, Petra in Jordan, the Colosseum in Rome, Chichén Itzá in Mexico, the Taj Mahal in India and the statue of Christ the Redeemer in Rio de Janeiro. The Pyramids of Giza were given an honorary place."],
g:[["Inca","The people and empire of the Andes in the 15th and 16th centuries"],["Citadel","A fortress or stronghold on high ground"],["Terrace","A flat step cut into a hillside for farming"],["Quipu","Knotted cords used by the Inca to keep records"]],
f:["Machu Picchu stands about 2,430 meters above sea level.","Its finest walls were built without mortar.","The Spanish never found it."],
fun:"Some walls at Machu Picchu fit so tightly that a knife blade cannot slip between the stones.",
tl:"Ang Machu Picchu ay sinaunang lungsod ng mga Inca sa kabundukan ng Andes sa Peru, na itinayo noong mga 1450. Kilala ito sa mga pader na batong walang semento at isa sa New Seven Wonders of the World."});

ADDART({id:"angkor",t:"Angkor",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🛕",w:"Angkor",r:["hindu","buddh","mekong","vietnamcountry"],
p:[["Capital of an empire","Angkor was the capital of the Khmer Empire, which ruled much of mainland Southeast Asia from the 9th to the 15th centuries."],["The greatest temple","Its great temple, Angkor Wat, is the largest religious monument in the world."],["A city of water","The city was surrounded by huge reservoirs and canals that watered rice fields."]],
l:"Angkor, in northwestern Cambodia near the city of Siem Reap, was the capital of the Khmer Empire from the 9th to the 15th centuries. Its ruins spread over about 400 square kilometers and include hundreds of temples, among them Angkor Wat, built in the 12th century, and the temple-city of Angkor Thom with its smiling stone faces. Studies suggest that at its height Greater Angkor was one of the largest urban areas in the pre-industrial world.",
s:[["The Khmer Empire","The Khmer Empire was founded in 802, when King Jayavarman II declared himself a universal king. Over the next centuries, Khmer kings controlled lands across present-day Cambodia, Thailand, Laos and southern Vietnam. They built roads, rest houses and hospitals as well as temples."],["Angkor Wat","Angkor Wat was built in the early 1100s by King Suryavarman II as a temple to the Hindu god Vishnu and probably his own tomb. Its five towers, shaped like lotus buds, represent Mount Meru, the home of the gods. It is surrounded by a moat about 190 meters wide and is so important to Cambodia that it appears on the national flag."],["Angkor Thom and the Bayon","Around 1200, King Jayavarman VII, a Buddhist, built the walled city of Angkor Thom. At its center stands the Bayon temple, with more than 200 huge stone faces smiling calmly in every direction. Its walls are carved with scenes of battles, markets, fishing and daily life."],["A hydraulic city","The Khmer built enormous reservoirs called barays to store water from the monsoon rains. The West Baray is about 8 kilometers long. Canals and channels carried water to rice fields and around the city, supporting a large population. Laser surveys from aircraft have revealed traces of the city hidden under the forest."]],
k:[["Location","Near Siem Reap, Cambodia"],["Capital of","The Khmer Empire, 9th–15th centuries"],["Area","About 400 km² of archaeological park"],["Angkor Wat built","Early 12th century"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1992"]],
d:[["802","Jayavarman II founds the Khmer Empire"],["c. 1113–1150","Angkor Wat built under Suryavarman II"],["c. 1181–1218","Jayavarman VII builds Angkor Thom and the Bayon"],["1431","Ayutthaya armies sack Angkor; the capital later moves south"],["1992","Named a UNESCO World Heritage Site"],["2012","Lidar survey reveals hidden city plan"]],
s2:[["Carvings in stone","Angkor Wat has more than 1,000 square meters of bas-relief carvings. One famous panel shows the Churning of the Ocean of Milk, a Hindu story in which gods and demons pull on a giant serpent to stir up the elixir of immortality. Thousands of carved apsaras, heavenly dancers, decorate the walls."],["Hindu and Buddhist","Angkor’s kings followed Hinduism or Buddhism, and the temples reflect both. Angkor Wat was built for Vishnu, but over time it became a Buddhist temple and has been used by Buddhist monks ever since. Cambodia today is mostly Theravada Buddhist."],["Why Angkor declined","After the 1300s, Angkor declined. Wars with the kingdom of Ayutthaya, in present-day Thailand, changes in trade toward the coast and long droughts followed by floods that damaged the water system probably all played a part. The capital moved south toward Phnom Penh, though Angkor Wat was never fully abandoned."],["Ta Prohm","At the temple of Ta Prohm, giant silk-cotton and strangler fig trees grow over the walls and roofs, their roots wrapping around the stones. It was deliberately left much as explorers found it, to show how the jungle reclaimed Angkor. It appeared in the 2001 film Lara Croft: Tomb Raider."],["Restoration","During Cambodia’s civil war and the rule of the Khmer Rouge in the 1970s, Angkor was neglected and some sculptures were looted. Since the 1990s, teams from Cambodia and many other countries have worked together to restore the temples, and stolen statues have been returned from museums abroad."],["Sunrise at Angkor Wat","Angkor is Cambodia’s most visited site. Many visitors gather before dawn to watch the Sun rise behind the towers of Angkor Wat, reflected in the pools in front of it. The temples are also used for festivals, Buddhist ceremonies and performances of classical Khmer dance."]],
sp:["Angkor on the flag","Cambodia’s flag shows a white outline of Angkor Wat on a red band between two blue bands. Cambodia is one of very few countries to show a building on its national flag. The temple is a deep source of national pride, and its image also appears on banknotes, beer labels and the badges of schools and sports teams."],
g:[["Khmer","The main people and language of Cambodia"],["Baray","A large reservoir built by the Khmer"],["Bas-relief","A carving that stands out slightly from a flat surface"],["Lidar","A way of mapping the ground using laser pulses"]],
f:["Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument in the world.","The Bayon has more than 200 carved stone faces.","Angkor Wat appears on Cambodia’s national flag."],
fun:"Angkor Wat appears on the flag of Cambodia.",
tl:"Ang Angkor sa Cambodia ang kabisera ng Imperyong Khmer mula ika-9 hanggang ika-15 siglo. Naroon ang Angkor Wat, ang pinakamalaking monumentong panrelihiyon sa mundo."});

ADDART({id:"paris",t:"Paris",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗼",w:"Paris",r:["francecountry","frrev","impress","gothic","davinci"],
p:[["The capital of France","Paris lies on the River Seine in northern France and is the country’s political, economic and cultural center."],["City of art","It is home to the Louvre, the world’s most visited art museum, and was a world center of painting for centuries."],["Famous landmarks","The Eiffel Tower, Notre-Dame Cathedral and the Arc de Triomphe are known around the world."]],
l:"Paris is the capital and largest city of France, with about 2.1 million people in the city itself and more than 12 million in its metropolitan region. Built on both banks of the River Seine, it began as a settlement on an island over 2,000 years ago. Paris has been a world center of art, fashion, food, science and ideas, and it hosted the Summer Olympic Games in 1900, 1924 and 2024.",
s:[["From Lutetia to Paris","Around the 3rd century BCE, a Celtic people called the Parisii settled on an island in the Seine, now the Île de la Cité. The Romans conquered it in 52 BCE and called it Lutetia. By the 6th century it had become the capital of the Frankish kings, and in the Middle Ages it grew into one of Europe’s largest cities."],["Kings and revolution","French kings built palaces such as the Louvre and, outside the city, Versailles. In 1789 the people of Paris stormed the Bastille prison, beginning the French Revolution, which ended the monarchy and spread ideas of liberty and equality. Paris saw more revolutions in 1830, 1848 and 1871."],["Haussmann’s Paris","In the 1850s and 1860s, under Emperor Napoleon III, the official Georges-Eugène Haussmann rebuilt much of Paris. Narrow medieval streets were replaced with wide, tree-lined boulevards, parks, sewers and cream-colored stone apartment buildings, creating the look the city still has today."],["The Eiffel Tower","Built by Gustave Eiffel’s company for the World’s Fair of 1889, the iron Eiffel Tower is about 330 meters tall. Many artists hated it at first, and it was meant to stand for only 20 years, but it proved useful for radio and became the symbol of Paris. Millions of people climb it every year."]],
k:[["Country","France"],["Population","About 2.1 million (city), over 12 million (metropolitan area)"],["River","Seine"],["Famous landmarks","Eiffel Tower, Louvre, Notre-Dame, Arc de Triomphe"],["Olympic Games","1900, 1924, 2024"]],
d:[["52 BCE","Romans conquer the settlement of Lutetia"],["1163","Building of Notre-Dame Cathedral begins"],["1789","Storming of the Bastille begins the French Revolution"],["1889","Eiffel Tower opens for the World’s Fair"],["2019","Fire badly damages Notre-Dame"],["2024","Paris hosts the Summer Olympics; Notre-Dame reopens in December"]],
s2:[["The Louvre","The Louvre began as a fortress around 1200, became a royal palace and opened as a public museum in 1793, during the Revolution. Its collection includes Leonardo da Vinci’s Mona Lisa, the ancient Greek statue Venus de Milo and the Winged Victory of Samothrace. A glass pyramid by the architect I. M. Pei became its entrance in 1989."],["Notre-Dame","Notre-Dame de Paris, begun in 1163, is a masterpiece of Gothic architecture, with flying buttresses, rose windows and gargoyles. In April 2019 a fire destroyed its roof and spire. After five years of work by hundreds of craftspeople, it reopened in December 2024."],["Artists’ city","In the 1800s and early 1900s, artists from around the world came to Paris. The Impressionists, such as Claude Monet, painted its streets and parks, and later Pablo Picasso, Henri Matisse and many others worked in districts such as Montmartre and Montparnasse. Writers, musicians and filmmakers followed."],["Food and fashion","Paris is famous for cafés, bakeries selling baguettes and croissants, and restaurants of every kind. It is also a world capital of fashion, home to fashion houses such as Chanel and Dior, and hosts Paris Fashion Week twice a year."],["Getting around","The Paris Métro, opened in 1900, is one of the world’s oldest and busiest subway systems, famous for its Art Nouveau entrances. In recent years the city has added many bike lanes and cleaned the Seine so that people could swim in it again, a goal reached in 2025 for the first time in about a century."],["A diverse city","Paris is home to people from across France and the world, including large communities with roots in North and West Africa, the Caribbean, Asia and the rest of Europe. Its suburbs, called the banlieues, are home to millions, and the Grand Paris Express project is building new metro lines to link them."]],
sp:["Paris 2024","In 2024 Paris hosted the Summer Olympics and Paralympics, exactly 100 years after it last held them. The opening ceremony was held along the Seine instead of in a stadium, with athletes sailing past landmarks on boats. Events took place beneath the Eiffel Tower, at the Grand Palais and at the Palace of Versailles."],
g:[["Boulevard","A wide city street, often lined with trees"],["Gothic","A style of medieval architecture with pointed arches"],["Metropolitan area","A city together with its surrounding suburbs"],["World’s Fair","A large international exhibition of inventions and culture"]],
f:["The Eiffel Tower was meant to stand for only 20 years.","The Louvre is the world’s most visited art museum.","Paris has hosted the Summer Olympics three times."],
fun:"The Eiffel Tower was supposed to be taken down after 20 years.",
tl:"Ang Paris ang kabisera ng France, nasa pampang ng Ilog Seine. Kilala ito sa Eiffel Tower, sa museong Louvre at sa sining, pagkain at moda. Dito ginanap ang Olimpiyada ng 2024."});

