ADDART({id:"newyork",t:"New York City",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🗽",w:"New York City",r:["usacountry","trade","un","atlantic"],
p:[["America’s biggest city","New York City is the most populous city in the United States, with about 8.5 million people."],["Five boroughs","It is made up of five boroughs: Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx and Staten Island."],["A world city","It is home to the United Nations headquarters, Wall Street, Broadway and the Statue of Liberty."]],
l:"New York City lies at the mouth of the Hudson River on the Atlantic coast of the northeastern United States. With about 8.5 million residents and around 20 million in its metropolitan area, it is the largest city in the country and one of the world’s great centers of finance, culture, media and trade. Founded as the Dutch trading post of New Amsterdam in the 1620s, it grew into a gateway for millions of immigrants.",
s:[["New Amsterdam","The land was home to the Lenape people when Europeans arrived. In 1609 Henry Hudson, sailing for the Dutch, explored the river now named after him, and in the 1620s the Dutch founded New Amsterdam on the southern tip of Manhattan. In 1664 the English seized the colony and renamed it New York, after the Duke of York."],["Gateway for immigrants","From the 1800s, New York became the main port of entry to the United States. Between 1892 and 1954, about 12 million immigrants passed through the inspection station on Ellis Island. Irish, Germans, Italians, Jews from Eastern Europe, Chinese and many others built neighborhoods, and later waves came from the Caribbean, Latin America, Asia and Africa."],["The five boroughs","In 1898 the city of New York joined with Brooklyn and other areas to form the five boroughs. Manhattan, a narrow island, is the dense center of business and culture. Brooklyn and Queens are the most populous boroughs; the Bronx is the birthplace of hip hop; and Staten Island is reached by a free ferry."],["City of skyscrapers","New York helped invent the skyscraper. The Woolworth Building, the Chrysler Building and the Empire State Building, completed in 1931, each became the world’s tallest building in turn. Today One World Trade Center, about 541 meters tall including its spire, is the tallest building in the Western Hemisphere."]],
k:[["Country","United States"],["Population","About 8.5 million (city), about 20 million (metropolitan area)"],["Boroughs","Manhattan, Brooklyn, Queens, the Bronx, Staten Island"],["Tallest building","One World Trade Center, about 541 m"],["Languages spoken","Possibly more than 600"]],
d:[["1624","Dutch settlers found New Amsterdam"],["1664","The English take control and rename it New York"],["1886","Statue of Liberty dedicated"],["1898","Five boroughs unite into one city"],["1931","Empire State Building completed"],["2001","September 11 attacks destroy the World Trade Center"]],
s2:[["The Statue of Liberty","The Statue of Liberty, a gift from France designed by Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi, was dedicated in 1886 on an island in New York Harbor. Holding a torch and a tablet, the copper statue turned green over time. For immigrants arriving by ship, it was the first sight of their new country."],["Central Park","Central Park, designed by Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux and opened in the 1850s and 1860s, covers about 340 hectares in the middle of Manhattan. Its lakes, meadows, woods and paths are visited by tens of millions of people every year and inspired city parks around the world."],["Money and the world","Wall Street, in lower Manhattan, is home to the New York Stock Exchange and has become a name for the whole American financial industry. The headquarters of the United Nations stands beside the East River, where leaders from nearly every country meet each year for the General Assembly."],["Arts and entertainment","Broadway theaters stage musicals and plays seen by millions of visitors. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, the Museum of Modern Art and the American Museum of Natural History are among the world’s great museums. Jazz, hip hop, salsa and punk all grew in New York’s neighborhoods."],["September 11","On 11 September 2001, terrorists flew hijacked planes into the twin towers of the World Trade Center, which collapsed, killing nearly 3,000 people in New York, Washington and Pennsylvania. A memorial with two reflecting pools now marks where the towers stood, beside the new One World Trade Center."],["A city of many languages","New York is one of the most linguistically diverse cities on Earth. Researchers estimate that more than 600 languages are spoken there, and over a third of its residents were born outside the United States. Its subway, one of the largest in the world, runs 24 hours a day."]],
sp:["The subway","The New York City Subway opened in 1904 and now has 472 stations, more than any other metro system in the world. It carries several million riders on a typical weekday and runs around the clock, which few metros do. Many lines have express and local tracks side by side, so some trains skip stations to travel faster."],
g:[["Borough","One of the five main divisions of New York City"],["Immigrant","A person who moves to live in another country"],["Skyscraper","A very tall building with many floors"],["Stock exchange","A market where shares in companies are bought and sold"]],
f:["About 12 million immigrants passed through Ellis Island.","The subway has 472 stations, more than any other system.","More than 600 languages may be spoken in New York."],
fun:"The New York City Subway has more stations than any other metro system in the world.",
tl:"Ang New York City ang pinakamalaking lungsod sa Estados Unidos, may limang borough kabilang ang Manhattan. Kilala ito sa Statue of Liberty, sa mga skyscraper, sa Broadway at sa punong-tanggapan ng United Nations."});

ADDART({id:"sydney",t:"Sydney",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🎭",w:"Sydney",r:["australiacountry","nzcountry","pacific","reef"],
p:[["Australia’s largest city","Sydney, on the southeast coast, is Australia’s largest and oldest city, with more than 5 million people."],["A great harbor","It is built around Sydney Harbour, one of the world’s finest natural harbors."],["Famous icons","The Sydney Opera House and the Sydney Harbour Bridge are among the most recognized structures on Earth."]],
l:"Sydney is the capital of the state of New South Wales and the largest city in Australia, with a population of about 5.5 million. It lies around Port Jackson, better known as Sydney Harbour, on the Pacific coast. The land has been home to Aboriginal peoples, including the Gadigal of the Eora Nation, for tens of thousands of years. Sydney began as a British penal colony in 1788 and has grown into a multicultural global city.",
s:[["First peoples","Aboriginal people have lived in the Sydney region for at least 30,000 years, and perhaps much longer. The Eora peoples fished in the harbor in bark canoes and left rock engravings and paintings around the coast. Many places in Sydney keep Aboriginal names, such as Parramatta, Woolloomooloo and Bondi."],["The First Fleet","In January 1788, eleven British ships known as the First Fleet arrived in Sydney Cove with about 1,500 people, around half of them convicts. Their landing on 26 January is marked as Australia Day, though many Aboriginal Australians call it Invasion Day or Survival Day, because it began the loss of their lands. Disease and conflict devastated local communities."],["From colony to city","The convict colony slowly grew into a trading port. Gold rushes in the 1850s brought new wealth and settlers. In 1901, when Australia’s colonies joined into one nation, Sydney and Melbourne were rivals to be capital, so a new capital, Canberra, was built between them."],["Opera House","The Sydney Opera House, designed by the Danish architect Jørn Utzon, opened in 1973. Its white roofs look like sails or shells and are covered in more than a million tiles. Building it was so difficult and costly that Utzon left the project in 1966. It was named a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2007."]],
k:[["Country","Australia"],["State","New South Wales (capital)"],["Population","About 5.5 million"],["Founded","1788 as a British colony"],["Famous landmarks","Opera House, Harbour Bridge, Bondi Beach"]],
d:[["1770","James Cook lands at Botany Bay, south of the harbor"],["1788","The First Fleet arrives in Sydney Cove"],["1932","Sydney Harbour Bridge opens"],["1973","Sydney Opera House opens"],["2000","Sydney hosts the Summer Olympics"],["2007","Opera House named a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["The Harbour Bridge","The steel arch of the Sydney Harbour Bridge, opened in 1932, is nicknamed the Coathanger. It carries trains, cars, bicycles and people across the harbor. Visitors can climb to the top of the arch, about 134 meters above the water, on guided climbs."],["Beaches","Sydney has dozens of ocean beaches, from Bondi and Manly to Coogee and Cronulla. Surf lifesaving clubs, whose volunteers patrol the beaches, began here in the early 1900s. Ocean pools cut into rocks along the shore let swimmers enjoy the sea in safety."],["New Year’s Eve","Sydney is one of the first major cities in the world to welcome the New Year, and its fireworks over the harbor, launched from the Harbour Bridge and from barges, are watched by more than a million people on the shore and by many more on television."],["Multicultural Sydney","More than a third of Sydney’s people were born overseas. After World War II, immigrants came from Britain, Italy, Greece and Lebanon, and later from Vietnam, China, India, the Philippines and many other countries. Suburbs such as Cabramatta, Harris Park and Leichhardt are known for the food of these communities."],["Nature in the city","Sydney is surrounded by national parks. The Royal National Park, created in 1879 to the south, is the world’s second-oldest national park. To the west, the Blue Mountains are named for the blue haze given off by their eucalyptus forests."],["The 2000 Olympics","Sydney hosted the Summer Olympics in 2000. The Aboriginal athlete Cathy Freeman lit the cauldron at the opening ceremony and later won gold in the 400 meters, a moment many Australians remember. The games were praised for their organization and friendly atmosphere."]],
sp:["Building the Opera House","In 1957 Jørn Utzon won an international design competition with sketches of sail-like roofs, but no one knew how to build them. After years of trials, Utzon found a solution: every roof shell is a piece cut from the same imaginary sphere, so they could all be made from standard parts. The building cost about 14 times its first estimate, yet it became one of the most admired buildings of the 20th century."],
g:[["Penal colony","A settlement where convicts were sent as punishment"],["Harbor","A sheltered stretch of water where ships can anchor"],["Eora","Aboriginal peoples of the Sydney coastal area"],["Multicultural","Made up of many cultures"]],
f:["The Opera House roofs are covered in more than a million tiles.","The Harbour Bridge is nicknamed the Coathanger.","Canberra was built as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne."],
fun:"The Sydney Harbour Bridge is nicknamed the Coathanger.",
tl:"Ang Sydney ang pinakamalaking lungsod ng Australia at nasa paligid ng Sydney Harbour. Kilala ito sa Sydney Opera House at Harbour Bridge. Matagal na itong tahanan ng mga Aboriginal na tao bago dumating ang mga Briton noong 1788."});

ADDART({id:"greenland",t:"Greenland",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🧊",w:"Greenland",r:["arctic","climchange","iceland","viking"],
p:[["The largest island","Greenland is the world’s largest island that is not a continent, about 2.2 million square kilometers in area."],["Under the ice","About four-fifths of it is covered by an ice sheet up to about 3 kilometers thick."],["Inuit homeland","Most of its 57,000 people are Inuit, and it is a self-governing territory within the Kingdom of Denmark."]],
l:"Greenland lies between the Arctic and Atlantic Oceans, northeast of Canada. It is the largest island in the world, yet one of the least populated places on Earth, with about 57,000 people, most of them Kalaallit, or Greenlandic Inuit. Its huge ice sheet holds about a tenth of the world’s fresh water. Greenland is an autonomous territory of the Kingdom of Denmark, with its own government in the capital, Nuuk.",
s:[["Land of ice","The Greenland ice sheet covers about 1.7 million square kilometers and in places is more than 3 kilometers thick. Its weight presses the rock beneath below sea level. At the edges, glaciers flow down to the sea and break off into icebergs. If all of it melted, global sea levels would rise by about 7 meters."],["First peoples","People reached Greenland from North America about 4,500 years ago. Several cultures came and went, and the ancestors of today’s Inuit, the Thule people, arrived around the 1200s. They hunted seals, whales and walruses from kayaks and traveled by dog sled across the ice."],["The Norse","Around 985, Erik the Red led Norse settlers from Iceland to southwestern Greenland, giving the island its inviting name to attract farmers. The Norse colonies lasted about 450 years before disappearing in the 1400s, perhaps because of a colder climate, falling trade and isolation."],["Danish rule and self-government","A Danish-Norwegian missionary, Hans Egede, arrived in 1721, beginning colonial rule. Greenland became part of Denmark in 1953. It gained home rule in 1979 and wider self-government in 2009, which recognized Greenlanders as a people with the right to self-determination. Denmark still handles defense and foreign policy."]],
k:[["Status","Autonomous territory within the Kingdom of Denmark"],["Capital","Nuuk"],["Area","About 2.2 million km²"],["Population","About 57,000"],["Languages","Greenlandic (Kalaallisut), Danish"]],
d:[["c. 2500 BCE","First people arrive from North America"],["c. 985","Erik the Red founds Norse settlements"],["c. 1200s","Thule ancestors of the Inuit spread across Greenland"],["1721","Hans Egede’s mission begins Danish colonial rule"],["1979","Home rule established"],["2009","Self-government expanded; Greenlandic becomes the official language"]],
s2:[["Life in Greenland","No roads link Greenland’s towns, so people travel by boat, plane, helicopter, snowmobile or dog sled. Most live along the southwestern coast, where the sea stays open longer. Fishing, especially for shrimp and halibut, provides most of the country’s exports."],["Northern lights and midnight sun","North of the Arctic Circle, the Sun does not set for weeks in summer and does not rise for weeks in winter. On dark winter nights, the aurora borealis, or northern lights, ripples across the sky in green and purple curtains."],["Melting ice","Greenland’s ice sheet has been losing ice since the 1990s as temperatures rise, and its melting is now one of the biggest causes of rising seas. Scientists drill ice cores, which hold air bubbles trapped for more than 100,000 years, to learn about past climates."],["Ilulissat Icefjord","The Ilulissat Icefjord on the west coast, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is fed by Sermeq Kujalleq, one of the fastest-moving glaciers in the world. Huge icebergs drift out of the fjord into Disko Bay. An iceberg from this region may have been the one that sank the Titanic in 1912."],["Greenlandic culture","Greenlanders keep traditions such as drum dancing, mask dancing and kayak building, and the national dress is decorated with tiny glass beads. Their language, Kalaallisut, is related to Inuit languages in Canada. The national day, 21 June, is the longest day of the year."],["A place in world affairs","Greenland’s location between North America and Europe makes it important for defense, and the United States has operated a military base there since World War II. Its minerals and the opening of Arctic sea routes as ice melts have drawn growing international interest, and Greenlanders debate their future, including possible full independence."]],
sp:["Why green?","According to old Icelandic sagas, Erik the Red named the land Greenland because people would be more eager to go there if it had a good name. His settlements were in sheltered southern fjords that are green in summer, but most of the island is white with ice. In Greenlandic, its name is Kalaallit Nunaat, Land of the Greenlanders."],
g:[["Ice sheet","A huge, thick layer of ice covering land"],["Inuit","Indigenous peoples of the Arctic"],["Fjord","A long, narrow inlet of the sea between cliffs"],["Autonomous","Self-governing"]],
f:["Greenland is the largest island in the world.","If its ice sheet melted, seas would rise by about 7 meters.","No roads connect its towns."],
fun:"There are no roads between towns in Greenland.",
tl:"Ang Greenland ang pinakamalaking pulo sa mundo, karamihang natatakpan ng makapal na yelo. Karamihan sa mga 57,000 naninirahan dito ay Inuit, at ito ay may sariling pamahalaan sa ilalim ng Kaharian ng Denmark."});

;
