addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{Object.assign(window.LOC=window.LOC||{},{hawaii:[-157,20.5,"",24,"Hawaii, United States"],galapagos:[-90.5,-0.6,"Ecuador",30,"Galápagos Islands"],borneo:[114,1,"",24,"Borneo (Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei)"]})});

ADDART({id:"hawaii",t:"Hawaii",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🌺",w:"Hawaii",r:["volc","pacific","usacountry","nzcountry"],
p:[["Islands of fire","The Hawaiian Islands were built by volcanoes rising from the floor of the Pacific Ocean over a hot spot in the Earth’s mantle."],["Polynesian homeland","Polynesian voyagers settled the islands more than 1,000 years ago, sailing thousands of kilometers by canoe."],["The 50th state","Once an independent kingdom, Hawaii became the 50th state of the United States in 1959."]],
l:"Hawaii is a chain of volcanic islands in the middle of the North Pacific Ocean, about 3,800 kilometers southwest of California. Its eight main islands include Hawaiʻi, often called the Big Island, Maui, Oʻahu and Kauaʻi. First settled by Polynesians, Hawaii was a kingdom until 1893 and became a US state in 1959. It is home to about 1.4 million people, active volcanoes, coral reefs and many plants and animals found nowhere else.",
s:[["Born of volcanoes","Hawaii sits over a hot spot, a place where molten rock rises from deep in the Earth. As the Pacific Plate slowly moves northwest over it, new volcanoes form and older ones drift away, wear down and sink. That is why the oldest main island, Kauaʻi, lies in the northwest, and the youngest, the Big Island, is still growing."],["Polynesian voyagers","Polynesian navigators reached Hawaii, probably from the Marquesas and Society Islands, sometime between about 1000 and 1200 CE, in double-hulled voyaging canoes. They found their way by reading the stars, the swells of the ocean, clouds and the flight of birds, and brought plants such as taro, breadfruit and coconut."],["The Hawaiian Kingdom","Chiefs ruled separate islands until Kamehameha I united them by 1810, founding the Kingdom of Hawaii. The kingdom traded with the world, adopted a written constitution and was recognized by many countries. In 1893 American businessmen, backed by US Marines, overthrew Queen Liliʻuokalani, and the United States annexed the islands in 1898."],["Statehood","Hawaii became a US territory and then, in 1959, the 50th state. On 7 December 1941, Japan’s surprise attack on the naval base at Pearl Harbor, on Oʻahu, brought the United States into World War II. Today tourism, the military and agriculture are major parts of Hawaii’s economy."]],
k:[["Status","US state since 1959"],["Capital","Honolulu, on Oʻahu"],["Population","About 1.4 million"],["Highest point","Mauna Kea, 4,207 m above sea level"],["Languages","English and Hawaiian (both official)"]],
d:[["c. 1000–1200","Polynesian voyagers settle the islands"],["1778","British explorer James Cook arrives"],["1810","Kamehameha I unites the islands"],["1893","Queen Liliʻuokalani is overthrown"],["1941","Japan attacks Pearl Harbor"],["1959","Hawaii becomes the 50th US state"]],
s2:[["Mauna Kea and Mauna Loa","Measured from its base on the ocean floor, Mauna Kea is more than 10,000 meters tall, taller than Mount Everest from base to peak. Its summit holds major astronomical observatories, which many Native Hawaiians oppose because the mountain is sacred. Neighboring Mauna Loa is the largest active volcano on Earth by volume."],["Kīlauea","Kīlauea, on the Big Island, is one of the most active volcanoes in the world. Its lava often flows into the sea, adding new land. Hawaiian tradition says it is the home of Pele, goddess of volcanoes and fire. In 2018 a long eruption destroyed more than 700 homes."],["Unique wildlife","Because Hawaii is so isolated, most of its native plants and many of its animals evolved there and live nowhere else, such as the honeycreeper birds and the nēnē, a goose that is the state bird. Many species have become extinct or endangered since people brought rats, pigs, mosquitoes and other animals."],["Hawaiian culture","Hula, a dance that tells stories through movement and chant, is at the heart of Hawaiian culture. The Hawaiian language was banned in schools in 1896 and nearly disappeared, but since the 1970s a cultural renaissance has revived it through immersion schools, music and the arts."],["Surfing","Surfing, called heʻe nalu, was practiced by Hawaiians of all ranks for centuries. The Olympic swimmer Duke Kahanamoku, from Honolulu, spread surfing around the world in the early 1900s. The North Shore of Oʻahu, with its giant winter waves, is one of the world’s most famous surfing spots."],["The Hōkūleʻa","In 1976 the voyaging canoe Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti without modern instruments, guided by the Micronesian navigator Mau Piailug. The voyage proved that ancient Polynesians could have navigated the Pacific deliberately. From 2014 to 2017, Hōkūleʻa sailed around the world."]],
sp:["The Lahaina fire","In August 2023, a wildfire driven by strong winds swept through the historic town of Lahaina on Maui, once the royal capital of the Hawaiian Kingdom. About 100 people died, making it the deadliest wildfire in the United States in more than a century, and most of the town was destroyed. Rebuilding has been slow, and the disaster raised questions about land, water and housing in Hawaii."],
g:[["Hot spot","A place where molten rock rises from deep within the Earth"],["Annex","To take control of a territory and make it part of a country"],["Hula","A traditional Hawaiian dance"],["Navigator","A person who finds the way on a journey"]],
f:["Hawaii is the only US state made up entirely of islands.","From its base on the sea floor, Mauna Kea is taller than Everest.","The Hawaiian alphabet has only 13 letters."],
fun:"The Hawaiian alphabet has only 13 letters, including the ʻokina.",
tl:"Ang Hawaii ay kapuluang bulkaniko sa gitna ng Karagatang Pasipiko na unang tinirhan ng mga Polynesian. Dating kaharian, ito ay naging ika-50 estado ng Estados Unidos noong 1959."});

ADDART({id:"galapagos",t:"Galápagos Islands",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🐢",w:"Galápagos Islands",r:["darwin","evo","turtle","birds"],
p:[["Islands of evolution","Animals on the Galápagos helped Charles Darwin form his theory of evolution by natural selection."],["Named for tortoises","The islands are named after their giant tortoises: galápago is an old Spanish word for tortoise."],["Part of Ecuador","They lie in the Pacific about 1,000 kilometers west of mainland Ecuador, which governs them."]],
l:"The Galápagos Islands are a volcanic archipelago straddling the equator in the eastern Pacific Ocean, belonging to Ecuador. Isolated far from any continent, they are home to animals found nowhere else, including giant tortoises, marine iguanas, flightless cormorants and Galápagos penguins. Charles Darwin’s visit in 1835 inspired ideas that changed biology. Most of the islands are a national park, and the surrounding waters are one of the world’s largest marine reserves.",
s:[["Volcanic islands","The islands formed over a hot spot in the Earth’s mantle, like Hawaii. The oldest are a few million years old, while western islands such as Isabela and Fernandina are still volcanically active. The archipelago has 13 large islands and many smaller ones, and about 30,000 people live on four of them."],["How animals arrived","No animals lived on the islands when they rose from the sea. Seeds, insects and birds were carried by wind and waves, and reptiles probably drifted on rafts of plants from South America. Ocean currents bring cold, nutrient-rich water from the south, which is why penguins live on the equator."],["Darwin’s visit","Charles Darwin spent five weeks on the islands in 1835 during the voyage of HMS Beagle. He noticed that mockingbirds and tortoises differed from island to island. Back in England, he realized that species could change over time as they adapted to different conditions, and he published On the Origin of Species in 1859."],["Darwin’s finches","The islands have more than a dozen species of finches, each with a beak suited to a different food, such as seeds, insects or cactus flowers. They probably descended from a single kind of finch that arrived long ago. Biologists Peter and Rosemary Grant have studied them since 1973 and watched their beaks change after droughts."]],
k:[["Country","Ecuador"],["Location","Eastern Pacific, about 1,000 km from mainland Ecuador"],["Islands","13 large islands and many smaller ones"],["Population","About 30,000"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1978, among the first sites listed"]],
d:[["1535","Spanish bishop Tomás de Berlanga reaches the islands by accident"],["1832","Ecuador claims the islands"],["1835","Charles Darwin visits on HMS Beagle"],["1959","Galápagos National Park created"],["1978","One of the first UNESCO World Heritage Sites"],["2012","Lonesome George, last Pinta Island tortoise, dies"]],
s2:[["Giant tortoises","Galápagos giant tortoises can weigh more than 250 kilograms and live well over 100 years. Tortoises on dry islands have shells raised at the front, like a saddle, so they can stretch their necks up to reach cactus. Whalers and pirates once took hundreds of thousands of them as food, and several kinds died out."],["Marine iguanas","Marine iguanas are the only lizards in the world that feed in the sea. They dive to eat seaweed from rocks, then bask on black lava to warm up again. They sneeze out salt through glands in their noses, leaving white crusts on their heads."],["Birds of the islands","Blue-footed boobies perform dances to show off their bright blue feet. Male frigatebirds inflate red throat pouches like balloons to attract mates. The flightless cormorant has lost the ability to fly, because it had no predators to escape on land and found its food in the sea."],["Lonesome George","Lonesome George was the last known tortoise from Pinta Island. For decades, scientists tried to find him a mate, without success, and he died in 2012, probably over 100 years old. He became a symbol of the need to protect endangered species."],["Protecting the islands","Goats, rats, cats and other animals brought by people have destroyed plants and eaten eggs. Conservationists have removed goats from several islands and bred tortoises to return to the wild. Tourism is limited: visitors must stay on marked trails with licensed guides."],["A marine reserve","The Galápagos Marine Reserve protects about 133,000 square kilometers of ocean, home to sea lions, hammerhead sharks, rays and whales. In 2022 Ecuador added a new reserve, the Hermandad Marine Reserve, to protect a migration route for sharks and turtles toward Costa Rica."]],
sp:["No fear of people","Many Galápagos animals have little fear of humans because they evolved with few predators. Sea lions sleep on park benches in town, iguanas lie on paths, and birds nest beside trails. Visitors must keep at least 2 meters away and are not allowed to touch or feed the animals, so that they stay wild."],
g:[["Archipelago","A group of islands"],["Natural selection","The process by which living things best suited to their surroundings survive and reproduce"],["Endemic","Found naturally in only one place"],["Marine reserve","A protected area of sea"]],
f:["The Galápagos penguin is the only penguin that lives north of the equator.","Marine iguanas are the only lizards that feed in the sea.","The islands are named after their giant tortoises."],
fun:"Galápagos penguins are the only penguins found north of the equator.",
tl:"Ang Galápagos ay mga pulong bulkaniko sa Pasipiko na bahagi ng Ecuador. Kilala ito sa mga higanteng pagong at iba pang hayop na dito lang matatagpuan, at dito nakakuha si Charles Darwin ng mga ideya tungkol sa ebolusyon."});

ADDART({id:"borneo",t:"Borneo",c:"Countries and Places",i:"🦧",w:"Borneo",r:["indonesiacountry","trees","palawan","mindanao"],
p:[["Third-largest island","Borneo is the third-largest island in the world, after Greenland and New Guinea."],["Shared by three","It is shared by Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei, the only island divided among three countries."],["Ancient rainforest","Its rainforests, among the oldest on Earth, are home to orangutans, pygmy elephants and the giant Rafflesia flower."]],
l:"Borneo is a large tropical island in Southeast Asia, straddling the equator between the South China Sea and the Java Sea. Covering about 743,000 square kilometers, it is divided among Indonesia, whose part is called Kalimantan, the Malaysian states of Sabah and Sarawak, and the small nation of Brunei. Borneo’s rainforests are among the richest habitats in the world, but large areas have been cleared for logging and plantations.",
s:[["The island and its peoples","More than 20 million people live on Borneo. Its Indigenous peoples, often called the Dayak, include many groups, such as the Iban, Kadazan-Dusun and Kenyah, with their own languages and customs. Malay, Chinese, Javanese and other communities live mostly in coastal towns and cities."],["Three countries","Indonesian Kalimantan covers about 73 percent of the island. Sabah and Sarawak became part of Malaysia in 1963. Brunei, a small sultanate on the north coast, was once a powerful kingdom that ruled much of Borneo and parts of the southern Philippines, and today is wealthy from oil and gas."],["The rainforest","Borneo’s lowland rainforest is estimated to be about 130 million years old. It has some 15,000 kinds of flowering plants, including more than 3,000 kinds of trees, and new species are still being discovered. Tall dipterocarp trees, prized for timber, rise to more than 60 meters, and the forest canopy is home to gliding frogs, lizards and snakes."],["Mountains and rivers","Mount Kinabalu, in Sabah, rises to 4,095 meters and is one of the highest peaks in Southeast Asia. Great rivers such as the Kapuas, Mahakam and Rajang flow from the central mountains, serving as highways for people living in the interior. Caves at Gunung Mulu in Sarawak include the Sarawak Chamber, one of the largest cave chambers in the world."]],
k:[["Area","About 743,000 km²"],["Countries","Indonesia, Malaysia, Brunei"],["Population","More than 20 million"],["Highest point","Mount Kinabalu, 4,095 m"],["Famous animals","Bornean orangutan, proboscis monkey, pygmy elephant"]],
d:[["c. 40,000 years ago","Humans are living in Niah Caves, Sarawak"],["14th–16th centuries","Sultanate of Brunei rises as a regional power"],["1841","James Brooke becomes Rajah of Sarawak"],["1963","Sabah and Sarawak join Malaysia"],["1984","Brunei becomes fully independent"],["2022","Indonesia begins building its new capital, Nusantara, in East Kalimantan"]],
s2:[["Orangutans","The Bornean orangutan, whose name means person of the forest in Malay, is a great ape that spends most of its life in the trees. Mothers care for their young for about seven years. Habitat loss and hunting have made it critically endangered, and rescue centers, such as Sepilok in Sabah, care for orphaned orangutans."],["Strange and wonderful animals","The proboscis monkey, found only on Borneo, has a large drooping nose and swims well. Bornean pygmy elephants are the smallest elephants in Asia. Clouded leopards, sun bears, hornbills and flying lizards also live in the forests."],["Rafflesia","Rafflesia arnoldii, which grows in Borneo, Sumatra and nearby regions, has the largest single flower in the world, up to about a meter across. It has no leaves, stems or roots of its own and lives as a parasite inside a vine. It smells of rotting meat to attract the flies that pollinate it."],["Disappearing forests","Since the 1970s, a large part of Borneo’s forest has been logged, burned or turned into oil palm and pulpwood plantations. Fires in drained peatlands send smoke across Southeast Asia. Governments, companies and local communities are working on protected areas, sustainable palm oil and replanting."],["The Heart of Borneo","In 2007 Indonesia, Malaysia and Brunei signed the Heart of Borneo declaration to conserve about 220,000 square kilometers of forest in the island’s interior. The area contains the headwaters of most of Borneo’s major rivers and many of its rarest animals."],["Neighbors across the sea","Borneo has long been linked to the islands around it, including Sumatra, Java, Sulawesi and the Philippines. Trade, migration and kingdoms such as Brunei and the Sultanate of Sulu connected these lands for centuries, and some Bornean peoples share languages and customs with groups in the southern Philippines."]],
sp:["A new capital in the forest","Indonesia is moving its capital from crowded and sinking Jakarta, on Java, to a new city called Nusantara in East Kalimantan, on Borneo. Construction began in 2022, and the first government buildings were used in 2024. Supporters say it will spread development more evenly, while critics worry about its cost and its effects on forests and local peoples."],
g:[["Rainforest","A dense forest with heavy rainfall all year"],["Sultanate","A state ruled by a sultan"],["Dipterocarp","A family of tall rainforest trees"],["Peatland","Wetland where dead plants build up as thick, wet soil"]],
f:["Borneo is the only island divided among three countries.","Its rainforest is estimated to be about 130 million years old.","The proboscis monkey is found only on Borneo."],
fun:"Borneo is the only island in the world shared by three countries.",
tl:"Ang Borneo ang ikatlong pinakamalaking pulo sa mundo, hati sa Indonesia, Malaysia at Brunei. Kilala ito sa sinaunang kagubatang ulan at sa mga orangutan."});

;
/* data/animals6.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 6: hummingbirds, ostriches and turtles.
ADDART({id:"hummingbird",t:"Hummingbirds",i:"🐦",w:"Hummingbird",r:["birds","flower","eagle","parrot"],
p:[["Tiny but mighty","Hummingbirds are the smallest birds in the world; the bee hummingbird of Cuba weighs about 2 grams."],["Hovering experts","They are the only birds that can hover in place for long periods and fly backward."],["Only in the Americas","All of the roughly 360 kinds of hummingbird live in North, Central and South America."]],
l:"Hummingbirds are small, brightly colored birds of the Americas, named for the humming sound made by their fast-beating wings. They feed mostly on flower nectar, drinking with long tongues while hovering in front of blossoms, and help pollinate many plants. Their wings can beat dozens of times a second, and they have the highest metabolism of any warm-blooded animal. Most kinds live in the tropics, especially in the Andes Mountains.",
s:[["Wings like a figure eight","A hummingbird’s wings rotate at the shoulder, tracing a figure-eight pattern that produces lift on both the forward and backward strokes. Small hummingbirds beat their wings about 50 to 80 times a second. This lets them hover, fly sideways, upside down for a moment and even backward."],["Nectar drinkers","Hummingbirds lick nectar with long, forked tongues that open and close to trap the sugary liquid, up to about 15 licks a second. They visit hundreds of flowers a day and also catch small insects and spiders for protein. Many flowers they visit are red or orange and tube-shaped, matching the bird’s long beak."],["A tiny engine","A hummingbird’s heart can beat more than 1,200 times a minute in flight, and it breathes about 250 times a minute even at rest. To fuel this, it must eat about half its body weight in sugar every day. At night, many hummingbirds enter torpor, a deep sleep in which their body temperature and heartbeat drop to save energy."],["Colors that shine","The dazzling throats of many males, called gorgets, are not colored by pigment. Their feathers have tiny structures that bend light, like a soap bubble, so the color flashes brilliantly when the bird faces the viewer and seems dark from other angles. Males show them off in courtship dives."]],
k:[["Species","About 360"],["Smallest","Bee hummingbird, about 5 cm long and 2 g"],["Largest","Giant hummingbird, about 20 cm long"],["Wingbeats","About 50–80 per second in small species"],["Range","The Americas, from Alaska to Tierra del Fuego"]],
d:[["c. 30 million years ago","Ancestors of hummingbirds live in Europe, as fossils show"],["1758","Carl Linnaeus names the first hummingbird species"],["1844","Bee hummingbird described from Cuba"],["2004","Fossil hummingbird found in Germany"],["2011","Study shows how hummingbird tongues trap nectar"]],
s2:[["Long journeys","The ruby-throated hummingbird, which weighs about 3 grams, migrates each year between eastern North America and Central America. Many fly nonstop across the Gulf of Mexico, about 800 kilometers, in roughly 18 to 22 hours. Before leaving, they almost double their weight with fat."],["The sword-billed hummingbird","The sword-billed hummingbird of the Andes has a beak longer than its body, about 10 centimeters, which lets it reach nectar at the bottom of long, trumpet-shaped flowers that other birds cannot. It has to hold its head tilted up when perching to balance the weight."],["Nests and chicks","Females build cup-shaped nests about the size of a walnut shell, made of plant down and held together with spider silk, which stretches as the chicks grow. They usually lay two eggs, each about the size of a jellybean, and raise the young alone."],["Fierce defenders","Despite their size, hummingbirds are bold. They defend patches of flowers or feeders against other hummingbirds with chases and dives, and some attack much larger birds such as hawks and crows that come too close to their nests."],["Partners with flowers","Many plants in the Americas have evolved to be pollinated by hummingbirds. Their flowers are often red, which bees see poorly, have little scent, because birds rely on sight, and produce lots of watery nectar. As the bird drinks, pollen dusts its head and is carried to the next flower."],["Hummingbirds and people","The Aztecs linked hummingbirds with their war god, Huitzilopochtli, whose name means hummingbird of the south. A giant hummingbird is one of the famous Nazca Lines drawn in the desert of Peru. Today many people hang sugar-water feeders to watch hummingbirds in their gardens."]],
sp:["The bee hummingbird","The bee hummingbird, found only in Cuba, is the smallest bird in the world. It is about 5 centimeters long, roughly the size of a large bee, and weighs less than a coin. Its nest is about the size of a thimble, and its eggs are the size of coffee beans. Males have bright red-pink heads and throats in the breeding season."],
g:[["Nectar","A sweet liquid made by flowers"],["Pollinate","To carry pollen from one flower to another so seeds can form"],["Torpor","A deep, energy-saving sleep"],["Metabolism","The chemical processes that turn food into energy in the body"]],
f:["Hummingbirds are the only birds that can fly backward.","A hummingbird’s heart can beat over 1,200 times a minute.","Hummingbirds live only in the Americas."],
fun:"Hummingbirds can fly backward, something no other bird can do for long.",
tl:"Ang hummingbird ang pinakamaliit na ibon sa mundo at nakatira lamang sa kontinente ng Amerika. Kaya nitong lumipad nang paatras at manatili sa iisang lugar sa himpapawid habang umiinom ng nektar ng bulaklak."});

ADDART({id:"ostrich",t:"Ostriches",i:"🪶",w:"Common ostrich",r:["birds","penguin","eco","eagle"],
p:[["The biggest bird","The ostrich is the largest and heaviest living bird, standing up to about 2.7 meters tall."],["Fast runner","It cannot fly, but it is the fastest bird on land, running at up to about 70 kilometers per hour."],["Giant eggs","Ostriches lay the largest eggs of any living animal, each weighing about 1.4 kilograms."]],
l:"Ostriches are large flightless birds native to Africa. They live on savannas, grasslands and semi-deserts, often in groups that roam alongside zebras and antelopes. With long, powerful legs and only two toes on each foot, they are built for running. Ostriches belong to a group of flightless birds called ratites, which also includes emus, rheas, cassowaries and kiwis. There are two species: the common ostrich and the Somali ostrich.",
s:[["Built to run","An ostrich’s legs are long and strongly muscled, and each foot has just two toes, one large one with a thick claw, helping it run like a sprinter on its toes. With strides of up to about 5 meters, it can reach about 70 kilometers per hour in short bursts and keep up about 50 kilometers per hour for longer distances."],["Wings and feathers","Ostriches have wings but cannot fly. They use their wings for balance when running and turning, for displays in courtship and to shade their chicks. Their soft, loose feathers do not lock together like those of flying birds. Males are black with white wing and tail feathers; females are grayish brown."],["Eating and drinking","Ostriches eat mostly plants, such as roots, leaves and seeds, as well as insects and small animals. They swallow pebbles, which help grind food in their gizzard. They can go a long time without drinking, getting much of their water from plants."],["Eyes and defense","An ostrich’s eyes, about 5 centimeters across, are the largest of any land animal, larger than its brain. Sharp eyesight lets ostriches spot predators such as lions and cheetahs far away. If cornered, an ostrich can deliver a powerful forward kick with its clawed feet, strong enough to kill a predator."]],
k:[["Height","Up to about 2.7 m"],["Weight","Up to about 145 kg"],["Top speed","About 70 km/h"],["Egg weight","About 1.4 kg"],["Range","Africa"]],
d:[["c. 20 million years ago","Ancestors of ostriches are living in Asia and Africa"],["1758","Linnaeus names the common ostrich, Struthio camelus"],["1860s","Ostrich farming for feathers begins in South Africa"],["1914","Feather fashion collapses at the start of World War I"],["2014","The Somali ostrich is recognized as a separate species"]],
s2:[["Head in the sand?","The old saying that ostriches bury their heads in the sand when scared is a myth. Ostriches dig shallow nests in the ground and turn their eggs with their beaks several times a day, which from a distance may look as if their heads are buried. When threatened, they run or lie flat with their necks stretched out."],["The nest","A dominant male scrapes a nest in the ground, and his main female lays her eggs there, along with eggs from other females. A nest may hold 20 or more eggs. The female sits on them by day, when her brown feathers blend with the ground, and the male by night, when his black feathers are hard to see."],["Chicks","Chicks hatch after about six weeks and can run within days. Young from several nests often gather into large groups, sometimes more than 100 chicks, watched over by a few adults. Only a small share survive their first year, as many are taken by jackals, hyenas, eagles and other predators."],["Feather fever","In the late 1800s and early 1900s, ostrich plumes were hugely fashionable for hats and fans in Europe and North America. Ostrich farms in South Africa’s Little Karoo region made farmers rich, and the town of Oudtshoorn built grand feather palaces. The boom ended suddenly around 1914."],["Ostriches today","Wild ostriches have disappeared from North Africa and the Middle East, where the Arabian ostrich became extinct in the 1960s, but they remain common in parts of East and southern Africa. Ostriches are farmed around the world for meat, leather and feathers, and their eggshells were used by early humans as water containers."],["Ancient engravings","Archaeologists have found pieces of ostrich eggshell engraved with patterns about 60,000 years ago at Diepkloof Rock Shelter in South Africa. Beads made from ostrich eggshell, among the oldest known jewelry, were traded across southern and East Africa for tens of thousands of years."]],
sp:["Ratites","Ratites are flightless birds with flat breastbones that lack the keel where flying birds attach their wing muscles. Living ratites include ostriches in Africa, rheas in South America, emus and cassowaries in Australia and New Guinea, and kiwis in New Zealand. Extinct giants included the moa of New Zealand and the elephant bird of Madagascar, which laid eggs even larger than an ostrich’s."],
g:[["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Ratite","A member of a group of large flightless birds"],["Gizzard","A muscular stomach that grinds food"],["Plume","A large, showy feather"]],
f:["The ostrich is the fastest bird on land.","Its eye is bigger than its brain.","Ostriches do not bury their heads in the sand."],
fun:"An ostrich’s eye is bigger than its brain.",
tl:"Ang ostrich ang pinakamalaki at pinakamabigat na ibon sa mundo. Hindi ito nakalilipad, ngunit ito ang pinakamabilis na ibon sa lupa. Nangingitlog ito ng pinakamalaking itlog ng anumang buhay na hayop."});

ADDART({id:"turtle",t:"Turtles and Tortoises",i:"🐢",w:"Turtle",r:["lizard","croc","reef","galapagos","plastics"],
p:[["Built-in armor","Turtles are reptiles with a shell made of bone that is part of their skeleton."],["Land, rivers and sea","Tortoises live on land, terrapins in fresh water, and sea turtles roam the oceans."],["Ancient survivors","Turtles have existed for more than 200 million years, since the time of the early dinosaurs."]],
l:"Turtles are reptiles whose bodies are protected by a hard shell. There are about 360 living species, found on every continent except Antarctica. The word turtle covers the whole group, while tortoises are turtles that live on land. Sea turtles travel thousands of kilometers across oceans and return to beaches to lay their eggs. Many turtles live for decades, some for more than a century, but over half of all species are threatened with extinction.",
s:[["The shell","A turtle’s shell has a domed upper part, the carapace, and a flat lower part, the plastron. It is made of bone fused to the ribs and backbone and covered with plates called scutes, made of keratin, like fingernails. A turtle can never leave its shell; it grows along with the animal."],["Pulling in","Most turtles can pull their heads into their shells for protection. One group bends its neck in an S-shape to pull it straight back; the other, called side-necked turtles, folds the neck sideways under the edge of the shell. Box turtles have hinged plastrons that close up like a box."],["Cold-blooded","Like other reptiles, turtles depend on their surroundings to warm up. They bask in the Sun on logs and rocks. In cold places, many freshwater turtles spend the winter resting in the mud at the bottom of ponds, taking in some oxygen through their skin."],["Eggs and hatchlings","All turtles lay eggs on land, usually burying them in sand or soil. In many species, the temperature of the nest decides whether the babies will be male or female: warmer nests produce more females. Hatchlings dig their way out and must survive on their own from the start."]],
k:[["Species","About 360"],["Largest","Leatherback sea turtle, up to about 2 m long and over 600 kg"],["Oldest group","More than 200 million years"],["Sea turtle species","7"],["Longest-lived","Some tortoises over 150 years"]],
d:[["c. 220 million years ago","Early turtle relatives with partial shells live in China"],["c. 210 million years ago","Proganochelys, an early turtle with a full shell, lives in Europe"],["1835","Darwin studies giant tortoises in the Galápagos"],["1960s","Sea turtle conservation programs begin"],["2022","Jonathan the tortoise turns about 190, the oldest known living land animal"]],
s2:[["Sea turtles","Seven species of sea turtle swim the world’s oceans, from the huge leatherback to the small Kemp’s ridley. Their legs have become flippers, and they cannot pull their heads into their shells. Females return to the beach where they hatched, sometimes decades later, crawling ashore at night to lay about 100 eggs in a hole in the sand."],["Navigators","Sea turtles find their way across oceans using the Earth’s magnetic field, which acts like an invisible map. Loggerheads that hatch in Florida swim around the whole North Atlantic before returning. Leatherbacks dive deeper than 1,000 meters in search of jellyfish, their main food."],["Giant tortoises","Giant tortoises live on the Galápagos Islands and on Aldabra in the Seychelles. They can weigh more than 250 kilograms. Jonathan, a Seychelles giant tortoise living on the island of Saint Helena, is thought to have hatched around 1832, making him the oldest known living land animal."],["Strange turtles","The alligator snapping turtle of North America lies on the riverbed with its mouth open, wiggling a pink, worm-like tongue to lure fish. The matamata of South America has a flat, leafy-looking head. The pig-nosed turtle of New Guinea and Australia has flippers like a sea turtle but lives in rivers."],["Threats","Many turtles are hunted for meat, eggs and shells, or collected as pets. Sea turtles drown in fishing nets, swallow plastic bags that look like jellyfish and lose nesting beaches to buildings. Bright lights confuse hatchlings, which head toward the brightest horizon, normally the sea."],["Helping turtles","Many countries protect turtles and their nesting beaches. Volunteers guard nests, move eggs to safe hatcheries and release hatchlings. Fishing nets fitted with turtle excluder devices let turtles escape. Turning off beach lights at night and reducing plastic waste also make a big difference."]],
sp:["Turtle or tortoise?","All tortoises are turtles, but not all turtles are tortoises. Tortoises live on land and have high, domed shells and sturdy, elephant-like legs. Freshwater turtles usually have flatter shells and webbed feet for swimming. Sea turtles have streamlined shells and flippers. In British English, terrapin often means a small freshwater turtle."],
g:[["Carapace","The upper part of a turtle’s shell"],["Plastron","The lower part of a turtle’s shell"],["Scute","A bony or horny plate on a shell"],["Hatchling","A young animal that has just hatched"]],
f:["Turtles have existed for more than 200 million years.","Nest temperature decides whether many baby turtles are male or female.","A turtle’s shell is part of its skeleton."],
fun:"In many turtles, warmer nests produce more female hatchlings.",
tl:"Ang pagong ay reptilyang may matigas na balat-kabibe na bahagi ng kanyang kalansay. May mga pagong sa lupa, sa tubig-tabang at sa dagat. Marami sa kanila ang nanganganib dahil sa pangangaso at plastik sa dagat."});

;
/* data/animals7.js */
// Animal Kingdom articles, part 7: chameleons, beetles and mosquitoes.
ADDART({id:"chameleon",t:"Chameleons",i:"🦎",w:"Chameleon",r:["lizard","madag","snake","eye"],
p:[["Color changers","Chameleons are lizards famous for changing their skin color to signal moods and control their temperature."],["Lightning tongues","They catch insects with a sticky tongue that can shoot out longer than their own body in a split second."],["Eyes that swivel","Each eye can move on its own, so a chameleon can look in two directions at once."]],
l:"Chameleons are a family of about 200 species of lizards found mainly in Africa and Madagascar, with a few in southern Europe, the Middle East and South Asia. Almost half of all species live only on Madagascar. They are known for their ability to change color, their independently moving eyes, their long, fast tongues and their grasping feet and tails, which help them move slowly and carefully through trees and bushes.",
s:[["How color changes","Chameleon skin has layers of cells containing pigments and tiny crystals. By relaxing or tightening the skin, a chameleon changes the spacing of the crystals, which changes how they reflect light, shifting its color from greens to yellows, reds and blues. The change can take from seconds to minutes."],["Why they change color","Despite popular belief, chameleons do not mainly change color to match their background. Most color changes are signals: males turn bright to warn rivals or court females, and stressed or frightened chameleons often turn dark. Color also helps control body temperature, since darker skin absorbs more heat."],["The tongue","A chameleon’s tongue is made of elastic tissue and muscle wrapped around a bone, like a spring loaded on a rod. When released, it shoots out with tremendous acceleration and catches prey with a sticky, sucker-like tip. In some small species, the tongue can be more than twice as long as the body."],["Feet and tails","Chameleons’ toes are fused into two groups on each foot, like a pair of tongs, which let them grip branches tightly. Most also have a prehensile tail, which can curl around twigs like an extra hand. They move slowly, rocking back and forth to look like leaves swaying in the breeze."]],
k:[["Species","About 200"],["Main home","Africa and Madagascar"],["Largest","Parson’s chameleon, up to about 68 cm"],["Smallest","Brookesia nana, about 2.2 cm"],["Eyes","Can move independently, giving almost 360° vision"]],
d:[["c. 60 million years ago","Chameleons evolve, probably in Africa"],["1758","Linnaeus names the common chameleon"],["2012","Tiny Brookesia micra described from Madagascar"],["2015","Study explains how chameleons change color using crystals"],["2021","Brookesia nana described as the smallest known reptile"]],
s2:[["Eyes like turrets","A chameleon’s eyes sit in cone-shaped turrets covered with skin, leaving only a small opening for the pupil. Each eye can rotate and focus on its own, scanning in different directions. When prey is spotted, both eyes lock on it together, judging the distance precisely before the tongue strikes."],["Madagascar’s chameleons","Madagascar has about half of the world’s chameleon species, from the huge Parson’s chameleon to the tiny leaf chameleons of the genus Brookesia. Panther chameleons there come in many different color patterns depending on the region they come from."],["The smallest reptile","In 2021 scientists described Brookesia nana, a chameleon from northern Madagascar. Adult males have bodies only about 1.35 centimeters long, or about 2.2 centimeters including the tail, small enough to sit on a fingertip. It may be the smallest reptile on Earth."],["Horns and crests","Many chameleons have decorations on their heads. Jackson’s chameleon of East Africa has three horns, like a tiny Triceratops, which males use when sparring. Veiled chameleons of Arabia have tall casques, helmet-like crests on their heads, which may help channel water toward their mouths."],["Chameleons in danger","Many chameleons live in small areas of forest and are threatened by forest clearing. Large numbers have also been caught for the pet trade, and some species are now protected by international law. Chameleons are hard to keep as pets because they need special lighting, temperatures and live food."],["A word for changing","Because of its color changes, the chameleon has become a symbol of change. A person who changes their opinions or behavior to fit in with whoever they are with is sometimes called a chameleon. The name comes from Greek words meaning ground lion."]],
sp:["Glowing bones","In 2018 scientists discovered that many chameleons have bones that glow under ultraviolet light. Small bumps on the bones of their heads show through their thin skin, creating patterns of blue dots that glow when lit by ultraviolet light, which is part of natural sunlight. The patterns may help chameleons recognize their own species in the shady forest."],
g:[["Prehensile","Able to grip or hold things"],["Casque","A helmet-like crest on the head"],["Pigment","A substance that gives color"],["Turret","A small rotating tower; for chameleons, a cone-shaped eyelid"]],
f:["Almost half of all chameleons live only on Madagascar.","A chameleon’s eyes can look in two directions at once.","Chameleons change color mainly to communicate."],
fun:"Chameleons change color mainly to send signals, not to hide.",
tl:"Ang chameleon ay butiking kayang magpalit ng kulay, may matang gumagalaw nang magkahiwalay at mahabang dila para sa paghuli ng insekto. Halos kalahati ng mga uri nito ay sa Madagascar lamang matatagpuan."});

ADDART({id:"beetle",t:"Beetles",i:"🪲",w:"Beetle",r:["insect","ants","bee","eco","egypt"],
p:[["The biggest group","Beetles are the largest order of animals, with around 400,000 known species, about a quarter of all known animals."],["Armored wings","Their front wings have become hard cases called elytra, which protect the delicate flying wings underneath."],["Everywhere","Beetles live almost everywhere on land and in fresh water, from deserts to mountaintops."]],
l:"Beetles are insects that belong to the order Coleoptera, meaning sheath wings. With about 400,000 described species and possibly over a million more still unknown, they are the most diverse group of animals on Earth. They range from tiny featherwing beetles smaller than a grain of sand to the huge Titan beetle of the Amazon. Ladybirds, fireflies, weevils, dung beetles and stag beetles are all beetles.",
s:[["Body plan","Like all insects, beetles have six legs and a body divided into head, thorax and abdomen. Their hardened front wings, the elytra, meet in a straight line down the back. To fly, a beetle lifts its elytra and unfolds its thin hind wings. Beetles have chewing mouthparts, and many have strong jaws called mandibles."],["Life cycle","Beetles go through complete metamorphosis: egg, larva, pupa and adult. Beetle larvae, called grubs, look very different from adults and often live in soil, wood or water. Some larvae live for years before changing into adults, which may live only a few weeks."],["What they eat","Beetles eat almost everything. Many eat plants, some hunt other insects, and others feed on dead wood, fungi, dung or dead animals. By breaking down waste and dead matter, beetles recycle nutrients into the soil, making them vital to ecosystems."],["Why so many?","Scientists think beetles became so diverse because their tough elytra let them squeeze into small spaces, such as soil and bark, while still being able to fly. They also spread alongside flowering plants, feeding on them and adapting to new kinds. The biologist J. B. S. Haldane is said to have joked that the Creator had an inordinate fondness for beetles."]],
k:[["Order","Coleoptera"],["Known species","About 400,000"],["Largest","Titan beetle, up to about 17 cm long"],["Smallest","Featherwing beetles, about 0.3 mm"],["Strongest","Horned dung beetle, can pull over 1,000 times its weight"]],
d:[["c. 300 million years ago","Earliest beetle-like insects appear"],["c. 2500 BCE","Egyptians honor the scarab beetle"],["1758","Linnaeus begins naming beetles in his system"],["1828–1831","Charles Darwin collects beetles as a student at Cambridge"],["2013","Study shows dung beetles navigating by the Milky Way"],["2019","Beetle in 99-million-year-old amber found carrying pollen"]],
s2:[["Ladybirds","Ladybirds, also called ladybugs or lady beetles, are small, round beetles, often red or orange with black spots. Their bright colors warn birds that they taste bad. Gardeners and farmers welcome them because a single ladybird can eat thousands of aphids, tiny insects that damage plants, during its life."],["Fireflies","Fireflies are soft-bodied beetles that make light in their abdomens through a chemical reaction, a process called bioluminescence. Males and females flash in patterns to find each other, and each species has its own code. In parts of Southeast Asia, thousands of fireflies along rivers flash together in time."],["Dung beetles","Dung beetles feed on animal droppings. Some roll dung into balls and push them away to bury, laying their eggs inside. They help keep pastures clean and fertile. Some species steer by the light of the Milky Way at night, the first animals known to navigate using the galaxy."],["Giants","The Titan beetle of the Amazon rainforest can grow up to about 17 centimeters long and has jaws strong enough to snap pencils. The Goliath beetles of Africa are among the heaviest insects, and the Hercules beetle of Central and South America has a huge horn longer than its body."],["Beetles and people","Ancient Egyptians saw the scarab beetle rolling its ball of dung as a symbol of the Sun god Khepri rolling the Sun across the sky. Scarab-shaped amulets were placed with mummies. Some beetles, such as weevils and bark beetles, are pests that damage crops, stored grain and forests."],["Clever chemistry","The bombardier beetle defends itself by mixing chemicals in a chamber in its abdomen, producing a boiling-hot spray that it fires at attackers with a popping sound. The Namib Desert beetle collects drinking water from fog on the bumps of its back, an idea engineers have copied to design water-collecting surfaces."]],
sp:["Beetles in amber","Amber is fossilized tree resin. When ancient beetles became trapped in sticky resin, they were sealed in perfect detail. Amber from Myanmar has preserved beetles from about 99 million years ago, in the age of dinosaurs, some still carrying pollen grains, showing that beetles were pollinating plants long before bees became common."],
g:[["Coleoptera","The insect order of beetles"],["Elytra","The hardened front wings of a beetle"],["Bioluminescence","Light produced by a living thing"],["Grub","A beetle larva"]],
f:["About a quarter of all known animal species are beetles.","Some dung beetles navigate by the Milky Way.","Ladybirds and fireflies are beetles."],
fun:"Some dung beetles find their way at night by looking at the Milky Way.",
tl:"Ang salagubang ay pinakamalaking pangkat ng mga hayop, may mga 400,000 kilalang uri. Kabilang dito ang ladybird at alitaptap. Mahalaga sila sa kalikasan dahil tumutulong sila sa pagbubulok ng patay na halaman at hayop."});

ADDART({id:"mosquito",t:"Mosquitoes",i:"🦟",w:"Mosquito",r:["insect","malaria","vacc","immune","wcycle"],
p:[["Tiny but deadly","Mosquitoes spread diseases that kill hundreds of thousands of people a year, making them the deadliest animals to humans."],["Only females bite","Female mosquitoes drink blood to get protein for their eggs; males feed only on nectar."],["Born in water","Mosquitoes lay their eggs in still water, where the young grow as wrigglers."]],
l:"Mosquitoes are small flies with long legs and a needle-like mouthpart called a proboscis. There are more than 3,500 species, found almost everywhere except Antarctica. Most are harmless nuisances, but a few species carry germs that cause malaria, dengue, Zika, yellow fever, chikungunya and other diseases. By spreading these illnesses, mosquitoes cause more human deaths than any other animal.",
s:[["Life cycle","A female mosquito lays her eggs on or near still water, in ponds, puddles, old tires or even a bottle cap of rainwater. The larvae, called wrigglers, hang at the surface breathing through tubes. They become pupae, called tumblers, and after about a week to ten days adults emerge from the water."],["Why females bite","Both male and female mosquitoes drink nectar for energy. But females need protein and iron from blood to produce eggs. A female finds a host by sensing the carbon dioxide in breath, body heat and the smells of skin. Her proboscis has six needle-like parts that slide into the skin to find a blood vessel."],["Why bites itch","When a mosquito bites, it injects saliva that keeps blood from clotting. The body’s immune system reacts to proteins in the saliva by releasing histamine, which causes the red, itchy bump. People who are bitten often may react less over time."],["Carrying disease","When a mosquito bites a person or animal with certain germs, the germs can grow inside the mosquito and then pass into the next person it bites. Anopheles mosquitoes carry the parasites that cause malaria. Aedes mosquitoes, often active by day, carry the viruses of dengue, Zika, yellow fever and chikungunya. Culex mosquitoes can spread West Nile virus."]],
k:[["Species","More than 3,500"],["Deaths caused","Hundreds of thousands a year, mostly from malaria"],["Wingbeats","About 300–600 per second"],["Life span","About 2 weeks to a month for adults"],["Disease carriers","Anopheles, Aedes and Culex"]],
d:[["1881","Carlos Finlay proposes that mosquitoes spread yellow fever"],["1897","Ronald Ross shows that mosquitoes transmit malaria"],["1900","Walter Reed’s team confirms Finlay’s yellow fever theory"],["1937","Yellow fever vaccine developed by Max Theiler"],["2021","WHO recommends the first malaria vaccine for children"]],
s2:[["Malaria","Malaria is caused by Plasmodium parasites spread by Anopheles mosquitoes. In 2023 there were an estimated 263 million cases and about 600,000 deaths, most of them young children in Africa. Bed nets treated with insecticide, indoor spraying, medicines and new vaccines have saved millions of lives."],["Dengue","Dengue, spread by Aedes mosquitoes, is the fastest-spreading mosquito-borne disease. It causes high fever and aches and can become severe. Cases have risen sharply in recent decades across Asia, Latin America and Africa, partly because of warmer weather, rain and crowded cities, with a record number reported worldwide in 2024."],["The whining sound","The high-pitched whine of a mosquito comes from its wings, which beat hundreds of times a second. Males and females find each other by adjusting the pitch of their wingbeats until they match, like a duet. The sound is why people often hear a mosquito before they feel it."],["Fighting mosquitoes","The best way to stop mosquitoes is to remove the still water where they breed: cover water containers, empty buckets and plant pots, and clear gutters. Window screens, nets, repellents and clothing that covers the skin all help prevent bites."],["New weapons","Scientists are testing new ways to control mosquitoes. In many countries, mosquitoes infected with bacteria called Wolbachia, which block dengue viruses, are released to replace wild populations. Others release sterile males so that females lay eggs that do not hatch, and some are testing gene drives."],["Not all bad","Most mosquito species never bite humans. Their larvae are food for fish, frogs and dragonfly larvae, and adults are eaten by birds, bats and spiders. Male mosquitoes and some females pollinate flowers, including some orchids. Scientists debate what would happen to ecosystems if all mosquitoes disappeared."]],
sp:["World Mosquito Day","Every year on 20 August, World Mosquito Day marks the day in 1897 when the British doctor Ronald Ross, working in India, found malaria parasites in the stomach of an Anopheles mosquito, proving that mosquitoes carry the disease. He won the Nobel Prize in 1902. The Cuban doctor Carlos Finlay had already suggested in 1881 that mosquitoes spread yellow fever."],
g:[["Proboscis","A long, tube-like mouthpart"],["Larva","The young, wormlike stage of an insect"],["Parasite","A living thing that lives on or in another and harms it"],["Repellent","A substance that keeps insects away"]],
f:["Only female mosquitoes bite.","Mosquitoes are the deadliest animals to humans.","They find people by sensing the carbon dioxide in their breath."],
fun:"Male and female mosquitoes match the pitch of their wingbeats when courting, like singing a duet.",
tl:"Ang lamok ay maliit na insektong nagdadala ng mga sakit gaya ng malarya at dengue, kaya ito ang pinakamapanganib na hayop sa tao. Babae lamang ang nangangagat. Nangingitlog sila sa tubig na hindi gumagalaw."});

;
/* data/health5.js */
// The Human Body articles: first aid, sanitation, antibiotics and pandemics.
// Same format as data/countries1.js (see data/animals-load.js).
ADDART({id:"firstaid",t:"First Aid",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🩹",w:"First aid",r:["heart","blood","hygiene","bones"],
p:[["Help until help arrives","First aid is the care given to someone who is hurt or suddenly ill before a doctor, nurse or ambulance arrives."],["Stay safe first","The first rule is to make sure the scene is safe for you, the injured person and others."],["Anyone can learn","Simple skills such as calling for help, stopping bleeding and CPR can save lives."]],
l:"First aid is the immediate help given to a person who is injured or taken ill, until professional medical help arrives. Its goals are often summed up as the three Ps: preserve life, prevent the condition from getting worse and promote recovery. Many first aid skills are simple and can be learned by children and adults alike. Organizations such as the Red Cross and Red Crescent teach first aid around the world.",
s:[["Check, call, care","A common way to remember what to do is check, call, care. Check that the area is safe and see what has happened. Call for help: shout to people nearby and phone the local emergency number, such as 911, 112 or 999. Then care for the person as best you can until help arrives."],["Is the person breathing?","If someone collapses, gently tap their shoulders and ask loudly if they are all right. If they do not respond, tilt the head back to open the airway and look, listen and feel for normal breathing for no more than 10 seconds. If they are breathing, they can be rolled onto their side in the recovery position, which keeps the airway clear."],["CPR","If a person is unresponsive and not breathing normally, their heart may have stopped. Cardiopulmonary resuscitation, or CPR, means pushing hard and fast in the center of the chest, about 100 to 120 times a minute, to keep blood flowing to the brain. Starting CPR immediately can double or triple the chances of survival."],["Bleeding","To stop heavy bleeding, press firmly on the wound with a clean cloth or bandage and keep pressing. If blood soaks through, add more cloth on top rather than removing the first layer. Wearing gloves, or using a plastic bag over the hand, protects both the helper and the injured person from germs."]],
k:[["Aims","Preserve life, prevent worsening, promote recovery"],["CPR rate","100–120 chest compressions a minute"],["Emergency numbers","Such as 911, 112, 999 and 000, depending on the country"],["World First Aid Day","Second Saturday of September"],["Teachers","Red Cross, Red Crescent, St John Ambulance and others"]],
d:[["1859","Henry Dunant organizes care for the wounded after the Battle of Solferino"],["1863","International Committee of the Red Cross founded"],["1877","St John Ambulance begins teaching first aid in England"],["1960","Modern CPR, combining chest compressions and rescue breaths, is developed"],["2000","First World First Aid Day held"]],
s2:[["Choking","A person who is choking cannot speak, cough or breathe properly and may clutch their throat. Encourage them to cough. If that does not work, give up to five firm back blows between the shoulder blades, then up to five abdominal thrusts, pulling inward and upward just above the belly button. Babies need gentler, special techniques."],["Burns","For a burn, cool the area under cool running water for at least 20 minutes, as soon as possible. Do not use ice, butter or toothpaste. Remove rings or tight clothing near the burn before it swells, then cover it loosely with cling film or a clean, non-fluffy cloth. Large or deep burns need medical care."],["Sprains and broken bones","For a sprain, rest the injured part, apply a cold pack wrapped in cloth and raise it if possible. If a bone may be broken, keep the injured part still and supported in the position you found it, and get medical help. Do not try to push a bone back into place."],["Defibrillators","An automated external defibrillator, or AED, is a portable machine that can restart a heart beating in a dangerous rhythm by giving it an electric shock. AEDs are found in many airports, schools, malls and sports centers. They give spoken instructions, so anyone can use one, and they shock only when needed."],["A first aid kit","A basic first aid kit includes adhesive bandages, sterile dressings, rolled bandages, tape, disposable gloves, scissors, tweezers and antiseptic wipes, along with a list of emergency numbers. Homes, cars, schools and workplaces should keep one where everyone can find it, and check it regularly."],["Disasters","In places where typhoons, earthquakes and floods are common, first aid is part of being prepared. Community volunteers trained in first aid often help neighbors before rescue teams can reach them. Many schools hold drills so that children know what to do and how to help."]],
sp:["Hands-only CPR","Many people worry about giving rescue breaths. For adults who suddenly collapse, emergency services now encourage bystanders who are not trained to give hands-only CPR: call the emergency number, then push hard and fast in the center of the chest without stopping. A song with the right beat, such as Stayin’ Alive by the Bee Gees, can help keep the pace of about 100 to 120 pushes a minute."],
g:[["CPR","Cardiopulmonary resuscitation, chest compressions that keep blood flowing"],["Recovery position","A safe position on the side that keeps the airway open"],["AED","A portable machine that can shock a heart back into rhythm"],["Airway","The passage through which air enters the lungs"]],
f:["Starting CPR quickly can double or triple the chance of survival.","Burns should be cooled with running water for at least 20 minutes.","Anyone can use an AED by following its spoken instructions."],
fun:"The beat of the song Stayin’ Alive is close to the right pace for CPR.",
tl:"Ang first aid ay agarang tulong sa taong nasaktan o biglang nagkasakit habang hinihintay ang doktor o ambulansya. Kabilang dito ang paghingi ng tulong, pagpigil sa pagdurugo at CPR."});

ADDART({id:"sanitation",t:"Sanitation and Clean Water",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🚰",w:"Sanitation",r:["water","hygiene","wcycle","pandemic","rome"],
p:[["Toilets save lives","Safe toilets and sewage systems keep human waste away from people and drinking water, preventing many diseases."],["A basic need","Billions of people still lack safely managed toilets or clean drinking water at home."],["An old idea","Ancient cities from the Indus Valley to Rome built drains and sewers thousands of years ago."]],
l:"Sanitation means the safe management of human waste, from toilets to the treatment of sewage, along with keeping water, streets and homes clean. Together with clean drinking water and handwashing, it is one of the most important ways to protect health. Diarrheal diseases spread by dirty water and poor sanitation kill hundreds of thousands of people every year, most of them young children. Access to safe water and sanitation is recognized by the United Nations as a human right.",
s:[["Why it matters","Human waste can carry germs that cause cholera, typhoid, diarrhea, polio and worms. When waste gets into rivers, wells or food, these germs spread quickly. Good sanitation breaks this chain. Historians count clean water and sewers among the biggest causes of the rise in life expectancy over the past 150 years."],["Ancient drains","The cities of the Indus Valley civilization, such as Mohenjo-daro, had covered brick drains and household toilets more than 4,000 years ago. The palace of Knossos on Crete had flushing water channels, and ancient Rome built aqueducts to bring fresh water and a great sewer, the Cloaca Maxima, parts of which still work."],["The Great Stink","In the 1800s, fast-growing cities such as London poured sewage into rivers that also supplied drinking water, and cholera killed tens of thousands. In the hot summer of 1858, the smell of the River Thames, the Great Stink, became so bad that Parliament acted. The engineer Joseph Bazalgette built a vast network of sewers that carried waste away from the city."],["From toilet to treatment","Modern sewage systems carry waste through pipes to treatment plants, where solids are removed, bacteria break down the waste and the cleaned water is returned to rivers or the sea. Where there are no sewers, well-built pit latrines and septic tanks can also be safe if they are emptied and managed properly."]],
k:[["Without safely managed sanitation","About 3.4 billion people (2024 estimate)"],["Without safely managed drinking water","About 2.1 billion people (2024 estimate)"],["World Toilet Day","19 November"],["UN goal","Water and sanitation for all by 2030 (Goal 6)"],["Human right","Recognized by the UN in 2010"]],
d:[["c. 2500 BCE","Indus Valley cities build covered drains"],["c. 600 BCE","Rome’s Cloaca Maxima is begun"],["1854","John Snow traces a cholera outbreak to a London water pump"],["1858","The Great Stink in London"],["2010","UN recognizes water and sanitation as human rights"],["2013","UN declares 19 November World Toilet Day"]],
s2:[["John Snow’s pump","In 1854, during a cholera outbreak in London, the doctor John Snow mapped the homes of people who had died and found they clustered around a water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded officials to remove the pump’s handle, and cases fell. His work helped prove that cholera spreads through dirty water, not bad air."],["Clean water","Water from rivers and wells can be made safe by filtering, boiling or adding chlorine, which kills germs. Since the early 1900s, chlorinating city water supplies has saved millions of lives. In homes without safe water, simple filters, chlorine tablets or leaving clear bottles of water in strong sunlight for several hours can help."],["Open defecation","Hundreds of millions of people still have no toilet at all and must go in fields, bushes or rivers. This spreads disease and is especially unsafe for women and girls at night. Many countries have run campaigns to build toilets, and the number of people with no toilet has fallen sharply since 2000."],["Schools and girls","When schools have clean, private toilets and water for washing, more girls attend, especially after they begin to menstruate. Programs that build toilets and handwashing stations in schools help children stay healthy and in class."],["New inventions","Engineers are designing toilets that work without sewers or water, turning waste into fertilizer, fuel or clean water. In 2018 the Reinvented Toilet Expo in Beijing showed off many such designs. Some cities collect waste from homes and process it safely, making sanitation possible in crowded neighborhoods."],["Everyone can help","Washing hands with soap after using the toilet and before eating is one of the simplest ways to stop germs. Keeping toilets clean, not throwing trash into drains and canals, and not wasting water all protect the health of whole communities."]],
sp:["World Toilet Day","Each year on 19 November, World Toilet Day draws attention to the billions of people who live without safe sanitation. It was started by the World Toilet Organization, founded in Singapore in 2001 by Jack Sim, and became an official United Nations day in 2013. Its cheerful name helps people talk about a subject that is often treated as embarrassing, but that is a matter of life and death."],
g:[["Sanitation","The safe management of human waste"],["Sewage","Waste water and waste from toilets"],["Latrine","A simple toilet, often a pit in the ground"],["Chlorine","A chemical used to kill germs in water"]],
f:["Parts of Rome’s ancient sewer still work today.","John Snow’s map helped prove cholera spreads through water.","19 November is World Toilet Day."],
fun:"London’s Great Stink of 1858 was so bad that Parliament soaked its curtains in chemicals.",
tl:"Ang sanitasyon ay ligtas na pamamahala ng dumi ng tao, mula palikuran hanggang paglilinis ng tubig-dumi. Kasama ng malinis na tubig at paghuhugas ng kamay, isa ito sa pinakamahalagang paraan upang maiwasan ang sakit."});

ADDART({id:"antibiotics",t:"Antibiotics",c:"Health and the Body",i:"💊",w:"Antibiotic",r:["microbe","immune","vacc","penicil","cell"],
p:[["Germ killers","Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria or stop them from growing."],["A lucky discovery","Alexander Fleming discovered penicillin, the first widely used antibiotic, in 1928 when mold killed bacteria on a dish."],["Not for viruses","Antibiotics do not work against viruses, such as those that cause colds and flu."]],
l:"Antibiotics are medicines that fight infections caused by bacteria. Since penicillin came into wide use in the 1940s, antibiotics have saved hundreds of millions of lives, turning once-deadly infections such as pneumonia, blood poisoning and infected wounds into treatable illnesses. They also make surgery, cancer treatment and the care of premature babies much safer. But bacteria can evolve resistance, and overuse of antibiotics is making some infections harder to treat.",
s:[["How they work","Antibiotics attack parts of bacteria that human cells do not have. Penicillin stops bacteria from building their cell walls, so they burst. Others block the tiny machines, called ribosomes, that bacteria use to make proteins, or stop them from copying their DNA. Some kill bacteria outright, while others stop them multiplying so the immune system can finish the job."],["Fleming and penicillin","In 1928 the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned from holiday to his London laboratory and noticed that a mold, Penicillium, had grown on a dish of bacteria and killed those around it. He named the substance penicillin, but he could not make enough of it to use as a medicine."],["Making a medicine","In 1939 to 1941, a team at Oxford University led by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, with Norman Heatley and others, purified penicillin and showed that it cured infections. During World War II, factories in the United States and Britain mass-produced it, saving many wounded soldiers. Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in 1945."],["The golden age","Between the 1940s and 1960s, scientists discovered most of the main groups of antibiotics, many from soil microbes. Streptomycin, found in 1943 by Albert Schatz in Selman Waksman’s laboratory, was the first effective treatment for tuberculosis. Since then, very few new types have been found."]],
k:[["First widely used","Penicillin, from the 1940s"],["Discovered by","Alexander Fleming, 1928"],["Works against","Bacteria, not viruses"],["Main threat","Antimicrobial resistance"],["World AMR Awareness Week","18–24 November"]],
d:[["1910","Paul Ehrlich introduces Salvarsan, a chemical cure for syphilis"],["1928","Fleming discovers penicillin"],["1935","Gerhard Domagk introduces sulfa drugs"],["1941","Penicillin is first tested in a patient in Oxford"],["1943","Streptomycin is discovered"],["1945","Fleming, Florey and Chain win the Nobel Prize"]],
s2:[["Antibiotic resistance","When bacteria are exposed to antibiotics, a few may survive because of chance changes in their genes. These survivors multiply and can pass on their resistance. Over time, whole populations of bacteria can become resistant. Some superbugs, such as MRSA, resist many antibiotics."],["Why it matters","Drug-resistant infections were directly responsible for more than a million deaths in 2019, according to a major study, and contributed to millions more. Without effective antibiotics, common operations, childbirth and chemotherapy would become much more dangerous. The World Health Organization calls antimicrobial resistance one of the top global threats to health."],["Using antibiotics wisely","Antibiotics should be taken only when prescribed by a health worker, in the dose and for the length of time advised. They should not be saved for later or shared with others. Taking antibiotics for colds or flu does not help, because these are caused by viruses, and it encourages resistance."],["On the farm","A large share of the world’s antibiotics is given to farm animals, sometimes to make them grow faster or prevent disease in crowded conditions. This can breed resistant bacteria that spread to people. Many countries, including those of the European Union, have banned antibiotics for growth promotion."],["Searching for new ones","Scientists are searching for new antibiotics in soil, the ocean, insects and even the human nose. Teixobactin, found in soil bacteria in 2015, works in a new way. Researchers are also using artificial intelligence to find promising molecules, and testing viruses called phages that infect and kill bacteria."],["Vaccines help too","Vaccines help fight resistance by preventing infections in the first place, so fewer antibiotics are needed. Vaccines against pneumococcal disease and Haemophilus influenzae type b, for example, have greatly reduced serious bacterial infections in children."]],
sp:["Mouldy Mary","In 1943 scientists in Peoria, in the United States, were searching for molds that made more penicillin. A laboratory worker, Mary Hunt, brought in a moldy cantaloupe melon from a local market. Its mold, Penicillium chrysogenum, produced far more penicillin than Fleming’s strain, and most penicillin made since then has come from its descendants. She became known as Mouldy Mary."],
g:[["Bacteria","Single-celled microbes, some of which cause disease"],["Resistance","The ability of bacteria to survive a medicine"],["Superbug","A bacterium resistant to many antibiotics"],["Phage","A virus that infects bacteria"]],
f:["Antibiotics do not work against viruses.","Penicillin was discovered by accident in 1928.","Much of the world’s penicillin came from mold on a melon."],
fun:"Much of the world’s penicillin descends from mold found on a melon in a market in Illinois.",
tl:"Ang antibiotic ay gamot na pumapatay o pumipigil sa paglago ng bakterya. Natuklasan ni Alexander Fleming ang penicillin noong 1928. Hindi ito gumagana laban sa virus, at ang labis na paggamit nito ay nagdudulot ng resistensya."});

ADDART({id:"pandemic",t:"Pandemics",c:"Health and the Body",i:"🦠",w:"Pandemic",r:["flu","vacc","immune","microbe","hygiene"],
p:[["Worldwide outbreaks","A pandemic is an outbreak of disease that spreads across many countries and continents, affecting large numbers of people."],["Changing history","Pandemics such as the Black Death and the 1918 flu killed millions and changed societies."],["The latest pandemic","The COVID-19 pandemic, which began in late 2019, was the most serious in a century."]],
l:"A pandemic is an epidemic that spreads across a large region, such as several continents or the whole world. Pandemics have been caused by bacteria, such as plague, and by viruses, such as influenza, HIV and the coronavirus that causes COVID-19. They can kill millions of people, disrupt economies and change the course of history. Public health measures, medicines and vaccines have made it possible to slow them and save many lives.",
s:[["Outbreak, epidemic, pandemic","An outbreak is a sudden rise in cases of a disease in one place. An epidemic is a larger spread within a country or region. A pandemic crosses borders and spreads across the world. A disease that is always present in an area, such as malaria in parts of Africa, is called endemic."],["The Black Death","In the 1340s, bubonic plague, caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis and spread by fleas on rats, swept from Central Asia along trade routes to the Middle East, North Africa and Europe. The Black Death killed perhaps a third or more of Europe’s people, and many in Asia and Africa. It caused labor shortages that changed economies and societies."],["The 1918 influenza","The influenza pandemic of 1918 to 1920 infected about a third of the world’s people and killed an estimated 50 million, more than World War I. Unusually, it killed many young, healthy adults. Spread by soldiers and ships at the end of the war, it reached even remote Pacific islands and the Arctic."],["COVID-19","COVID-19 is caused by a coronavirus, SARS-CoV-2, first identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019. The World Health Organization declared it a pandemic in March 2020. Countries closed borders, schools and businesses to slow its spread. By official counts more than 7 million people died, and studies suggest the true toll was much higher."]],
k:[["Definition","An epidemic spreading across several countries or continents"],["Deadliest recorded","Black Death (1340s)"],["1918 flu deaths","About 50 million (estimate)"],["COVID-19 reported deaths","More than 7 million"],["Global body","World Health Organization (WHO)"]],
d:[["541","Plague of Justinian strikes the Byzantine Empire"],["1347–1351","Black Death spreads across Europe"],["1918–1920","Influenza pandemic"],["1981","First cases of AIDS reported"],["2020","WHO declares COVID-19 a pandemic"],["2023","WHO ends the COVID-19 global health emergency"]],
s2:[["How diseases spread","Germs can spread through droplets and tiny particles in the air when people cough, talk or breathe, through touching contaminated surfaces, through food and water, or through insects such as fleas and mosquitoes. Many pandemic diseases began in animals and jumped to humans."],["Slowing the spread","Measures such as washing hands, wearing masks, staying home when sick, testing, tracing contacts and quarantining help slow pandemics. Slowing the spread, often called flattening the curve, keeps hospitals from being overwhelmed while scientists develop treatments and vaccines."],["Vaccines at record speed","COVID-19 vaccines were developed, tested and approved in less than a year, much faster than ever before, thanks to decades of research on new technologies such as mRNA vaccines. Billions of doses were given worldwide. Getting vaccines to poorer countries was slower, an inequality that many experts criticized."],["HIV and AIDS","HIV, the virus that causes AIDS, spread around the world from the late 1900s. About 40 million people have died of AIDS-related illnesses. There is still no cure, but medicines called antiretrovirals now let people with HIV live long, healthy lives and prevent the virus from being passed on."],["Changing the world","Pandemics have reshaped societies. The Black Death raised wages for surviving workers in Europe. Smallpox and other diseases brought by Europeans devastated Indigenous peoples of the Americas from the 1500s. COVID-19 sped up working from home, online learning and digital payments in many countries."],["Preparing for the next one","Scientists watch for new diseases in people and animals, share information through networks run by the WHO and develop vaccine technologies that can be adapted quickly. In 2025 the member states of the WHO adopted a Pandemic Agreement aimed at better preparation and fairer sharing of vaccines and medicines."]],
sp:["The word pandemic","The word pandemic comes from the Greek pan, meaning all, and demos, meaning people: a disease of all the people. The related word epidemic means upon the people. The word quarantine comes from the Italian quaranta giorni, forty days, the time ships arriving in Venice had to wait before landing during plague outbreaks in the 1300s and 1400s."],
g:[["Epidemic","A widespread outbreak of disease in a region"],["Endemic","Always present in a particular area"],["Quarantine","Keeping people apart to stop disease spreading"],["Coronavirus","A family of viruses that includes the cause of COVID-19"]],
f:["The 1918 flu killed more people than World War I.","The word quarantine comes from the Italian for forty days.","COVID-19 vaccines were developed in under a year."],
fun:"The word quarantine comes from the Italian words for forty days.",
tl:"Ang pandemya ay paglaganap ng sakit sa maraming bansa at kontinente. Kabilang dito ang Black Death, ang trangkaso noong 1918 at ang COVID-19. Nakatutulong ang kalinisan, bakuna at iba pang hakbang pangkalusugan upang mapigilan ito."});

