ADDART({id:"persia",t:"The Persian Empire",c:"History",i:"🏛️",w:"Achaemenid Empire",r:["irancountry","mesop","greece","alexander","silk"],
p:[["The first superpower","The Achaemenid Persian Empire, founded by Cyrus the Great around 550 BCE, was the largest empire the world had yet seen."],["Ruling many peoples","It governed dozens of peoples, from Egypt to India, often allowing them to keep their own customs and religions."],["Roads and messengers","Its Royal Road and relay messengers carried news across the empire at remarkable speed."]],
l:"The Persian Empire, known to historians as the Achaemenid Empire, was founded by Cyrus the Great in about 550 BCE in what is now Iran. At its height under Darius I, it stretched from the Balkans and Egypt in the west to the Indus River in the east, ruling perhaps 40 percent of the world’s population. It was famous for its organized government, roads, tolerance of local customs and the great palace city of Persepolis. Alexander the Great conquered it in 330 BCE.",
s:[["Cyrus the Great","Cyrus II, a prince of the Persians in southwestern Iran, defeated the Medes around 550 BCE, then conquered the rich kingdom of Lydia in Anatolia and the city of Babylon in 539 BCE. He allowed exiled peoples, including the Jews held in Babylon, to return home and rebuild their temples, and he is remembered in the Bible as a liberator."],["Darius and the satrapies","Darius I, who ruled from 522 to 486 BCE, organized the empire into provinces called satrapies, each run by a governor, or satrap, who collected taxes and kept order. Royal inspectors known as the eyes and ears of the king checked on them. Darius introduced standard coins, weights and measures, which helped trade."],["The Royal Road","The Royal Road ran about 2,700 kilometers from Sardis, near the Aegean Sea, to Susa in Iran. Along it were relay stations with fresh horses, so royal messengers could cover the distance in about a week. The Greek historian Herodotus admired them, and his words are now the unofficial motto of the United States Postal Service."],["Wars with Greece","The Persians clashed with Greek city-states after Greek cities in Anatolia rebelled. A Persian army was defeated at Marathon in 490 BCE, and a huge invasion led by Xerxes I was turned back at Salamis and Plataea in 480 and 479 BCE, after the famous stand of the Spartans at Thermopylae. The wars shaped how Greeks, and later Europeans, saw Persia."]],
k:[["Founded","c. 550 BCE by Cyrus the Great"],["Capitals","Pasargadae, Persepolis, Susa, Babylon, Ecbatana"],["Greatest extent","About 5.5 million km² under Darius I"],["Religion","Zoroastrianism was important among the rulers"],["Ended","330 BCE, conquered by Alexander the Great"]],
d:[["c. 550 BCE","Cyrus the Great founds the empire"],["539 BCE","Cyrus captures Babylon"],["522 BCE","Darius I becomes king"],["c. 518 BCE","Building of Persepolis begins"],["480 BCE","Xerxes invades Greece"],["330 BCE","Alexander the Great burns Persepolis; the empire falls"]],
s2:[["Persepolis","Darius began building Persepolis around 518 BCE as a ceremonial capital. Its great halls stood on a huge stone terrace, with columns about 20 meters tall. Carvings on its staircases show people from all over the empire, Egyptians, Indians, Ethiopians and many more, bringing gifts to the king. Alexander the Great burned it in 330 BCE, but its ruins remain impressive."],["A tolerant empire","The Persians usually let conquered peoples keep their languages, laws and gods, as long as they paid taxes and stayed loyal. Inscriptions were written in several languages, such as Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian. This approach helped hold together a vast empire of many cultures."],["Zoroastrianism","Many Persians followed Zoroastrianism, a religion based on the teachings of the prophet Zarathustra, or Zoroaster. It teaches a struggle between good and evil, and honors fire as a symbol of purity and truth. Zoroastrianism influenced later religions, and it is still practiced today by small communities, including the Parsis of India."],["The Cyrus Cylinder","The Cyrus Cylinder is a clay cylinder inscribed in Babylonian cuneiform after Cyrus captured Babylon in 539 BCE. It describes how he restored temples and let displaced peoples return home. Now in the British Museum, it is sometimes described as an early charter of rights, though historians debate this view."],["Later Persian empires","After Alexander, Persia was ruled by the Seleucids, then by the Parthians, who fought Rome for centuries, and from 224 CE by the Sasanian Empire, a great power rivaling the Byzantines until Arab Muslim armies conquered it in the 650s. Persian language and culture then deeply shaped the Islamic world."],["Legacy","Persian ideas of government, such as provinces, roads and postal systems, influenced later empires, including those of Alexander and Rome. Persian art, gardens and architecture spread across Asia. Iran’s people still celebrate Nowruz, the Persian New Year, at the spring equinox, a festival with roots going back thousands of years."]],
sp:["Neither snow nor rain","The Greek historian Herodotus wrote that nothing mortal travels faster than the Persian messengers, who were stopped by neither snow, nor rain, nor heat, nor gloom of night from completing their rounds. These words, carved on the James Farley Post Office in New York City, are often quoted as the creed of mail carriers in the United States, more than 2,400 years later."],
g:[["Satrapy","A province of the Persian Empire"],["Achaemenid","The royal family that ruled the first Persian Empire"],["Cuneiform","Wedge-shaped writing pressed into clay"],["Zoroastrianism","An ancient Persian religion founded by Zarathustra"]],
f:["The Persian Empire may have ruled about 40 percent of the world’s people.","Its Royal Road was about 2,700 kilometers long.","Cyrus the Great allowed exiles in Babylon to return home."],
fun:"Words written about Persian royal messengers 2,400 years ago are carved on a post office in New York.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Persiano ay itinatag ni Ciro ang Dakila noong mga 550 BCE sa kasalukuyang Iran. Ito ang pinakamalaking imperyo noong panahon nito, kilala sa maayos na pamahalaan at mahabang Royal Road."});

ADDART({id:"kush",t:"The Kingdom of Kush",c:"History",i:"🔻",w:"Kingdom of Kush",r:["egypt","nile","pyramids","aksum","egyptcountry"],
p:[["Africa’s black pharaohs","Kings of Kush, from what is now Sudan, conquered and ruled Egypt as its 25th dynasty in the 700s BCE."],["More pyramids than Egypt","Kush built more than 200 pyramids, more than ancient Egypt."],["Iron and gold","Kush grew rich from gold, ivory and trade, and its city of Meroë was a great center of ironworking."]],
l:"The Kingdom of Kush was an ancient African civilization along the Nile River, in the region called Nubia, in present-day Sudan and southern Egypt. It flourished for more than a thousand years, from about 1070 BCE to 350 CE, with earlier Kushite kingdoms going back much further. Its kings ruled Egypt for nearly a century, and its later capital, Meroë, became a center of trade, ironworking and pyramid building. Kush developed its own script, Meroitic.",
s:[["Land of the bow","Nubia lies south of Egypt, where the Nile flows through deserts and rocky cataracts. Egyptians called it Ta-Seti, the land of the bow, because Nubian archers were famous. An earlier Nubian kingdom centered on Kerma traded and fought with Egypt as early as 2500 BCE, before Egypt conquered Nubia around 1500 BCE."],["Rise of Kush","As Egypt weakened after about 1070 BCE, an independent kingdom of Kush arose with its capital at Napata, near the holy mountain of Jebel Barkal. Its kings worshipped Egyptian gods such as Amun, built temples and used Egyptian writing, while keeping their own traditions."],["Pharaohs of Egypt","Around 747 BCE, King Piye of Kush marched north and conquered Egypt, uniting the two lands. He and his successors ruled as Egypt’s 25th dynasty, restoring old temples and reviving classical styles. The most famous, Taharqa, built monuments across Egypt and Nubia. In the 660s BCE, Assyrian invaders drove the Kushites back to Nubia."],["Meroë","Around the 500s to 300s BCE, the royal center shifted south to Meroë, between the Nile and Atbara rivers. Meroë was surrounded by good farmland and grazing land and lay on trade routes to the Red Sea and inner Africa. Its people smelted iron on such a scale that heaps of slag still mark the site, and some have called it the Birmingham of ancient Africa."]],
k:[["Location","Nubia, in present-day Sudan and southern Egypt"],["Period","c. 1070 BCE–c. 350 CE"],["Capitals","Napata, later Meroë"],["Script","Meroitic, not yet fully understood"],["Pyramids","More than 200"]],
d:[["c. 2500 BCE","The Kerma culture rises in Nubia"],["c. 1070 BCE","Kush becomes independent from Egypt"],["c. 747 BCE","King Piye conquers Egypt"],["690 BCE","Taharqa becomes pharaoh"],["c. 300 BCE","Royal burials move to Meroë"],["c. 350 CE","Kush declines; Aksum attacks the region"]],
s2:[["Pyramids of Kush","Kushite kings and queens were buried under steep, narrow pyramids, built at El-Kurru, Nuri and Meroë. There are more than 200 of them, more than in Egypt, though they are much smaller, usually less than 30 meters tall. The pyramids of Meroë are a UNESCO World Heritage Site."],["The kandakes","Several powerful queens ruled Kush, known by the title kandake, from which the name Candace comes. Queen Amanirenas led armies against the Romans in the 20s BCE after they took over Egypt. Her forces captured a bronze head of the emperor Augustus and buried it beneath a temple step, where archaeologists found it in 1910."],["Meroitic writing","The Kushites developed their own script, Meroitic, written in two forms, one using signs like hieroglyphs and the other a cursive form. Scholars can read the sounds, because the signs are known, but they understand only a little of the language. Fully deciphering it is one of the great puzzles of archaeology."],["Trade and wealth","Kush traded gold, ivory, ebony, incense, animal skins and enslaved people with Egypt, the Mediterranean world and Arabia. Exotic animals from Kush, such as elephants and giraffes, amazed foreigners. Kushite elephants may have been trained for war."],["Decline","Kush declined in the first centuries CE, perhaps because of changing trade routes to the Red Sea, overuse of land and forests for ironworking, and pressure from nomadic peoples. Around 350 CE, an army of Aksum, from what is now Ethiopia and Eritrea, campaigned in the region, and Kush faded away."],["Rediscovering Kush","For a long time, Kush was overlooked by historians who focused on Egypt. Archaeologists working in Sudan since the 1900s, including teams from Sudan, Europe and the United States, have revealed its cities, temples and art, showing it as a great African civilization in its own right."]],
sp:["The head of Augustus","The Meroë Head is a bronze head of the Roman emperor Augustus, larger than life, with eyes of glass and stone. It probably came from a statue in Egypt that Kushite forces seized during raids in about 24 BCE. Burying it under the steps of a victory temple meant that people walked over the emperor’s head every time they entered. It is now in the British Museum."],
g:[["Nubia","The region along the Nile in southern Egypt and Sudan"],["Cataract","A stretch of shallow rapids in a river"],["Kandake","A title of queens of Kush"],["Slag","Waste left over after smelting metal"]],
f:["Kush built more pyramids than ancient Egypt.","Kings of Kush ruled Egypt as its 25th dynasty.","The Meroitic language is still not fully understood."],
fun:"Sudan has more pyramids than Egypt.",
tl:"Ang Kaharian ng Kush ay sinaunang sibilisasyon sa Nubia, sa kasalukuyang Sudan. Namuno ang mga hari nito sa Ehipto, at nagtayo sila ng mahigit 200 piramide, higit pa kaysa sa Ehipto."});

ADDART({id:"aksum",t:"The Kingdom of Aksum",c:"History",i:"🗿",w:"Kingdom of Aksum",r:["ethiopiacountry","kush","islam","silk","rome"],
p:[["A trading power","Aksum, in what is now northern Ethiopia and Eritrea, was a major trading empire from about the 1st to the 7th centuries CE."],["Giant obelisks","Its people raised tall carved stone stelae, among the largest single stones ever set up."],["An early Christian kingdom","Its king Ezana adopted Christianity in the 300s, making Aksum one of the first Christian states."]],
l:"The Kingdom of Aksum was an ancient state in the northern Horn of Africa, in present-day Ethiopia and Eritrea, that flourished from about 100 to 700 CE. Through the Red Sea port of Adulis, it traded with Rome, Egypt, Arabia, Persia and India. Aksum minted its own coins, developed the Ge’ez script and raised huge carved stelae. A Persian writer of the 200s counted it among the four great powers of the world, with Rome, Persia and China.",
s:[["Highland kingdom","Aksum grew in the fertile highlands of Tigray, where farmers grew wheat and barley with plows and terraces. Its capital, also called Aksum, became a city of palaces, tombs and monuments. Over time, its kings controlled lands on both sides of the Red Sea, including parts of Yemen."],["Trade","Ships from Aksum’s port of Adulis carried ivory, gold, rhinoceros horn, incense and tortoiseshell to the Roman world, and brought back cloth, glass, wine, olive oil and metal goods. Aksum was a link between the Mediterranean and the Indian Ocean trade routes."],["Coins and writing","Aksum was one of the first African states, after Egypt and Carthage, to mint its own coins, in gold, silver and bronze, from the late 200s. It developed Ge’ez, a written language and script that is still used in the Ethiopian Orthodox Church, and from which the scripts of Amharic and Tigrinya developed."],["King Ezana","King Ezana, who ruled in the mid-300s, converted to Christianity, influenced by a Syrian Christian named Frumentius, who became the first bishop of Ethiopia. Aksum’s coins began to show a cross instead of the earlier crescent and disk. Ezana also led campaigns into the Nile valley, toward Kush."]],
k:[["Location","Northern Ethiopia and Eritrea"],["Period","c. 100–700 CE"],["Capital","Aksum"],["Main port","Adulis, on the Red Sea"],["Language","Ge’ez"]],
d:[["c. 100 CE","Aksum emerges as a kingdom"],["c. 270s","Aksum begins minting coins"],["c. 330s","King Ezana adopts Christianity"],["c. 520s","Aksum conquers parts of Yemen"],["615","Early Muslims find refuge in Aksum"],["c. 700s","Aksum declines as trade routes shift"]],
s2:[["The stelae","Aksum’s rulers raised great stone stelae over royal tombs, carved to look like multi-story buildings with doors and windows. The largest, about 33 meters long, probably fell and broke when it was raised. Another, about 24 meters tall, was taken to Rome by Italian troops in 1937 and returned to Ethiopia in 2005."],["Refuge for early Muslims","In about 615, early followers of the Prophet Muhammad, facing persecution in Mecca, fled across the Red Sea to Aksum. The Christian king, known in Islamic tradition as the Negus, welcomed and protected them. This event, the first hijra or migration in Islam, is remembered as a story of kindness between faiths."],["The Ark of the Covenant","Ethiopian tradition holds that the Ark of the Covenant, the chest that held the tablets of the Ten Commandments, was brought to Ethiopia by Menelik I, said to be the son of King Solomon and the Queen of Sheba. Many Ethiopian Christians believe it is kept in a chapel at the Church of Our Lady Mary of Zion in Aksum, seen only by its guardian monk."],["Decline","From the 600s, Aksum declined. The rise of Islamic powers changed trade routes in the Red Sea, the port of Adulis lost importance, and soil erosion and changes in rainfall may have weakened farming. The center of Ethiopian power moved south into the highlands, though Aksum remained a holy city."],["Lalibela and after","Later Ethiopian dynasties saw themselves as heirs of Aksum. In the 1100s and 1200s, the Zagwe kings built churches carved down into solid rock at Lalibela. The Solomonic dynasty, which ruled from 1270 until 1974, claimed descent from Solomon and Sheba through the kings of Aksum."],["Aksum today","Aksum is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and a place of pilgrimage for Ethiopian Orthodox Christians, especially during the festival of Hidar Tsion in November. Its ruins of palaces, tombs and stelae draw scholars and visitors, and excavations continue to reveal more about this great African kingdom."]],
sp:["The four great kingdoms","The Persian prophet Mani, writing in the 200s CE, described the four great kingdoms of the world: Rome, Persia, Aksum and China. This shows how important and well known Aksum was in its time, as a trading and military power linking Africa, the Mediterranean, Arabia and India. Its gold coins have been found as far away as India."],
g:[["Stela","A tall carved stone pillar, often a monument (plural: stelae)"],["Ge’ez","The ancient language and script of Ethiopia"],["Hijra","Migration; in Islam, the journey of early Muslims to safety"],["Horn of Africa","The northeastern corner of Africa"]],
f:["Aksum was one of the first states to adopt Christianity.","One of its stelae was taken to Rome in 1937 and returned in 2005.","Its Ge’ez script is still used in Ethiopian churches."],
fun:"A 24-meter stone stela from Aksum stood in Rome for almost 70 years before returning home in 2005.",
tl:"Ang Kaharian ng Aksum sa kasalukuyang Ethiopia at Eritrea ay makapangyarihang kahariang pangkalakalan mula ika-1 hanggang ika-7 siglo. Isa ito sa mga unang estadong Kristiyano at kilala sa matataas na batong stelae."});

;
