addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{Object.assign(window.LOC=window.LOC||{},{mali:[-3.0,16.77,"Mali",30,"Timbuktu, in the Mali Empire"],zimbabwe:[30.93,-20.27,"Zimbabwe",30,"Great Zimbabwe"],benin:[5.62,6.34,"Nigeria",30,"Benin City"]})});

ADDART({id:"mali",t:"The Mali Empire",c:"History",i:"🐪",w:"Mali Empire",r:["mansamusa","sahara","islam","ghanacountry","trade"],
p:[["Empire of gold","The Mali Empire, in West Africa, controlled rich goldfields and the trade routes across the Sahara from the 1200s to the 1400s."],["Sundiata and Mansa Musa","It was founded by Sundiata Keita, and under Mansa Musa it became famous across the world."],["City of learning","Timbuktu grew into a great center of Islamic scholarship, trade and books."]],
l:"The Mali Empire was one of the largest and richest empires in African history. Founded by Sundiata Keita around 1235, it stretched across West Africa from the Atlantic coast to the middle of the Niger River, in parts of present-day Mali, Senegal, Guinea, Mauritania and other countries. Its wealth came from gold, salt and trade across the Sahara. Under Mansa Musa in the 1300s, its fame reached Europe and the Middle East, and its cities became centers of Islamic learning.",
s:[["Before Mali","Before Mali, the Ghana Empire, also called Wagadu, controlled trade in West Africa from about the 300s to the 1000s. It was located in what is now Mauritania and Mali, northwest of present-day Ghana. Arab writers called it the land of gold. After it declined, several smaller states competed for power."],["Sundiata Keita","According to the epic told by griots, Sundiata was a prince of the Mandinka people who could not walk as a child and was mocked, but grew up strong. After going into exile, he returned to lead an alliance against the Sosso king Sumanguru, defeating him at the Battle of Kirina around 1235, and founded the Mali Empire."],["Gold and salt","West Africa had gold, but little salt, which was needed for health and to preserve food. The Sahara had salt mines, such as Taghaza, but no gold. Merchants crossed the desert in camel caravans, trading salt, cloth, horses and books for gold, ivory and enslaved people. Mali grew rich by controlling and taxing this trade."],["Government","The ruler, called the mansa, governed through provincial chiefs and a large army. The traveler Ibn Battuta, who visited Mali in 1352 and 1353, praised its safety, writing that travelers had no fear of thieves or violence. Islam was the religion of the court and merchants, while many people kept traditional beliefs."]],
k:[["Founded","c. 1235 by Sundiata Keita"],["Capital","Niani (probably)"],["Famous cities","Timbuktu, Djenné, Gao"],["Famous ruler","Mansa Musa (ruled c. 1312–1337)"],["Main trade","Gold, salt, copper, cloth, books"]],
d:[["c. 1235","Sundiata defeats Sumanguru at Kirina"],["c. 1312","Mansa Musa becomes ruler"],["1324","Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage to Mecca"],["c. 1327","Djinguereber Mosque built in Timbuktu"],["1352","Ibn Battuta visits Mali"],["1400s","Mali declines; the Songhai Empire rises"]],
s2:[["Mansa Musa’s pilgrimage","In 1324 Mansa Musa traveled to Mecca with a caravan said to include thousands of people and camels carrying gold. In Cairo he gave away so much gold that its value fell for years. The journey made Mali famous, and the Catalan Atlas, a map made in Spain in 1375, shows Mansa Musa on his throne holding a golden nugget."],["Timbuktu","Timbuktu, near the Niger River at the edge of the Sahara, became a meeting place of camel and river trade. Mansa Musa built the Djinguereber Mosque there, and later the Sankore Mosque became a center of learning. Scholars came to study law, astronomy, mathematics and medicine, and books were among the most valuable goods traded."],["Mud-brick mosques","West African builders created mosques of sun-dried mud brick with wooden beams sticking out of the walls. The beams act as scaffolding for the yearly replastering of the walls after the rains. The Great Mosque of Djenné, rebuilt in 1907, is the largest mud-brick building in the world, and its community replasters it every year in a festival."],["Griots","Griots, or jeliw, are West African storytellers, singers and historians who memorize the histories of families and kingdoms. The Epic of Sundiata has been passed down by griots for centuries. They often perform with the kora, a harp-lute with 21 strings."],["Decline and Songhai","After Mansa Musa, Mali was weakened by quarrels over the throne and attacks by Tuareg, Mossi and other peoples. In the 1400s, the city of Gao broke away, and the Songhai Empire under Sunni Ali and Askia Muhammad became the greatest power in the region. Mali shrank to a small state."],["Legacy","The Mali Empire showed the wealth, learning and organization of medieval Africa. Mandinka culture, the Manding languages and the griot tradition remain strong across West Africa. When the French colony of Soudan became independent in 1960, it took the name Mali in honor of the empire."]],
sp:["The manuscripts of Timbuktu","Families in Timbuktu have kept hundreds of thousands of manuscripts, some dating back to the 1200s, on subjects such as religion, law, astronomy, medicine, poetry and trade. In 2012 and 2013, when armed groups occupied the city, librarians and families secretly smuggled hundreds of thousands of manuscripts to safety in Bamako, hidden in boxes and rice sacks."],
g:[["Mansa","The title of the ruler of Mali"],["Caravan","A group of travelers and animals crossing a desert together"],["Griot","A West African storyteller, musician and historian"],["Pilgrimage","A journey to a holy place"]],
f:["Mansa Musa is often called one of the richest people in history.","The Great Mosque of Djenné is the largest mud-brick building in the world.","The country of Mali is named after the empire."],
fun:"Mansa Musa gave away so much gold in Cairo that its value fell there for years.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Mali sa Kanlurang Africa ay itinatag ni Sundiata Keita noong mga 1235. Yumaman ito sa kalakalan ng ginto at asin sa Sahara, at naging tanyag sa ilalim ni Mansa Musa. Ang Timbuktu ay naging sentro ng karunungan."});

ADDART({id:"zimbabwe",t:"Great Zimbabwe",c:"History",i:"🧱",w:"Great Zimbabwe",r:["southafricacountry","trade","mali","decol"],
p:[["City of stone","Great Zimbabwe was a city of massive stone walls built between the 11th and 15th centuries in southern Africa."],["No mortar","Its walls, up to 11 meters high, were built from granite blocks fitted together without mortar."],["A nation’s name","The country of Zimbabwe takes its name from the site, which means houses of stone in the Shona language."]],
l:"Great Zimbabwe is the ruined capital of a kingdom that flourished in southeastern Africa from about the 11th to the 15th centuries, in what is now Zimbabwe. Built by ancestors of the Shona people, it is the largest ancient stone structure in sub-Saharan Africa. At its height, perhaps 10,000 to 18,000 people lived there. The city grew rich from cattle, farming and trade in gold and ivory with the Swahili coast, linking it to Indian Ocean trade as far as China.",
s:[["The site","Great Zimbabwe covers about 7 square kilometers. It has three main parts: the Hill Complex, built on a rocky hill and probably the oldest; the Great Enclosure in the valley below; and the Valley Ruins, where many people lived. Houses were made of daga, a mix of mud and gravel, inside stone walls."],["The Great Enclosure","The Great Enclosure is the largest single ancient structure south of the Sahara. Its outer wall is about 250 meters long, up to 11 meters high and up to 5 meters thick, built from an estimated 900,000 granite blocks. Inside stands a solid conical tower about 10 meters tall, whose purpose is unknown, perhaps a symbol of a grain store or of power."],["Trade","Great Zimbabwe’s rulers controlled gold mined in the region and traded it, with ivory, to Swahili merchants at ports such as Sofala and Kilwa on the Indian Ocean. In return came glass beads, cloth and porcelain. Archaeologists have found Chinese pottery, Persian bowls and an Arab coin at the site."],["The Zimbabwe birds","Eight soapstone birds, each on top of a tall column, were found at Great Zimbabwe. They are unlike any other carvings, combining features of birds such as eagles with human-like details. They may have represented royal ancestors. The Zimbabwe bird now appears on the country’s flag and coat of arms."]],
k:[["Location","Near Masvingo, Zimbabwe"],["Period","c. 1000s–1400s"],["Builders","Ancestors of the Shona people"],["Great Enclosure wall","About 250 m long and up to 11 m high"],["World Heritage Site","Since 1986"]],
d:[["c. 1000s","Building begins on the Hill Complex"],["c. 1300s","Great Enclosure built; the city reaches its height"],["c. 1450","Great Zimbabwe is largely abandoned"],["1871","German explorer Karl Mauch visits the ruins"],["1905–1929","Archaeologists confirm the African origin of the site"],["1980","Zimbabwe takes its name from the site at independence"]],
s2:[["Why it declined","By around 1450, Great Zimbabwe was largely abandoned. Overgrazing of cattle, shortages of firewood and farmland, and the shift of gold trade routes to the north probably played a part. Power moved to new states such as the Mutapa kingdom in the north and the Torwa state at Khami in the west."],["A stolen history","When European colonists arrived in the late 1800s, many refused to believe that Africans had built Great Zimbabwe. They claimed it was made by Phoenicians, Arabs or the Queen of Sheba. Treasure hunters damaged the site. Archaeologists David Randall-MacIver in 1905 and Gertrude Caton-Thompson in 1929 showed it was built by local African people."],["Colonial denial","The white minority government of Rhodesia, as the country was then called, continued to promote false ideas about the builders and pressured archaeologists to keep quiet. For Africans fighting for independence, Great Zimbabwe became a symbol of their history and abilities, and they took its name for the new nation in 1980."],["Return of the birds","Some of the Zimbabwe birds were taken abroad in the colonial period. One was sold to Cecil Rhodes and kept at his house in South Africa, and another went to a museum in Germany. South Africa returned four birds in 1981 in exchange for other objects, and Germany returned its part of a bird in 2003, though the bird bought by Rhodes is still at his former house."],["Life in the city","Most people lived in round houses with thatched roofs in the valley. They herded cattle, which were a measure of wealth, grew sorghum and millet, and worked gold, copper and iron. The ruling elite lived in the stone enclosures, and craftsmen made jewelry, tools and weapons."],["Similar sites","Great Zimbabwe is the largest of about 200 stone-walled sites known as zimbabwes in the region, including Khami and Mapungubwe, the latter in present-day South Africa, which was an earlier kingdom famous for a small golden rhinoceros found in a royal grave."]],
sp:["The golden rhinoceros","At Mapungubwe, a kingdom that flourished just before Great Zimbabwe, near where South Africa, Zimbabwe and Botswana meet, archaeologists in 1933 found a small rhinoceros made of thin gold foil over a wooden core in a royal grave. Made in the 1200s, it is a symbol of the region’s early kingdoms, and South Africa now awards the Order of Mapungubwe, its highest honor."],
g:[["Enclosure","An area surrounded by a wall or fence"],["Mortar","A paste used to hold bricks or stones together"],["Shona","A people and language of Zimbabwe"],["Soapstone","A soft stone that is easy to carve"]],
f:["Zimbabwe means houses of stone in Shona.","The Great Enclosure was built without mortar.","The Zimbabwe bird appears on the country’s flag."],
fun:"Zimbabwe is one of very few countries named after an archaeological site.",
tl:"Ang Great Zimbabwe ay lungsod ng malalaking pader na bato sa timog Africa na itinayo mula ika-11 hanggang ika-15 siglo ng mga ninuno ng mga Shona. Ang bansang Zimbabwe ay ipinangalan dito."});

ADDART({id:"benin",t:"The Kingdom of Benin",c:"History",i:"🗿",w:"Kingdom of Benin",r:["nigeriacountry","decol","trade","mali"],
p:[["A West African kingdom","The Kingdom of Benin, in what is now southern Nigeria, was ruled by kings called obas from about the 1200s."],["Master metalworkers","Its artists made thousands of brass and bronze plaques and sculptures of extraordinary skill."],["Walls and a planned city","Benin City was surrounded by earthworks that stretched for thousands of kilometers."]],
l:"The Kingdom of Benin was a powerful state in the forests of West Africa, in present-day southern Nigeria, not to be confused with the modern country of Benin. Founded by the Edo people, it flourished from about the 13th century until 1897, when a British force captured and burned Benin City. The kingdom was famous for its kings, the obas, its organized government, its vast earthworks and its art, especially the brass works known as the Benin Bronzes.",
s:[["Origins","According to Edo tradition, Benin was first ruled by kings called the Ogiso. Around the 1200s, a prince from the Yoruba city of Ife, Oranmiyan, came to Benin, and his son Eweka I became the first oba of a new dynasty. The current oba, Ewuare II, who came to the throne in 2016, is a member of this line."],["Oba Ewuare the Great","Ewuare the Great, who ruled in the mid-1400s, expanded the kingdom through conquest and rebuilt Benin City. He strengthened the walls and moats, organized the city into districts for craftsmen’s guilds, and set up a council of chiefs. Later obas, such as Esigie in the 1500s, expanded the kingdom further."],["Trade with Europe","Portuguese explorers reached Benin in the 1480s, and Benin traded pepper, ivory, cloth and, for a time, captives in exchange for brass, cloth and guns. The Portuguese, and later the Dutch and English, described Benin City as large, orderly and well governed. Portuguese soldiers sometimes appear in Benin art."],["Benin City","Visitors in the 1600s compared Benin City to the great cities of Europe. A Dutch writer in 1668 described wide straight streets, large houses and the oba’s palace, a huge complex with galleries decorated with brass plaques. The city was laid out in a planned pattern with walls and moats."]],
k:[["Location","Southern Nigeria"],["People","Edo"],["Ruler","The oba"],["Period","c. 1200s to 1897, with the monarchy continuing today"],["Famous for","The Benin Bronzes and the Walls of Benin"]],
d:[["c. 1200s","Eweka I becomes the first oba"],["c. 1440","Ewuare the Great begins his reign"],["1485","Portuguese reach Benin"],["1897","British forces capture and burn Benin City"],["1914","The British restore the oba as a traditional ruler"],["2022","Germany returns ownership of over 1,000 Benin Bronzes to Nigeria"]],
s2:[["The Benin Bronzes","The Benin Bronzes are thousands of plaques, heads, figures and other objects made mostly of brass, as well as ivory and wood. Made by a royal guild of casters using the lost-wax method, they recorded the history of the kingdom, showing obas, warriors, ceremonies and Portuguese traders. Heads of past obas stood on altars in their memory."],["The lost-wax method","To make a Benin brass, an artist shaped a model in wax over a clay core, covered it with more clay and heated it so that the wax melted away. Molten brass was poured into the space left behind. When the metal cooled, the clay was broken away to reveal the sculpture. The guild of casters still works in Benin City today."],["The Walls of Benin","Benin was surrounded by a network of earthen walls and ditches, built over centuries, that enclosed the city and surrounding villages and farmlands. By some estimates, they totaled about 16,000 kilometers, making them one of the largest earthworks ever built. Most were destroyed in the 20th century."],["The 1897 expedition","In 1897, after a British party trying to reach the oba against his wishes was attacked and most of its members killed, Britain sent a large military force. It captured Benin City, burned much of it and sent Oba Ovonramwen into exile. Soldiers looted thousands of artworks, which were sold to museums and collectors in Europe and America."],["Returning the treasures","For more than a century, Nigeria and the royal court of Benin have asked for the looted artworks to be returned. Since 2021, institutions in Germany, Britain, the United States and other countries have begun returning Benin Bronzes, and Nigeria plans a museum in Benin City to display them."],["The Queen Mother mask","One of the most famous Benin works is an ivory pendant mask of Idia, the mother of Oba Esigie, who helped him win a war in the early 1500s and became the first iyoba, or queen mother. A copy of the mask was the emblem of FESTAC 77, a great festival of African culture held in Lagos in 1977, after Britain declined to lend the original."]],
sp:["Two Benins","The Kingdom of Benin should not be confused with the Republic of Benin, a country to the west of Nigeria. The modern country was known as Dahomey until 1975, when it chose the name Benin after the Bight of Benin, the bay on which it lies. That bay had itself been named after the old Kingdom of Benin, which lies entirely within present-day Nigeria."],
g:[["Oba","The king of Benin"],["Guild","An association of craftspeople in the same trade"],["Lost-wax casting","A way of making metal sculptures from a wax model"],["Earthworks","Large banks and ditches made of earth"]],
f:["The Benin Bronzes are mostly made of brass.","The Walls of Benin may have totaled about 16,000 kilometers.","The Kingdom of Benin is in present-day Nigeria, not the country of Benin."],
fun:"The Walls of Benin may once have been longer than the Great Wall of China’s Ming section.",
tl:"Ang Kaharian ng Benin sa kasalukuyang timog Nigeria ay pinamunuan ng mga oba mula noong mga ika-13 siglo. Kilala ito sa mga Benin Bronze, mga eskulturang tanso na kinuha ng mga Briton noong 1897 at ngayon ay unti-unting ibinabalik."});

;
