addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>{Object.assign(window.LOC=window.LOC||{},{inca:[-71.97,-13.53,"Peru",30,"Cusco, capital of the Inca Empire"],caliphate:[44.4,33.3,"Iraq",36,"Baghdad, capital of the Abbasid Caliphate"]})});

ADDART({id:"inca",t:"The Inca Empire",c:"History",i:"🦙",w:"Inca Empire",r:["machupicchu","perucountry","andes","aztec","maya"],
p:[["Largest in the Americas","In the early 1500s, the Inca Empire was the largest empire in the Americas, stretching about 4,000 kilometers along the Andes."],["Roads in the mountains","The Inca built a road network of about 40,000 kilometers, with rope bridges across deep gorges."],["Without writing as we know it","They kept records with knotted cords called quipu."]],
l:"The Inca Empire, called Tawantinsuyu, the Four Regions Together, by its people, was the largest state in the Americas before the arrival of Europeans. From its capital, Cusco, in the Andes of Peru, it grew in less than a century to rule perhaps 10 million people in present-day Peru, Ecuador, Bolivia, Chile, Argentina and Colombia. The Inca were brilliant engineers, farmers and organizers. In 1532 Spanish conquistadors led by Francisco Pizarro began their conquest of the empire.",
s:[["From Cusco to empire","The Inca began as a small group in the valley of Cusco around the 1200s. In 1438, according to tradition, the ruler Pachacuti defeated the rival Chanka people and began a period of rapid conquest. He and his son Topa Inca and grandson Huayna Capac expanded the empire north to Ecuador and south to central Chile."],["The Sapa Inca","The emperor, called the Sapa Inca, or Unique Inca, was believed to be descended from Inti, the Sun god. He owned all the land and was treated as divine. When an emperor died, his body was mummified and continued to be cared for, consulted and even taken to festivals."],["Labor instead of taxes","The Inca had no money. Instead, people paid taxes in labor through a system called mit’a, working for set periods on farms, roads, buildings, mines or in the army. In return, the state stored food in thousands of warehouses called qullqa and gave it out in times of need or for festivals."],["Farming the mountains","Inca farmers grew potatoes, maize, quinoa and many other crops on terraces built up steep mountainsides. They developed thousands of potato varieties and freeze-dried potatoes into chuño, which could be stored for years. Llamas and alpacas carried loads and provided wool and meat."]],
k:[["Name","Tawantinsuyu, the Four Regions Together"],["Capital","Cusco"],["Population","Perhaps 10 million or more"],["Height","Early 1500s"],["Language","Quechua"]],
d:[["c. 1200s","The Inca settle in the Cusco valley"],["1438","Pachacuti begins to build the empire"],["c. 1450","Machu Picchu is built"],["1527","Huayna Capac dies; civil war between Huáscar and Atahualpa"],["1532","Pizarro captures Atahualpa at Cajamarca"],["1572","The last Inca ruler, Túpac Amaru, is executed"]],
s2:[["The road system","The Qhapaq Ñan, or Royal Road, linked the empire with about 40,000 kilometers of roads, through deserts, forests and mountains up to 5,000 meters high. Relay runners called chasquis carried messages and goods, covering perhaps 240 kilometers a day. The road system is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site shared by six countries."],["Rope bridges","To cross deep gorges, the Inca wove suspension bridges from grass rope. The Q’eswachaka bridge in Peru, about 28 meters long, is still rebuilt every year by local communities using traditional methods, a practice recognized by UNESCO as part of the world’s intangible heritage."],["Quipu","The Inca kept records using quipu, cords of cotton or wool with knots tied in different positions and colors. Officials called quipucamayocs used them to record numbers such as census data, taxes and goods in storehouses. Some scholars think quipu may also have recorded stories or names, but no one has yet fully deciphered them."],["Spanish conquest","Pizarro arrived in 1532 with fewer than 200 men, at a time when the empire was weakened by a civil war between two brothers, Huáscar and Atahualpa, and by smallpox, which had spread ahead of the Spanish. At Cajamarca, the Spanish ambushed and captured Atahualpa. Though he paid a room full of gold and silver as ransom, they executed him in 1533."],["Resistance","Inca resistance continued for decades. Manco Inca led a great revolt in 1536 and set up an independent state in the forests of Vilcabamba. It survived until 1572, when the Spanish captured and executed Túpac Amaru, its last ruler. In 1780, a descendant who took the name Túpac Amaru II led a major rebellion against Spanish rule."],["Living heritage","Millions of people in the Andes still speak Quechua and keep Inca traditions in farming, weaving, music and festivals. Inti Raymi, the Festival of the Sun, is celebrated in Cusco every June. The rainbow flag of Cusco and the Andean wiphala flag are symbols of Indigenous Andean identity."]],
sp:["Cusco, the navel of the world","Cusco means navel or center in Quechua, and the Inca saw their capital as the center of the world. Its main temple, the Qorikancha, had walls covered in sheets of gold. The Spanish built churches and houses on top of Inca foundations, and in earthquakes many Spanish walls fell while the Inca walls beneath them stood firm. The famous twelve-angled stone, perfectly fitted into a wall, can still be seen today."],
g:[["Sapa Inca","The emperor of the Inca"],["Mit’a","A system of labor paid to the state as tax"],["Quipu","Knotted cords used to keep records"],["Conquistador","A Spanish conqueror in the Americas"]],
f:["The Inca Empire had no money.","Its roads totaled about 40,000 kilometers.","Inca farmers grew thousands of potato varieties."],
fun:"Inca walls in Cusco have survived earthquakes that toppled the Spanish buildings built on top of them.",
tl:"Ang Imperyong Inca ang pinakamalaking imperyo sa Amerika bago dumating ang mga Europeo. Mula sa kabisera nitong Cusco sa Peru, nagtayo ito ng malawak na network ng daan sa Andes. Sinakop ito ng mga Espanyol simula 1532."});

ADDART({id:"caliphate",t:"The Islamic Caliphates",c:"History",i:"🕌",w:"Caliphate",r:["islam","goldage","ibnsina","silk","byzant","crusades"],
p:[["Successors of the Prophet","After the Prophet Muhammad died in 632, his successors, called caliphs, led a state that grew into a vast empire."],["From Spain to Central Asia","Within about a century, the caliphate stretched from Spain and Morocco to the Indus River."],["Baghdad’s golden age","Under the Abbasids, Baghdad became one of the largest and most learned cities in the world."]],
l:"A caliphate is a state led by a caliph, meaning successor, regarded as the political and religious leader of the Muslim community after the Prophet Muhammad. The early caliphates, from 632 to 1258, built one of the largest empires in history, linking Europe, Africa and Asia. They spread the Arabic language and Islamic culture, promoted trade and learning, and presided over an age of great advances in science, medicine, mathematics and philosophy.",
s:[["The Rashidun","The first four caliphs, Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman and Ali, are called the Rashidun, or Rightly Guided, by Sunni Muslims. Between 632 and 661, Arab armies conquered the Sasanian Persian Empire and took Syria, Palestine and Egypt from the Byzantine Empire. Disagreement over who should lead the community led to the split between Sunni and Shia Muslims."],["The Umayyads","The Umayyad dynasty ruled from Damascus from 661 to 750. Their empire reached its greatest size, stretching from Spain in the west to Central Asia and the Indus valley in the east. They made Arabic the language of government, minted Islamic coins and built the Dome of the Rock in Jerusalem and the Great Mosque of Damascus."],["The Abbasids","In 750 the Abbasid family overthrew the Umayyads and moved the capital east. In 762 the caliph al-Mansur founded Baghdad, a round city on the Tigris River, which grew to perhaps a million people. Under caliphs such as Harun al-Rashid, Baghdad became a center of trade, wealth and culture."],["Al-Andalus","One Umayyad prince, Abd al-Rahman, escaped to Spain and founded an Umayyad state there in 756, which later declared itself a caliphate, with its capital at Córdoba. Muslim, Christian and Jewish scholars worked there, and Córdoba became one of the largest and most cultured cities in Europe. Muslim rule in parts of Spain lasted until 1492."]],
k:[["Began","632, after the death of the Prophet Muhammad"],["Main dynasties","Rashidun, Umayyad, Abbasid, Fatimid"],["Capitals","Medina, Damascus, Baghdad, Cairo, Córdoba"],["Greatest extent","Umayyad period, about 11 million km²"],["Abbasid Baghdad fell","1258, to the Mongols"]],
d:[["632","Abu Bakr becomes the first caliph"],["661","Umayyad dynasty begins in Damascus"],["711","Muslim armies enter Spain"],["750","Abbasids overthrow the Umayyads"],["762","Baghdad is founded"],["1258","Mongols sack Baghdad"]],
s2:[["The House of Wisdom","In Baghdad, the Abbasid caliphs supported scholars who translated Greek, Persian and Indian works into Arabic, in a center often called the House of Wisdom. Scholars such as al-Khwarizmi, whose name gives us the word algorithm and whose book gives us algebra, built on this knowledge. Many works of ancient Greek science survived because of these translations."],["Trade and paper","The caliphate linked the Mediterranean with the Indian Ocean and the Silk Road. Merchants traded spices, silk, textiles, glass and metalwork. Papermaking, learned from China, spread through the Islamic world from the 700s, making books cheaper and helping knowledge to spread. Baghdad had streets of booksellers."],["Science and medicine","Scholars under the caliphates made advances in astronomy, optics, chemistry and medicine. Hospitals in Baghdad, Damascus and Cairo treated patients and trained doctors. Ibn al-Haytham studied how light and vision work, and Ibn Sina wrote a medical encyclopedia used for centuries. Hindu-Arabic numerals, including zero, spread to Europe through Arabic texts."],["The Fatimids","The Fatimids, a Shia dynasty, founded a rival caliphate in North Africa in 909 and conquered Egypt in 969, founding the city of Cairo. There they established al-Azhar, a mosque and center of learning that remains one of the most important universities in the Muslim world."],["Decline and the Mongols","Over time, the Abbasid caliphs lost real power to local rulers and military commanders, such as the Seljuk Turks. In 1258 the Mongols under Hulagu Khan captured Baghdad, killed the caliph and destroyed much of the city. Legend says the Tigris ran black with ink from the books thrown into it."],["Later caliphs","After 1258, a line of Abbasid caliphs lived in Cairo under the Mamluk sultans, with little power. The Ottoman sultans later claimed the title of caliph. The Ottoman caliphate was abolished in 1924 by the new Republic of Türkiye, and since then no caliph has been widely recognized."]],
sp:["The round city of Baghdad","Al-Mansur’s Baghdad, built in the 760s, was designed as a perfect circle about 2 kilometers across, with the caliph’s palace and a great mosque at the center and four gates leading toward the corners of the empire. Thousands of workers, architects and engineers built it in about four years. Nothing of the round city survives above ground today, but it was one of the most famous examples of city planning in history."],
g:[["Caliph","A successor of the Prophet Muhammad as leader of the Muslim community"],["Dynasty","A series of rulers from the same family"],["Al-Andalus","The parts of Spain and Portugal under Muslim rule"],["Sunni and Shia","The two main branches of Islam"]],
f:["Baghdad was founded in 762 as a round city.","The words algebra and algorithm come from the work of a Baghdad scholar.","The last caliphate was abolished in 1924."],
fun:"The word algorithm comes from the name of the Baghdad scholar al-Khwarizmi.",
tl:"Ang mga kalipato ay mga estadong pinamunuan ng mga kalipa, mga kahalili ni Propeta Muhammad. Lumawak ang mga ito mula Espanya hanggang Gitnang Asya, at naging sentro ng karunungan ang Baghdad sa ilalim ng mga Abbasid."});

;
