ADDART({id:"slavetrade",t:"The Atlantic Slave Trade",c:"History",i:"⛓️",w:"Atlantic slave trade",r:["tubman","toussaint","lincoln","ghanacountry","benin"],
p:[["Forced migration","From the 1500s to the 1800s, about 12.5 million Africans were forcibly carried across the Atlantic Ocean to the Americas."],["A deadly voyage","Captives endured a terrible voyage in crowded ships, and around 1.8 million died on the way."],["Abolition","Enslaved people resisted and campaigners fought for abolition, and the trade and slavery were gradually outlawed in the 1800s."]],
l:"The Atlantic slave trade was the forced transportation of enslaved African people across the Atlantic Ocean, mostly to the Americas, from the 16th to the 19th centuries. Europeans and Americans bought captives in West and Central Africa and shipped them to work on plantations and in mines in Brazil, the Caribbean, North America and elsewhere. It was one of the largest forced migrations in history, causing immense suffering and shaping the societies of Africa, Europe and the Americas to this day.",
s:[["How it began","After Europeans colonized the Americas from the 1490s, they wanted workers for sugar plantations and mines. Many Indigenous people died from violence and diseases brought by Europeans. Portuguese and Spanish colonists began bringing enslaved Africans, and soon the British, French, Dutch and others joined the trade."],["The triangular trade","Ships from Europe carried goods such as cloth, guns and metal to Africa and exchanged them for captives. They then crossed the Atlantic, a journey known as the Middle Passage, and sold the enslaved people in the Americas. Ships returned to Europe with sugar, tobacco, cotton and coffee produced by enslaved labor."],["Capture in Africa","Most captives were taken in wars, raids and kidnappings, or as punishment, and sold to European traders at forts and ports along the coast, from Senegal to Angola. Some African rulers and merchants profited from the trade, while it brought violence, insecurity and loss of population to many regions."],["The Middle Passage","Captives were chained and packed tightly below deck for voyages of one to three months, with little air, food or water. Disease spread quickly. Around 1.8 million people died during the crossing. Some captives rebelled on board or refused to eat, and some leaped into the sea rather than live enslaved."]],
k:[["Period","Early 1500s to 1860s"],["People embarked","About 12.5 million"],["People who arrived","About 10.7 million"],["Largest destination","Brazil, about 4.9 million arrivals"],["Main regions of origin","West Central Africa, the Bight of Benin, the Bight of Biafra, the Gold Coast"]],
d:[["1526","First known slave voyage directly from Africa to the Americas"],["1791","Revolution begins in Saint-Domingue (Haiti)"],["1807","Britain and the United States ban the slave trade (US ban effective 1808)"],["1833","Britain abolishes slavery in most of its empire"],["1865","The 13th Amendment ends slavery in the United States"],["1888","Brazil becomes the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery"]],
s2:[["Life under slavery","Enslaved people in the Americas were treated as property, forced to work long hours, often on sugar, cotton, coffee and tobacco plantations, and could be sold away from their families. They were punished brutally. Yet they built families and communities and kept alive African music, beliefs, foods and languages, which shaped the cultures of the Americas."],["Resistance","Enslaved people resisted in many ways, from working slowly and escaping to armed revolt. Some who escaped formed free communities, such as Palmares in Brazil and the Maroons of Jamaica. In Saint-Domingue, a rebellion beginning in 1791 and led by figures such as Toussaint Louverture created Haiti, the first independent nation founded by formerly enslaved people, in 1804."],["Abolitionists","Campaigners for abolition included formerly enslaved writers such as Olaudah Equiano, whose autobiography of 1789 described the Middle Passage, as well as Quakers and activists such as Thomas Clarkson and William Wilberforce in Britain and Frederick Douglass and Harriet Tubman in the United States. Boycotts of slave-grown sugar and petitions drew wide public support."],["The end of slavery","Britain and the United States banned the transatlantic trade in 1807, though it continued illegally for decades. Slavery was abolished in British colonies from 1834, in the French colonies in 1848, in the United States after the Civil War in 1865, in Cuba in 1886 and in Brazil in 1888. Slaveholders were often compensated; the enslaved were not."],["Lasting effects","The slave trade left deep scars. It contributed to the wealth of European and American economies, while it disrupted African societies. Racism used to justify slavery outlasted it, leading to segregation and discrimination. Many countries and institutions are now examining this history, and some have discussed apologies and reparations."],["Remembering","25 March is the United Nations International Day of Remembrance of the Victims of Slavery and the Transatlantic Slave Trade, and 23 August is the International Day for the Remembrance of the Slave Trade and its Abolition, marking the revolt in Saint-Domingue. Museums and memorials, such as those at Gorée Island in Senegal, Cape Coast Castle in Ghana and Liverpool in England, help people learn this history."]],
sp:["Olaudah Equiano","Olaudah Equiano wrote that he was born in what is now southeastern Nigeria, kidnapped as a child and shipped across the Atlantic. After years of enslavement, he bought his freedom in 1766 and settled in London. His autobiography, The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano, published in 1789, became a bestseller and helped turn British public opinion against the slave trade."],
g:[["Abolition","The ending of slavery or the slave trade by law"],["Plantation","A large farm growing crops such as sugar or cotton, often worked by enslaved people in the past"],["Middle Passage","The voyage of enslaved Africans across the Atlantic"],["Maroon","A person who escaped slavery and lived in a free community"]],
f:["About 12.5 million Africans were forced onto slave ships.","Brazil received more enslaved Africans than any other country.","Haiti was the first nation founded by formerly enslaved people."],
fun:"Brazil was the last country in the Americas to end slavery, in 1888.",
tl:"Ang kalakalan ng alipin sa Atlantiko ay sapilitang pagdadala ng mga 12.5 milyong Aprikano sa Amerika mula ika-16 hanggang ika-19 na siglo. Lumaban ang mga inalipin, at kalaunan ay ipinagbawal ang pang-aalipin."});

ADDART({id:"latinind",t:"Latin American Independence",c:"History",i:"🗽",w:"Spanish American wars of independence",r:["bolivar","toussaint","usrev","frrev","spaincountry"],
p:[["A wave of revolutions","Between 1808 and 1826, most of Spain’s and Portugal’s colonies in the Americas won their independence."],["The Liberators","Leaders such as Simón Bolívar and José de San Martín led armies across mountains and plains to free a continent."],["New nations","Mexico, Colombia, Venezuela, Peru, Argentina, Chile, Brazil and many more countries were born."]],
l:"The Latin American wars of independence were a series of revolutions in the early 19th century that ended about 300 years of Spanish and Portuguese colonial rule over most of Central and South America. Inspired by the American and French revolutions and the Haitian Revolution, and triggered when Napoleon invaded Spain in 1808, leaders such as Simón Bolívar, José de San Martín, Bernardo O’Higgins, Miguel Hidalgo and José María Morelos led movements that created many of today’s Latin American nations.",
s:[["Colonial society","In Spanish America, power was held by officials born in Spain, called peninsulares. Creoles, people of Spanish descent born in the Americas, were often wealthy but excluded from top positions, and resented taxes and trade restrictions. Below them were people of mixed descent, Indigenous peoples and enslaved Africans, who had the fewest rights."],["The spark","In 1808 Napoleon invaded Spain and put his brother Joseph on the throne. In the colonies, local leaders formed juntas, ruling councils, claiming to govern in the name of the captured Spanish king. Many soon began to demand full independence."],["Mexico","On 16 September 1810, the priest Miguel Hidalgo rang the church bell in the town of Dolores and called on the people to rise up, an event known as the Grito de Dolores. Hidalgo was captured and executed, as was José María Morelos after him, but the struggle continued, and Mexico won independence in 1821. 16 September is Mexico’s Independence Day."],["Bolívar in the north","Simón Bolívar, from Caracas, fought for more than a decade to free Venezuela and New Granada. In 1819 he led his army across the freezing Andes and won the Battle of Boyacá, freeing what is now Colombia. He created a large republic, Gran Colombia, and helped free Ecuador, Peru and Bolivia, which is named after him."]],
k:[["Period","1808–1826"],["Main leaders","Simón Bolívar, José de San Martín, Bernardo O’Higgins, Miguel Hidalgo, José María Morelos, Antonio José de Sucre"],["Colonial powers","Spain and Portugal"],["Key battles","Boyacá (1819), Maipú (1818), Ayacucho (1824)"],["Earlier example","Haiti, independent in 1804"]],
d:[["1804","Haiti becomes independent from France"],["1808","Napoleon invades Spain"],["1810","Grito de Dolores in Mexico; juntas form in Caracas and Buenos Aires"],["1816","Argentina declares independence"],["1822","Brazil declares independence from Portugal"],["1824","Battle of Ayacucho ends Spanish rule in South America"]],
s2:[["San Martín in the south","José de San Martín, from what is now Argentina, led an army across the Andes into Chile in 1817, one of the great military feats in history. With the Chilean leader Bernardo O’Higgins, he won the Battle of Chacabuco and later Maipú, securing Chile’s independence. He then sailed to Peru and declared its independence in Lima in 1821."],["The meeting at Guayaquil","In 1822 Bolívar and San Martín met privately at Guayaquil, in Ecuador. No one knows exactly what they said, but afterward San Martín withdrew from public life and left for Europe, leaving Bolívar to finish the fight. Bolívar’s general Antonio José de Sucre won the decisive Battle of Ayacucho in Peru in 1824."],["Brazil’s different path","When Napoleon invaded Portugal in 1807, the Portuguese royal family fled to Brazil, making Rio de Janeiro the capital of their empire. In 1822, Prince Pedro, the king’s son, declared Brazil independent and became its emperor. Brazil remained a monarchy until 1889, when it became a republic."],["Central America and the Caribbean","Central America broke from Spain in 1821, briefly joined Mexico, and then formed a federation that split into five countries by 1841. Cuba and Puerto Rico remained Spanish until 1898. The Dominican Republic won independence from Haiti in 1844 and, after a brief return of Spanish rule, restored it in 1865."],["After independence","Independence did not bring equality for everyone. Wealthy creoles often replaced Spanish officials, while many Indigenous people, people of African descent and poor farmers saw little change. Bolívar’s dream of a united Latin America failed as Gran Colombia split apart in 1830, and many new nations faced civil wars and military rulers called caudillos."],["Legacy","The heroes of independence are celebrated across Latin America in statues, street names, currencies and national holidays. Countries such as Venezuela and Bolivia bear Bolívar’s name. The period created the map of most of Latin America today and ideas of liberty and nationhood that continue to shape the region."]],
sp:["The Philippine connection","The independence of Spain’s American colonies changed the Philippines too. The galleon trade between Manila and Acapulco ended in 1815, partly because of the Mexican revolution, and the Philippines came under direct rule from Madrid instead of through Mexico. Some Spanish officers born in the Americas who served in Manila were suspected of supporting independence, and the ideas of liberty from the Americas later influenced Filipino reformers."],
g:[["Creole","A person of European descent born in the colonies"],["Junta","A council that takes power to govern"],["Caudillo","A military strongman leader in Latin America"],["Peninsular","A person born in Spain living in the colonies"]],
f:["Bolivia is named after Simón Bolívar.","San Martín led an army across the Andes in 1817.","Brazil became independent as an empire, not a republic."],
fun:"Bolivia and Venezuela both carry Simón Bolívar’s name in their official names or titles.",
tl:"Mula 1808 hanggang 1826, nakamit ng karamihan sa mga kolonya ng Espanya at Portugal sa Amerika ang kalayaan sa pamumuno nina Simón Bolívar, José de San Martín at iba pa. Isinilang ang mga bansang gaya ng Mexico, Colombia, Peru at Brazil."});

ADDART({id:"meiji",t:"Meiji Japan",c:"History",i:"🚂",w:"Meiji era",r:["japan","japancountry","indrev","ww2","koreanwar"],
p:[["Opening up","In 1853 American warships forced Japan to end more than two centuries of near isolation."],["The Meiji Restoration","In 1868 power was restored to the emperor, and Japan began transforming itself at astonishing speed."],["A modern power","Within about 40 years, Japan built railways, factories, a modern army and a constitution, and defeated Russia in war."]],
l:"The Meiji era, from 1868 to 1912, was the period when Japan rapidly changed from a feudal society ruled by samurai and shoguns into a modern industrial nation. It began with the Meiji Restoration, which returned power to Emperor Meiji. Japanese leaders studied Western countries and adopted their technology, education and institutions while keeping Japanese traditions. By 1912 Japan was a major world power, but its rise also led to wars and colonial expansion in Asia.",
s:[["The closed country","From the 1630s, the Tokugawa shoguns, military rulers, limited contact with the outside world, a policy later called sakoku. Only Dutch and Chinese traders were allowed, at Nagasaki. In 1853, the American commodore Matthew Perry sailed into Edo Bay with steam-powered warships and demanded that Japan open its ports. Japan signed treaties that many Japanese saw as unequal."],["The Restoration","Anger at the shogunate’s weakness led samurai from the domains of Satsuma and Chōshū to overthrow it. In 1868 the young Emperor Meiji was declared ruler, and the capital was moved from Kyoto to Edo, renamed Tokyo, eastern capital. The new government was run by a small group of reformers in the emperor’s name."],["Ending feudalism","The government abolished the feudal domains and replaced them with prefectures. The samurai class lost its privileges, including the right to carry swords. In 1877, discontented samurai led by Saigō Takamori rose in the Satsuma Rebellion, but they were defeated by the new conscript army of ordinary citizens."],["Learning from the world","In 1871 the Iwakura Mission, a group of leaders, set out on a nearly two-year journey to the United States and Europe to study their governments, industries and schools. Japan hired foreign experts, sent students abroad and adopted ideas from many countries: a navy modeled on Britain’s, an army on Germany’s and laws on French and German systems."]],
k:[["Period","1868–1912"],["Emperor","Meiji (Mutsuhito)"],["New capital","Tokyo (formerly Edo)"],["Constitution","1889"],["Motto","Rich country, strong army (fukoku kyōhei)"]],
d:[["1853","Commodore Perry arrives in Edo Bay"],["1868","Meiji Restoration"],["1872","First railway opens between Tokyo and Yokohama"],["1889","Meiji Constitution proclaimed"],["1895","Japan defeats China in the First Sino-Japanese War"],["1905","Japan defeats Russia in the Russo-Japanese War"]],
s2:[["Industry and railways","Japan’s first railway, between Tokyo and Yokohama, opened in 1872, built with British help. The government built factories for silk, cotton, shipbuilding and steel, later selling many to private companies that grew into large business groups called zaibatsu, such as Mitsubishi and Mitsui. Silk became Japan’s main export."],["Schools for all","In 1872 Japan introduced compulsory education for both boys and girls. By the early 1900s, nearly all children attended primary school, giving Japan one of the highest literacy rates in the world. Universities trained engineers, doctors and officials."],["The Meiji Constitution","In 1889 Japan adopted a constitution, one of the first in Asia, modeled partly on Prussia’s. It created a parliament, the Diet, with an elected lower house, though only a small share of men could vote and the emperor held supreme power. Political parties grew, and voting rights later expanded."],["Western ways","Many Japanese adopted Western clothing, haircuts, foods and buildings, and the Western calendar replaced the old lunar one in 1873. Brick buildings, gas lamps and newspapers appeared in Tokyo. At the same time, traditional arts and Shinto were promoted as part of a national identity centered on the emperor."],["Wars and empire","Japan’s new army and navy defeated China in 1895, gaining Taiwan, and then Russia in 1905, the first time an Asian nation had defeated a European power in modern war. The victory inspired people across Asia but also encouraged Japanese expansion. Japan annexed Korea in 1910, ruling it harshly until 1945."],["Legacy","The Meiji era made Japan the first non-Western country to industrialize. Its reforms laid the foundations of modern Japan, but the growing power of the military and the drive for empire led, in later decades, to aggression in China, Southeast Asia and the Pacific during World War II."]],
sp:["The black ships","The Japanese called Perry’s ships kurofune, black ships, because of their dark hulls and the black smoke from their steam engines. They had never seen steamships before. Woodblock prints of the time show the ships and the Americans as strange and frightening. The arrival of the black ships is remembered as the moment Japan’s modern history began."],
g:[["Shogun","A military ruler of Japan"],["Restoration","The return of power to a former ruler"],["Feudalism","A system in which lords ruled land and people in return for loyalty"],["Zaibatsu","Large family-controlled business groups in Japan"]],
f:["Tokyo was called Edo until 1868.","Japan’s first railway opened in 1872.","Japan was the first Asian country to defeat a European power in modern war."],
fun:"Tokyo means eastern capital; the city was called Edo until 1868.",
tl:"Ang panahong Meiji (1868–1912) ay panahon ng mabilis na pagbabago ng Japan mula pyudal na lipunan tungo sa modernong bansang industriyal. Nagtayo ito ng mga riles, pabrika at paaralan, at naging makapangyarihang bansa."});

;
