ADDART({id:"russrev",t:"The Russian Revolution",c:"History",i:"🚩",w:"Russian Revolution",r:["russiacountry","wwi","coldwar","ussrfall","frrev"],
p:[["The end of the tsars","In 1917 revolutions ended more than 300 years of rule by the Romanov tsars in Russia."],["The Bolsheviks","In October 1917, the Bolsheviks led by Vladimir Lenin seized power and created the world’s first communist state."],["A world-changing event","The revolution led to the Soviet Union, which shaped world politics for most of the 20th century."]],
l:"The Russian Revolution was a pair of revolutions in 1917 that ended the rule of the tsars and brought the Bolsheviks to power. In February 1917, protests in Petrograd forced Tsar Nicholas II to give up the throne. In October, the Bolsheviks, a communist party led by Vladimir Lenin, overthrew the new Provisional Government. After a brutal civil war, they founded the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics, or Soviet Union, in 1922, which lasted until 1991.",
s:[["Russia under the tsars","In the early 1900s, Russia was a vast empire ruled by Tsar Nicholas II, who held almost unlimited power. Most people were poor peasants, and serfdom had been abolished only in 1861. Factory workers in growing cities faced long hours and harsh conditions. In 1905, after troops fired on peaceful marchers on Bloody Sunday, a revolution forced the tsar to allow a parliament, the Duma, but he kept most of his power."],["War and hunger","Russia entered World War I in 1914. Its armies suffered millions of casualties, and the war caused shortages of food and fuel. Nicholas took personal command of the army, leaving the government to his unpopular wife, Alexandra, who was influenced by the mystic Grigori Rasputin. Discontent grew everywhere."],["The February Revolution","In February 1917 (March by the Western calendar), women workers in Petrograd went on strike demanding bread, and huge crowds joined them. Soldiers refused to fire on the protesters and joined them. On 15 March Nicholas II abdicated. A Provisional Government took over, while councils of workers and soldiers, called soviets, also claimed authority."],["The October Revolution","The Provisional Government kept fighting the war, which made it unpopular. Lenin returned from exile and promised peace, land and bread. On 25 October 1917 (7 November by the Western calendar), Bolshevik forces led by Leon Trotsky seized key points in Petrograd and stormed the Winter Palace. Lenin announced a new government of the soviets."]],
k:[["Year","1917"],["Main leaders","Vladimir Lenin, Leon Trotsky"],["Last tsar","Nicholas II"],["Result","Communist rule; Soviet Union founded in 1922"],["Civil war","1918–1922 (main fighting to 1920)"]],
d:[["1905","Bloody Sunday and the 1905 Revolution"],["1914","Russia enters World War I"],["March 1917","February Revolution; Nicholas II abdicates"],["November 1917","October Revolution; the Bolsheviks seize power"],["1918","Treaty of Brest-Litovsk; the royal family is killed"],["1922","The Soviet Union is founded"]],
s2:[["Peace at a price","In March 1918, the Bolsheviks signed the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk with Germany, leaving the war but giving up huge territories, including Ukraine, Poland, Finland and the Baltic lands. Many Russians saw it as a humiliation. The Bolsheviks also moved the capital from Petrograd to Moscow."],["Civil war","From 1918 to about 1920, the Red Army of the Bolsheviks fought the Whites, a mix of monarchists, liberals and other opponents, helped by foreign troops from Britain, France, the United States and Japan. Millions died in fighting, terror on both sides, famine and disease. In July 1918, the Bolsheviks shot Nicholas II and his family."],["A new kind of state","The Bolsheviks, renamed the Communist Party, took over land, banks and factories, and banned other parties. A secret police force, the Cheka, arrested and executed opponents. In 1922 Russia joined with Ukraine, Belarus and the Transcaucasian republics to form the Soviet Union."],["Lenin and Stalin","Lenin died in 1924, and his body was preserved and displayed in a mausoleum in Red Square, where it remains. Joseph Stalin defeated his rivals, including Trotsky, and ruled as a dictator. He forced peasants onto collective farms, causing famines, including in Ukraine, and sent millions to labor camps called the Gulag, while rapidly industrializing the country."],["Effects on the world","The revolution inspired communist movements and parties around the world, and worried governments in many countries. After World War II, the Soviet Union became a superpower, and its rivalry with the United States, the Cold War, shaped world politics until the Soviet Union broke apart in 1991."],["Debating the revolution","Historians still debate the revolution. Some see it as a popular uprising against injustice that was later betrayed, others as a coup by a small, determined party that led to dictatorship. Its promises of equality, education and industrial progress, and its human costs, remain subjects of discussion."]],
sp:["Two calendars","Russia still used the old Julian calendar in 1917, which was 13 days behind the Gregorian calendar used in most of the world. So the February Revolution happened in March by the Western calendar, and the October Revolution in November. The Bolsheviks switched Russia to the Gregorian calendar in February 1918, and from then on the anniversary of the October Revolution was celebrated on 7 November."],
g:[["Tsar","The title of the emperor of Russia"],["Bolshevik","A member of Lenin’s communist party"],["Soviet","A council of workers and soldiers; later, the name for the Soviet Union and its people"],["Abdicate","To give up a throne"]],
f:["The October Revolution took place in November by the Western calendar.","Lenin’s body is still displayed in Moscow.","The Soviet Union lasted from 1922 to 1991."],
fun:"The October Revolution happened in November, because Russia used an older calendar.",
tl:"Ang Rebolusyong Ruso noong 1917 ang nagwakas sa pamumuno ng mga tsar at nagdala sa kapangyarihan sa mga Bolshevik ni Vladimir Lenin. Humantong ito sa pagtatatag ng Unyong Sobyet noong 1922."});

ADDART({id:"depression",t:"The Great Depression",c:"History",i:"📉",w:"Great Depression",r:["wwi","ww2","usacountry","money","trade"],
p:[["The worst economic crisis","The Great Depression of the 1930s was the deepest and longest economic downturn of the modern industrial world."],["The crash","It began in the United States after the stock market crashed in October 1929."],["Worldwide hardship","Banks failed, factories closed, and tens of millions of people lost their jobs across the world."]],
l:"The Great Depression was a severe worldwide economic depression that began in 1929 and lasted through much of the 1930s. Starting with the Wall Street Crash in the United States, it spread around the world as trade collapsed, banks failed and prices fell. At its worst, about a quarter of American workers were unemployed. The Depression caused great suffering, changed how governments manage economies and helped bring extremist leaders to power, contributing to World War II.",
s:[["The Roaring Twenties","In the 1920s, the American economy boomed. People bought cars, radios and household appliances, often on credit, and many borrowed money to buy shares, hoping prices would keep rising. But farmers were struggling, wealth was unequally shared, and share prices rose far above what companies were really worth."],["The Wall Street Crash","In October 1929, share prices on the New York Stock Exchange began to fall, and panic selling followed. On Black Tuesday, 29 October, about 16 million shares were sold. Over the next three years, shares lost almost 90 percent of their value. Many investors were ruined."],["Spreading crisis","As people lost savings, they spent less, and businesses cut production and jobs. Thousands of banks failed, wiping out depositors’ money. American banks called back loans from Europe, especially Germany, spreading the crisis. Countries raised tariffs, taxes on imports, which made world trade shrink by about two-thirds."],["Life in the Depression","Millions of people lost jobs, homes and farms. In the United States, families lived in shacks in camps called Hoovervilles, named after President Herbert Hoover, and lined up at soup kitchens and bread lines. Young people rode freight trains looking for work. In other countries, from Germany to Australia, unemployment and poverty spread."]],
k:[["Began","1929"],["Lasted","Through most of the 1930s"],["US unemployment","About 25 percent at its worst (1933)"],["World trade","Fell by about two-thirds, 1929–1934"],["Key US response","The New Deal"]],
d:[["October 1929","Wall Street Crash"],["1930","US raises tariffs with the Smoot-Hawley Act"],["1931","Major Austrian bank collapses; crisis spreads in Europe"],["1933","Franklin D. Roosevelt launches the New Deal"],["1933","Adolf Hitler becomes chancellor of Germany"],["1936","John Maynard Keynes publishes his General Theory"]],
s2:[["The Dust Bowl","In the 1930s, drought and poor farming methods turned large parts of the American Great Plains into a Dust Bowl. Huge dust storms, called black blizzards, buried farms and darkened skies. Hundreds of thousands of families, many called Okies, left for California in search of work, a journey described in John Steinbeck’s novel The Grapes of Wrath."],["The New Deal","President Franklin D. Roosevelt, elected in 1932, launched the New Deal, a series of programs to provide relief, recovery and reform. Government projects employed millions of people building roads, dams, schools and parks. New laws protected bank deposits, regulated the stock market and created Social Security, pensions for older people."],["Around the world","The Depression hit countries everywhere. Prices for exports such as sugar, rubber, coffee and wheat collapsed, hurting farmers in Latin America, Africa and Asia, including sugar workers in the Philippines and rubber workers in Malaya. Canada, Australia and many European countries suffered high unemployment."],["Rise of extremism","In Germany, where unemployment reached about 6 million, many people lost faith in democracy. Adolf Hitler’s Nazi Party promised jobs and national revival and gained power in 1933. In Japan and elsewhere, military and nationalist groups also grew stronger. Historians see the Depression as one of the causes of World War II."],["New economic ideas","The British economist John Maynard Keynes argued that during a depression, governments should spend more money, even if they had to borrow, to create jobs and demand. His ideas shaped economic policy for decades and are still debated, especially during later crises such as the financial crisis of 2008 and the COVID-19 pandemic."],["The end","Recovery came slowly through the 1930s, as countries left the gold standard, governments spent more and trade revived. In many countries, full recovery came only with the huge spending and production of World War II. Afterward, countries created institutions such as the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank to prevent such a crisis happening again."]],
sp:["Migrant Mother","One of the most famous photographs of the Depression, Migrant Mother, was taken by Dorothea Lange in California in 1936. It shows Florence Owens Thompson, a 32-year-old mother of seven, with a worried face, her children leaning on her shoulders. Lange worked for a government agency recording the lives of poor farmers, and her photograph became a symbol of the hardships of the era."],
g:[["Depression","A long and severe downturn in the economy"],["Stock market","A place where shares in companies are bought and sold"],["Tariff","A tax on imported goods"],["Unemployment","Not having a paid job when wanting one"]],
f:["About a quarter of American workers were unemployed in 1933.","World trade fell by about two-thirds.","The Dust Bowl drove hundreds of thousands of families from their farms."],
fun:"Camps of homeless people in the United States were called Hoovervilles after the president at the time.",
tl:"Ang Great Depression ay malubhang krisis pang-ekonomiya sa buong mundo noong dekada 1930. Nagsimula ito sa pagbagsak ng stock market sa Estados Unidos noong 1929, at milyon-milyong tao ang nawalan ng trabaho."});

;
