ADDART({id:"partition",t:"The Partition of India",c:"History",i:"🧭",w:"Partition of India",r:["gandhi","indiacountry","pakistancountry","decol","indus"],
p:[["Two new nations","In August 1947, British India was divided into two independent countries, India and Pakistan."],["A mass migration","About 15 million people crossed the new borders, one of the largest migrations in history."],["Terrible violence","Violence between communities killed hundreds of thousands, perhaps up to two million people."]],
l:"The Partition of India was the division of British India in August 1947 into two independent dominions, India and Pakistan, at the end of nearly two centuries of British rule. Pakistan was created as a homeland for Muslims, in two parts, West Pakistan and East Pakistan, which later became Bangladesh. The new borders split the provinces of Punjab and Bengal. Partition brought independence but also mass migration and terrible violence, and its effects are still felt across South Asia.",
s:[["The road to independence","For decades, Indians campaigned for independence through the Indian National Congress, led by figures such as Mahatma Gandhi and Jawaharlal Nehru. The All-India Muslim League, led by Muhammad Ali Jinnah, increasingly argued that Muslims, a minority in India as a whole, needed their own state. In 1940 it called for a separate homeland."],["Britain leaves","After World War II, Britain was exhausted and decided to leave India. Talks among British officials, Congress and the Muslim League failed to agree on a united India. In 1946, riots in Calcutta and elsewhere killed thousands. The last viceroy, Lord Mountbatten, arrived in 1947 and brought forward the date of independence to August 1947."],["Drawing the lines","A British lawyer, Cyril Radcliffe, who had never been to India, was given about five weeks to draw the borders through Punjab and Bengal. The boundaries were announced only on 17 August, two days after independence, leaving millions unsure which country they would live in."],["Independence","Pakistan became independent on 14 August 1947, with Jinnah as governor-general, and India on 15 August, with Nehru as prime minister. At midnight, Nehru spoke of India’s tryst with destiny. But the celebrations were overshadowed by the violence and migration already under way."]],
k:[["Date","14–15 August 1947"],["New countries","India and Pakistan (East Pakistan became Bangladesh in 1971)"],["People displaced","About 15 million"],["Deaths","Estimates range from about 200,000 to 2 million"],["Divided provinces","Punjab and Bengal"]],
d:[["1885","Indian National Congress founded"],["1906","All-India Muslim League founded"],["1940","Lahore Resolution calls for a Muslim homeland"],["1947","Partition and independence"],["1948","Gandhi is assassinated; first India-Pakistan war over Kashmir"],["1971","East Pakistan becomes Bangladesh"]],
s2:[["The great migration","Hindus and Sikhs moved from Pakistan to India, and Muslims moved from India to Pakistan, on foot, in bullock carts and on overcrowded trains. Refugee camps sprang up in cities such as Delhi and Lahore. Many families lost homes, land and relatives, and some were separated forever."],["Violence","Gangs from all communities attacked people of other religions. Trains arrived carrying only the dead. Tens of thousands of women were abducted and assaulted. Estimates of the deaths range from about 200,000 to 2 million. Gandhi went on hunger strikes to stop the killing, and in 1948 he was assassinated by a Hindu extremist who opposed his efforts at reconciliation."],["Kashmir","The princely states, ruled by local princes, had to choose which country to join. The Hindu ruler of Kashmir, a Muslim-majority state, hesitated, and after an invasion by tribesmen from Pakistan he joined India. India and Pakistan fought their first war over Kashmir in 1947 and 1948, and the region remains divided and disputed."],["Bangladesh","East and West Pakistan were separated by about 1,600 kilometers of Indian territory, and had different languages and cultures. Bengali-speaking East Pakistanis felt neglected by the government in the west. In 1971, after a war in which India supported them, East Pakistan became the independent nation of Bangladesh."],["Memories","For decades, many survivors did not speak about Partition. Since the 2000s, projects such as the 1947 Partition Archive have recorded thousands of survivors’ stories. The Partition Museum in Amritsar opened in 2017. Novels and films, such as Khushwant Singh’s Train to Pakistan, explore its human cost."],["Shared heritage","Despite Partition, India, Pakistan and Bangladesh share much: languages such as Punjabi, Bengali, Hindi and Urdu, foods, music, films, poetry and a passion for cricket. Matches between India and Pakistan are among the most watched sporting events in the world."]],
sp:["Tryst with destiny","At midnight on 14–15 August 1947, Jawaharlal Nehru told India’s Constituent Assembly: Long years ago we made a tryst with destiny, and now the time comes when we shall redeem our pledge. At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India will awake to life and freedom. The speech is one of the most famous of the 20th century."],
g:[["Partition","The division of a country into separate parts"],["Dominion","A self-governing country within the British Commonwealth"],["Viceroy","A ruler governing a colony on behalf of a monarch"],["Refugee","A person forced to leave their home to escape danger"]],
f:["About 15 million people crossed the borders during Partition.","The borders were announced two days after independence.","Pakistan celebrates independence on 14 August and India on 15 August."],
fun:"The man who drew the India-Pakistan border had never been to India before.",
tl:"Noong Agosto 1947, hinati ang British India sa dalawang bansa, ang India at Pakistan. Mga 15 milyong tao ang lumipat ng tirahan, at maraming namatay sa karahasan."});

ADDART({id:"apartheid",t:"The End of Apartheid",c:"History",i:"✊",w:"Apartheid",r:["mandela","tutu","southafricacountry","hrights","decol"],
p:[["Separation by law","Apartheid, meaning apartness in Afrikaans, was South Africa’s system of racial segregation from 1948 to the early 1990s."],["Years of struggle","South Africans resisted through protests, strikes, armed struggle and international campaigns."],["A peaceful transition","In 1994 South Africa held its first democratic election, and Nelson Mandela became president."]],
l:"Apartheid was a system of laws in South Africa that separated people by race and kept power in the hands of the white minority. Introduced by the National Party government after 1948, it denied Black South Africans the vote, forced them into separate areas and restricted where they could live, work and travel. After decades of resistance and growing international pressure, apartheid was dismantled in the early 1990s, leading to the country’s first democratic election in 1994.",
s:[["Apartheid laws","Every South African was classified by race as white, Black, Coloured or Indian. Laws forbade marriage between races, created separate schools, hospitals, beaches and buses, and forced millions of people from their homes. Black people had to carry passbooks and could be arrested for being in white areas without permission."],["The homelands","The government created ten homelands, or Bantustans, for Black South Africans, based on ethnic groups, and declared some of them independent. Black people lost their South African citizenship and were treated as foreigners in most of the country. No other nation recognized the homelands."],["Resistance","The African National Congress, the ANC, and other groups led campaigns of defiance against unjust laws. In 1960 police killed 69 unarmed protesters at Sharpeville, and the government banned the ANC. Nelson Mandela and others turned to sabotage and were sentenced to life imprisonment in 1964. In 1976, police shot schoolchildren protesting in Soweto, sparking protests across the country."],["International pressure","Many countries imposed sanctions on South Africa, cutting trade and investment. It was banned from the Olympic Games and other international sports. People around the world boycotted South African goods and campaigned for Mandela’s release. The United Nations called apartheid a crime against humanity."]],
k:[["Period","1948 to early 1990s"],["Meaning","Apartness, in Afrikaans"],["Mandela released","11 February 1990"],["First democratic election","27 April 1994"],["Freedom Day","27 April, a national holiday"]],
d:[["1948","National Party wins power and introduces apartheid"],["1960","Sharpeville massacre; the ANC is banned"],["1964","Mandela sentenced to life imprisonment"],["1976","Soweto uprising"],["1990","Mandela is released; the ANC is unbanned"],["1994","First democratic election; Mandela becomes president"]],
s2:[["De Klerk and Mandela","In 1989 F. W. de Klerk became president and began reforms. In February 1990 he lifted the ban on the ANC and other groups and released Nelson Mandela after 27 years in prison. Mandela and de Klerk then led negotiations for a new constitution, and in 1993 they shared the Nobel Peace Prize."],["Dangerous years","The years of negotiation were violent. Thousands died in fighting between supporters of different groups, and in 1993 the popular ANC leader Chris Hani was assassinated. Mandela appealed for calm on television, and many credit him with preventing wider violence. Talks continued, leading to an interim constitution."],["The 1994 election","On 27 April 1994, South Africans of all races voted together for the first time. Millions waited for hours in long lines, many voting for the first time in their lives. The ANC won about 62 percent of the vote, and Nelson Mandela was inaugurated as president on 10 May."],["A new constitution","South Africa’s 1996 constitution is one of the most progressive in the world, with a Bill of Rights protecting equality and banning discrimination on grounds including race, gender, religion and sexual orientation. Its Constitutional Court protects these rights."],["Truth and Reconciliation","From 1996, the Truth and Reconciliation Commission, chaired by Archbishop Desmond Tutu, heard testimony from victims and perpetrators of apartheid-era violence. Those who fully confessed political crimes could apply for amnesty. The process helped the country face its past, though some felt justice was not done."],["Challenges today","Apartheid ended in law, but its effects remain. South Africa is one of the most unequal countries in the world, and many Black South Africans still live in poverty in former townships. Unemployment, crime and corruption are serious problems. Yet the country has held regular free elections, and in 2024 the ANC lost its majority and formed a coalition government."]],
sp:["Freedom Day and the rainbow","27 April is celebrated in South Africa as Freedom Day, marking the 1994 election. The new national flag, adopted that year, combines colors from many of the country’s political traditions, with a Y shape representing different groups coming together. The national anthem combines verses in five languages, isiXhosa, isiZulu, Sesotho, Afrikaans and English."],
g:[["Apartheid","South Africa’s system of racial segregation"],["Sanctions","Penalties, such as trade bans, imposed on a country"],["Township","An area set aside for Black South Africans under apartheid"],["Homeland","An area created by the apartheid government for a Black ethnic group"]],
f:["Apartheid means apartness in Afrikaans.","Nelson Mandela spent 27 years in prison.","South Africa’s national anthem uses five languages."],
fun:"South Africa’s national anthem is sung in five languages.",
tl:"Ang apartheid ay sistema ng paghihiwalay ng lahi sa South Africa mula 1948. Matapos ang mahabang pakikibaka at presyon ng mundo, natapos ito noong unang bahagi ng dekada 1990, at naging pangulo si Nelson Mandela noong 1994."});

ADDART({id:"eu",t:"The European Union",c:"History",i:"⭐",w:"European Union",r:["ww2","coldwar","ussrfall","germanycountry","francecountry","asean"],
p:[["Peace through unity","The European Union grew from efforts after World War II to make war between European countries impossible."],["27 countries","It now unites 27 countries with about 450 million people."],["A single market","Goods, services, money and people can move freely between its members, and 21 of them share the euro."]],
l:"The European Union, or EU, is a political and economic union of 27 European countries. It began in the 1950s, when six countries, still recovering from World War II, joined their coal and steel industries so that war between them would be unthinkable. It has grown into a single market with shared laws, common policies on trade, farming and the environment, and a currency, the euro, used by most members. Its main institutions are in Brussels, Luxembourg and Strasbourg.",
s:[["Origins","After World War II, leaders such as Robert Schuman and Jean Monnet of France, Konrad Adenauer of Germany and Alcide De Gasperi of Italy sought to bind their countries together. On 9 May 1950, Schuman proposed pooling French and German coal and steel, the materials of war. In 1951, six countries, Belgium, France, West Germany, Italy, Luxembourg and the Netherlands, formed the European Coal and Steel Community."],["The common market","In 1957 the same six signed the Treaty of Rome, creating the European Economic Community, a common market without tariffs between members. Over the following decades, others joined, including the United Kingdom, Ireland and Denmark in 1973, Greece in 1981, and Spain and Portugal in 1986."],["Becoming the EU","The Maastricht Treaty of 1992 created the European Union, adding cooperation on foreign policy, justice and a future common currency, and introduced EU citizenship. The euro was launched in 1999, and euro coins and banknotes came into use in 2002. The Schengen Agreement allowed travel without passport checks between many countries."],["Enlargement","After the Cold War ended, many former communist countries in Central and Eastern Europe wanted to join. In 2004, ten countries joined at once, including Poland, Hungary, Czechia and the Baltic states, followed by Romania and Bulgaria in 2007 and Croatia in 2013. Several more countries, including Ukraine, are candidates to join."]],
k:[["Members","27 countries"],["Population","About 450 million"],["Founded","1993 as the EU (from communities created in the 1950s)"],["Currency","The euro, used by 21 members (since Bulgaria joined in 2026)"],["Main seats","Brussels, Luxembourg, Strasbourg"]],
d:[["1950","Schuman Declaration"],["1957","Treaty of Rome"],["1992","Maastricht Treaty signed"],["2002","Euro coins and notes enter circulation"],["2004","Ten countries join"],["2020","The United Kingdom leaves the EU"]],
s2:[["How it works","The European Commission proposes laws and makes sure they are applied. The European Parliament, elected by citizens every five years, and the Council of the EU, made up of government ministers from each country, pass the laws together. The European Council of national leaders sets the overall direction, and the Court of Justice ensures EU law is applied equally."],["Freedom to move","EU citizens can live, work, study and retire in any member country. The Erasmus program has helped more than 15 million students and others study or train abroad since 1987. Within the Schengen Area, which includes most EU countries and a few others, people can cross borders without passport checks."],["The euro","The euro is used by 21 EU countries, a group called the eurozone; Bulgaria became the 21st on 1 January 2026. It is the second most widely held reserve currency in the world after the US dollar. The European Central Bank, in Frankfurt, sets interest rates for the eurozone."],["Brexit","In a 2016 referendum, the United Kingdom voted by 52 to 48 percent to leave the EU. After years of negotiations, it left on 31 January 2020, the first member to do so. Brexit changed trade, travel and work between the UK and the EU, and its effects are still debated."],["Nobel Peace Prize","In 2012 the EU was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for over six decades of contributing to peace, reconciliation, democracy and human rights in Europe. The prize recognized its role in turning former enemies, especially France and Germany, into partners."],["Debates","The EU has many supporters and critics. Supporters praise its role in peace, trade, travel, consumer protection and the environment. Critics argue that it takes too much power from national governments or is too distant from citizens. Debates continue over migration, the euro, enlargement and how closely members should integrate."]],
sp:["United in diversity","The EU’s motto is United in diversity. It has 24 official languages, and its laws are translated into all of them, making it one of the world’s largest employers of translators and interpreters. Its flag shows a circle of 12 gold stars on a blue background; the number 12 is a symbol of completeness and does not represent the number of members. Europe Day is celebrated on 9 May, the anniversary of the Schuman Declaration."],
g:[["Single market","An area where goods, services, money and people move freely"],["Eurozone","The EU countries that use the euro"],["Referendum","A vote by all citizens on a single question"],["Treaty","A formal agreement between countries"]],
f:["The EU has 24 official languages.","The 12 stars on its flag do not stand for member countries.","The United Kingdom left the EU in 2020."],
fun:"The 12 stars on the EU flag stand for completeness, not for the number of member countries.",
tl:"Ang European Union ay samahan ng 27 bansang Europeo na nagsimula pagkatapos ng Ikalawang Digmaang Pandaigdig upang mapanatili ang kapayapaan. May iisang pamilihan ito, at 21 sa mga kasapi nito ang gumagamit ng euro."});

;
