// Space articles, part 2.1: Earth as a planet, the Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud, and space weather.
ADDART({id:"earthplanet",t:"Earth as a Planet",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌍",w:"Earth",r:["solar","moon","sun","tect","climate"],
p:[["Our home planet","Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only place in the universe known to have life."],["A water world","About 71 percent of Earth’s surface is covered by oceans of liquid water."],["Protected by layers","Its atmosphere and magnetic field shield life from harmful radiation and small space rocks."]],
l:"Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the largest of the four rocky planets. It formed about 4.54 billion years ago from a disk of gas and dust around the young Sun. Earth has one natural satellite, the Moon, and is the only known world with liquid water on its surface and with life. It orbits the Sun once every 365.25 days at an average distance of about 150 million kilometers, while spinning on its axis once every 24 hours.",
s:[["Shape and size","Earth is nearly a sphere, but its spin makes it bulge slightly at the equator, where it is about 12,756 kilometers across, about 43 kilometers wider than from pole to pole. Its highest point, the top of Mount Everest, is about 8.8 kilometers above sea level, and its deepest, the Challenger Deep in the Pacific, about 11 kilometers below."],["Inside Earth","Earth has layers like an onion. The thin rocky crust floats on a hot, slowly flowing mantle. At the center is a core made mostly of iron and nickel: a liquid outer core and a solid inner core about as hot as the surface of the Sun. Movements in the liquid outer core create Earth’s magnetic field."],["Day, night and seasons","Earth spins once every 24 hours, giving day and night. Its axis is tilted by about 23.4 degrees, so as it orbits the Sun, first one hemisphere and then the other leans toward the Sun, causing the seasons. When it is summer in the Northern Hemisphere, it is winter in the Southern Hemisphere."],["The atmosphere","Earth’s atmosphere is about 78 percent nitrogen and 21 percent oxygen, with small amounts of argon, carbon dioxide and water vapor. It keeps the planet warm through the greenhouse effect, gives us air to breathe, and burns up most meteors before they reach the ground. The ozone layer within it blocks much of the Sun’s harmful ultraviolet light."]],
k:[["Distance from the Sun","About 150 million km (1 astronomical unit)"],["Diameter","About 12,756 km at the equator"],["Year","365.25 days"],["Day","About 24 hours"],["Age","About 4.54 billion years"],["Moons","1"]],
d:[["c. 4.54 billion years ago","Earth forms"],["c. 4.5 billion years ago","The Moon forms after a giant collision"],["c. 3.7 billion years ago","Earliest evidence of life"],["c. 2.4 billion years ago","Oxygen begins to build up in the atmosphere"],["240 BCE","Eratosthenes estimates Earth’s circumference"],["1968","Apollo 8 astronauts photograph Earthrise"]],
s2:[["The Goldilocks zone","Earth orbits in the Sun’s habitable zone, the region where a planet is neither too hot nor too cold for liquid water. Venus, closer in, is scorching under a thick carbon dioxide atmosphere, while Mars, farther out, is cold and dry. Earth’s size also matters: its gravity is strong enough to hold on to a thick atmosphere."],["Measuring Earth","Around 240 BCE, the Greek scholar Eratosthenes, working in Alexandria, measured the angle of the Sun’s shadow in two Egyptian cities and calculated Earth’s circumference. His answer was remarkably close to the true value of about 40,000 kilometers. Today satellites measure Earth’s shape to within centimeters."],["The magnetic shield","Earth’s magnetic field reaches tens of thousands of kilometers into space, forming a bubble called the magnetosphere. It deflects most of the solar wind, the stream of charged particles from the Sun. Without it, the solar wind could slowly strip away the atmosphere, as scientists think happened on Mars."],["Earth and the Moon","The Moon is unusually large compared with its planet. It steadies Earth’s tilt, which keeps the seasons fairly stable, and its gravity raises the ocean tides. The Moon is slowly moving away from Earth, about 3.8 centimeters a year, and Earth’s day is very gradually getting longer."],["Seen from space","Photographs of Earth from space changed how people see their planet. Earthrise, taken by the Apollo 8 crew in 1968, and The Blue Marble, taken by Apollo 17 in 1972, showed Earth as a small, fragile world. In 1990 the Voyager 1 probe photographed Earth from about 6 billion kilometers away as a tiny dot, the Pale Blue Dot."],["A changing planet","Earth is always changing. Plates of its crust move a few centimeters a year, building mountains and opening oceans. Over millions of years its climate has swung between ice ages and warm periods. Today, human activities, such as burning fossil fuels, are warming the climate faster than at any time in human history."]],
sp:["The Pale Blue Dot","On 14 February 1990, Voyager 1 turned its camera back toward home and photographed Earth from about 6 billion kilometers away. Earth appears as a tiny speck, less than a single pixel, in a ray of scattered sunlight. The astronomer Carl Sagan, who suggested the photograph, wrote that everyone who has ever lived, lived out their life on that mote of dust suspended in a sunbeam."],
g:[["Axis","An imaginary line through a planet’s poles around which it spins"],["Habitable zone","The region around a star where liquid water could exist on a planet"],["Magnetosphere","The region around a planet controlled by its magnetic field"],["Astronomical unit","The average distance from Earth to the Sun, about 150 million km"]],
f:["Earth is the densest planet in the solar system.","About 71 percent of Earth’s surface is covered by water.","The Moon is moving away from Earth by about 3.8 centimeters a year."],
fun:"Earth is the densest planet in the solar system.",
tl:"Ang Daigdig ang ikatlong planeta mula sa Araw at ang tanging lugar na alam nating may buhay. Mga 71 porsiyento ng ibabaw nito ay tubig, at pinoprotektahan ito ng atmospera at magnetic field."});

ADDART({id:"kuiper",t:"The Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🧊",w:"Kuiper belt",r:["pluto","comet","solar","uranep"],
p:[["Beyond Neptune","The Kuiper Belt is a vast ring of icy bodies beyond the orbit of Neptune."],["Home of dwarf planets","Pluto, Eris, Haumea and Makemake are among the dwarf planets found in this distant region."],["A distant shell","Much farther out, a huge shell of icy objects called the Oort Cloud may surround the whole solar system."]],
l:"The Kuiper Belt is a doughnut-shaped region of icy objects that orbits the Sun beyond Neptune, roughly 30 to 50 times farther from the Sun than Earth. It contains dwarf planets such as Pluto and Makemake and probably hundreds of thousands of objects more than 100 kilometers across. Far beyond it, the Oort Cloud is thought to be a giant spherical shell of trillions of icy bodies, stretching perhaps a quarter of the way to the nearest star. These regions are leftovers from the formation of the solar system and the source of many comets.",
s:[["Leftovers of creation","When the planets formed about 4.6 billion years ago, not all the material clumped into large worlds. Far from the Sun, where it was cold, countless small bodies of ice and rock were left behind. Gravity from the giant planets pushed many of them outward, forming the Kuiper Belt and the Oort Cloud."],["Discovering the belt","In the mid-1900s astronomers including Kenneth Edgeworth and Gerard Kuiper suggested that there might be many small bodies beyond Neptune. Pluto, discovered in 1930, was the first known. In 1992 David Jewitt and Jane Luu found a second, and since then astronomers have found thousands of Kuiper Belt objects."],["Dwarf planets","Several Kuiper Belt objects are large enough to be round. In 2005 the discovery of Eris, almost the same size as Pluto, led the International Astronomical Union in 2006 to create a new category, dwarf planets. Pluto, Eris, Haumea and Makemake are classed as dwarf planets, and others may join them."],["The Oort Cloud","The Oort Cloud is named after the Dutch astronomer Jan Oort, who proposed it in 1950 to explain where long-period comets come from. It may extend from about 2,000 to 100,000 or more astronomical units from the Sun. No object in the main Oort Cloud has ever been seen directly, because they are so small and far away."]],
k:[["Kuiper Belt distance","About 30–50 AU from the Sun"],["Oort Cloud distance","Perhaps 2,000–100,000 AU"],["Dwarf planets","Pluto, Eris, Haumea, Makemake"],["First object found","Pluto (1930)"],["Visited by","New Horizons spacecraft"]],
d:[["1930","Clyde Tombaugh discovers Pluto"],["1950","Jan Oort proposes the Oort Cloud"],["1992","First Kuiper Belt object after Pluto and Charon is found"],["2005","Eris is discovered"],["2015","New Horizons flies past Pluto"],["2019","New Horizons flies past Arrokoth"]],
s2:[["New Horizons","NASA’s New Horizons spacecraft, launched in 2006, flew past Pluto in July 2015, revealing mountains of water ice, a heart-shaped plain of frozen nitrogen and a hazy atmosphere. On 1 January 2019, it flew past Arrokoth, a small object shaped like a snowman, the most distant object ever explored up close."],["Short and long comets","Comets that return every 200 years or less, such as Halley’s Comet, mostly come from the Kuiper Belt and a related region called the scattered disk. Comets with very long orbits, taking thousands or millions of years, are thought to fall in from the Oort Cloud when passing stars or the Milky Way’s gravity nudge them toward the Sun."],["Pluto’s neighbors","Pluto has five known moons; the largest, Charon, is half its size, so the two orbit a point in space between them. Haumea spins so fast that it is stretched into an egg shape and has rings. Makemake has a small dark moon, and Eris has a moon named Dysnomia."],["Is there a Planet Nine?","Some astronomers have noticed that several distant objects have oddly lined-up orbits. They suggest that an undiscovered planet, perhaps several times Earth’s mass, might be shaping them from far beyond the Kuiper Belt. Others think the pattern is due to chance or to how telescopes search the sky. No such planet has been found."],["Frozen time capsules","Because Kuiper Belt and Oort Cloud objects have stayed cold and far from the Sun since the solar system formed, they preserve its original ingredients. Studying them, and the comets that come from them, helps scientists understand how planets formed and where Earth’s water may have come from."],["How far is it?","The Oort Cloud is so distant that light from the Sun takes more than a year to reach its outer parts. The Voyager probes, the fastest objects leaving the solar system, will take around 300 years to reach its inner edge and perhaps 30,000 years to pass through it."]],
sp:["Arrokoth","Arrokoth, about 36 kilometers long, is made of two flattened lobes gently stuck together, like a snowman. It probably formed when two smaller bodies slowly drifted together soon after the solar system was born. Its name comes from the Powhatan and Algonquian languages and means sky. It is the most distant and most primitive object ever visited by a spacecraft."],
g:[["Dwarf planet","A round body orbiting the Sun that has not cleared its orbit of other objects"],["Comet","An icy body that grows a glowing tail when it nears the Sun"],["Astronomical unit (AU)","The average distance from Earth to the Sun"],["Long-period comet","A comet that takes more than 200 years to orbit the Sun"]],
f:["Pluto was the first Kuiper Belt object discovered.","No Oort Cloud object has ever been seen directly.","Arrokoth is the most distant object explored up close by a spacecraft."],
fun:"The Voyager probes will take about 300 years just to reach the inner edge of the Oort Cloud.",
tl:"Ang Kuiper Belt ay malawak na singsing ng mga nagyeyelong bagay lampas sa Neptune, kung saan naroon si Pluto. Mas malayo pa ang Oort Cloud, na pinagmumulan ng maraming kometa."});

ADDART({id:"spaceweather",t:"Space Weather and Auroras",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌌",w:"Space weather",r:["sun","earthplanet","magnet","sat","aurora"],
p:[["Storms from the Sun","Space weather is the changing conditions in space caused by the Sun, such as solar flares and blasts of charged particles."],["Lights in the sky","When charged particles reach Earth, they can make the sky glow with auroras near the poles."],["Effects on technology","Strong solar storms can disturb satellites, radio, GPS and power grids."]],
l:"Space weather describes the conditions in space around Earth, driven mainly by the Sun. The Sun constantly sends out a stream of charged particles called the solar wind, and sometimes erupts with solar flares and huge clouds of magnetized gas called coronal mass ejections. When these reach Earth, they can trigger geomagnetic storms, produce beautiful auroras and disrupt satellites, communications and electrical power systems. Space agencies monitor the Sun to forecast space weather, much as meteorologists forecast weather on Earth.",
s:[["The solar wind","The Sun’s outer atmosphere, the corona, is so hot, over a million degrees Celsius, that particles escape into space. This flow, the solar wind, streams past Earth at around 400 kilometers per second. Earth’s magnetic field deflects most of it, but some particles are channeled toward the poles."],["Flares and eruptions","Sunspots are cooler, darker regions where the Sun’s magnetic field is tangled. Here, sudden releases of energy cause solar flares, bursts of light and radiation. Coronal mass ejections hurl billions of metric tons of charged gas into space and can reach Earth in one to three days."],["Auroras","Auroras, called the northern lights or aurora borealis in the north and the southern lights or aurora australis in the south, happen when charged particles strike gases high in the atmosphere, making them glow. Oxygen gives green and red light, and nitrogen gives blue and purple. Auroras usually appear in rings around the magnetic poles."],["The solar cycle","The Sun’s activity rises and falls over a cycle of about 11 years. At solar maximum there are many sunspots, flares and eruptions; at solar minimum, few. The current cycle, Solar Cycle 25, reached its maximum around 2024 and 2025, bringing auroras seen much farther from the poles than usual."]],
k:[["Main source","The Sun"],["Solar wind speed","Around 400 km/s, faster in storms"],["Solar cycle","About 11 years"],["Aurora colors","Green and red (oxygen), blue and purple (nitrogen)"],["Strongest recorded storm","The Carrington Event, 1859"]],
d:[["1610s","Galileo and others observe sunspots with telescopes"],["1859","The Carrington Event, the largest known solar storm"],["1989","Solar storm causes a blackout in Quebec, Canada"],["1995","SOHO solar observatory is launched"],["2018","Parker Solar Probe is launched"],["May 2024","Strongest geomagnetic storm in two decades brings auroras to low latitudes"]],
s2:[["The Carrington Event","On 1 September 1859, the astronomer Richard Carrington saw a brilliant flare on the Sun. Within a day, a huge geomagnetic storm hit Earth. Auroras were seen as far south as the Caribbean, and telegraph systems sparked and failed. A storm that strong today could damage satellites and power grids worldwide."],["Blackouts and satellites","In March 1989 a geomagnetic storm caused electrical currents in the ground that overloaded the power grid in Quebec, Canada, leaving millions of people without electricity for hours. Space weather can also damage satellite electronics, increase drag on low satellites, disrupt GPS and radio signals and expose airline crews and astronauts to more radiation."],["Watching the Sun","Spacecraft such as SOHO, the Solar Dynamics Observatory and the Parker Solar Probe, which flies closer to the Sun than any spacecraft before, study the Sun constantly. Satellites between Earth and the Sun measure the solar wind before it arrives, giving warnings of up to about an hour."],["Forecasts","Space weather centers, such as the US Space Weather Prediction Center and similar services in Europe, Asia and elsewhere, issue alerts and forecasts. Power companies, airlines and satellite operators use them to protect equipment, for example by changing flight routes over the poles during storms."],["The May 2024 storm","In May 2024, a series of coronal mass ejections caused the strongest geomagnetic storm in more than 20 years. Auroras were photographed from places where they are almost never seen, including parts of Mexico, northern India and southern Europe. Phone cameras often captured the colors better than the human eye."],["Auroras on other worlds","Auroras also occur on other planets with atmospheres and magnetic fields. Jupiter has the most powerful auroras in the solar system, bigger than Earth itself, and Saturn, Uranus and Neptune have them too. Telescopes have even seen auroral glows on Mars, which has only patches of magnetism in its crust."]],
sp:["Where to see the northern lights","The best places to see auroras are under the auroral ovals, which circle the magnetic poles. In the north, these include northern Norway, Sweden, Finland, Iceland, Greenland, Alaska and northern Canada; in the south, Antarctica, and sometimes southern New Zealand and Tasmania. Auroras are best seen on clear, dark winter nights far from city lights."],
g:[["Solar wind","A stream of charged particles flowing from the Sun"],["Coronal mass ejection","A huge burst of magnetized gas from the Sun’s corona"],["Geomagnetic storm","A disturbance of Earth’s magnetic field caused by the Sun"],["Aurora","Natural colored light in the sky near the poles"]],
f:["The northern lights are called the aurora borealis.","The Sun’s activity follows a cycle of about 11 years.","Jupiter has the strongest auroras in the solar system."],
fun:"Jupiter’s auroras are bigger than the whole Earth.",
tl:"Ang space weather ay mga pagbabago sa kalawakan na dulot ng Araw, gaya ng solar flare. Kapag umabot sa Daigdig ang mga partikulong ito, maaaring lumitaw ang aurora malapit sa mga polo at maapektuhan ang mga satellite at kuryente."});

;
/* data/space2-2.js */
// Space articles, part 2.2: constellations, nebulae, supernovae and galaxies.
ADDART({id:"constellations",t:"Constellations and the Night Sky",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"✨",w:"Constellation",r:["stars","milky","gmyth","explor"],
p:[["Pictures in the stars","Constellations are patterns of stars that people have named and used to tell stories for thousands of years."],["88 official areas","Astronomers divide the whole sky into 88 official constellations."],["Finding the way","Sailors and travelers have used stars and constellations to find directions and tell the seasons."]],
l:"A constellation is a group of stars that forms a recognizable pattern in the night sky, such as Orion the hunter or the Southern Cross. Cultures around the world have seen different pictures in the stars and linked them to myths, farming seasons and navigation. Since 1922, the International Astronomical Union has recognized 88 official constellations, which together cover the entire sky like the pieces of a map. The stars in a constellation usually only look close together; they may lie at very different distances from Earth.",
s:[["Patterns, not groups","From Earth, stars seem to lie on a great dome, but in reality they are spread through space at vastly different distances. The stars of Orion’s Belt, for example, are hundreds of light-years apart. Constellations are like drawings made by connecting dots that happen to line up from our point of view."],["Ancient sky maps","Babylonian astronomers named many constellations over 3,000 years ago, and Greek writers such as Ptolemy, in the 2nd century CE, listed 48 of them. Chinese astronomers divided the sky into 28 lunar mansions and hundreds of small star groups, and many other cultures created their own sky maps."],["The 88 constellations","As European explorers sailed south from the 1500s, they mapped southern stars never seen from Europe and added new constellations, such as Crux, the Southern Cross. In 1922 the International Astronomical Union fixed the list at 88, and in 1930 drew official boundaries, so that every point in the sky belongs to one constellation."],["The zodiac","As Earth orbits the Sun, the Sun appears to move against the background stars through a band of constellations called the zodiac, such as Taurus, Leo and Scorpius. Astronomy, the science of the universe, is different from astrology, the belief that the positions of stars and planets influence people’s lives, which is not supported by scientific evidence."]],
k:[["Official constellations","88"],["Largest","Hydra"],["Smallest","Crux (the Southern Cross)"],["Brightest star","Sirius, in Canis Major"],["Fixed by","International Astronomical Union, 1922 and 1930"]],
d:[["c. 1000 BCE","Babylonian star catalogs name many constellations"],["c. 150 CE","Ptolemy lists 48 constellations"],["964","Al-Sufi publishes the Book of Fixed Stars"],["1500s–1700s","European navigators chart southern constellations"],["1922","IAU adopts 88 official constellations"],["1930","Official constellation boundaries are set"]],
s2:[["Stars of many cultures","The Pleiades star cluster is called Matariki by the Māori of New Zealand, whose New Year begins when it rises before dawn in midwinter. It is Subaru in Japan, Krittika in India and the Seven Sisters in Greek myth. Aboriginal Australians see an emu in the sky, formed by the dark dust lanes of the Milky Way."],["Navigating by the stars","In the Northern Hemisphere, Polaris, the North Star, sits almost directly above the North Pole, so it shows which way is north. In the south, sailors use the Southern Cross to find south. Polynesian navigators crossed thousands of kilometers of ocean by memorizing where stars rise and set on the horizon."],["Star names","Many bright stars have names from Arabic, given by astronomers of the Islamic world, such as Aldebaran, Betelgeuse, Rigel and Vega. In his Book of Fixed Stars of 964, the Persian astronomer Abd al-Rahman al-Sufi described the constellations and recorded the earliest known observation of the Andromeda Galaxy, as a small cloud."],["Orion","Orion, the hunter, is one of the easiest constellations to recognize, seen from almost everywhere on Earth. Its belt of three stars points to Sirius, the brightest star in the night sky. The red giant Betelgeuse marks his shoulder, and below the belt glows the Orion Nebula, a nursery where new stars are forming."],["Light pollution","Artificial light from cities makes the sky glow, hiding all but the brightest stars. Studies suggest that more than a third of humanity can no longer see the Milky Way from home. Dark-sky parks and reserves around the world protect places where people can still see thousands of stars."],["Asterisms","Some famous star patterns are not official constellations but parts of them, called asterisms. The Big Dipper, or Plough, is part of Ursa Major, the Great Bear, and its two end stars point to Polaris. The Summer Triangle of Vega, Deneb and Altair spans three constellations."]],
sp:["Matariki","Matariki, the Māori name for the Pleiades star cluster, marks the Māori New Year in midwinter, when the cluster reappears in the dawn sky in June or July. It is a time for remembering those who have died, celebrating the present and planning for the year ahead. Since 2022 Matariki has been a public holiday in New Zealand, the first official holiday based on Indigenous knowledge of the stars."],
g:[["Constellation","A named pattern or area of stars in the sky"],["Asterism","A star pattern that is not an official constellation"],["Zodiac","The band of constellations the Sun appears to pass through in a year"],["Light-year","The distance light travels in one year, about 9.46 trillion km"]],
f:["There are 88 official constellations.","Hydra is the largest constellation and Crux the smallest.","Many star names come from Arabic."],
fun:"The smallest constellation, Crux (the Southern Cross), appears on the flags of Australia, New Zealand, Brazil, Papua New Guinea and Samoa.",
tl:"Ang mga konstelasyon ay mga hugis o pangkat ng mga bituin na binigyan ng pangalan, gaya ng Orion at Southern Cross. May 88 opisyal na konstelasyon, at ginamit ang mga ito ng mga mandaragat upang malaman ang direksiyon."});

ADDART({id:"nebula",t:"Nebulae and the Birth of Stars",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"☁️",w:"Nebula",r:["stars","supernova","milky","jwst"],
p:[["Clouds in space","A nebula is a giant cloud of gas and dust in space."],["Star nurseries","New stars and planets are born when parts of these clouds collapse under gravity."],["Glowing colors","Some nebulae glow with light from hot young stars, painting space in red, green and blue."]],
l:"A nebula is an enormous cloud of gas, mostly hydrogen and helium, and dust floating in space. Some nebulae are regions where new stars are being born; others are the glowing remains of dying stars. Nebulae can be hundreds of light-years across. Telescopes such as Hubble and the James Webb Space Telescope have photographed them in stunning detail, revealing pillars, bubbles and shells of glowing gas.",
s:[["How stars are born","Inside cold, dense clouds of gas and dust, gravity pulls clumps of material together. As a clump collapses, it spins faster and heats up, forming a protostar surrounded by a flat disk. When its core becomes hot enough, around 10 million degrees Celsius, hydrogen begins to fuse into helium, and a new star shines. Leftover material in the disk can form planets."],["Kinds of nebulae","Emission nebulae glow because nearby hot stars energize their gas, often making it shine red from hydrogen. Reflection nebulae shine blue by reflecting starlight off dust. Dark nebulae are dense clouds of dust that block the light behind them, appearing as dark shapes against brighter regions."],["Nebulae from dying stars","When a star like the Sun runs out of fuel, it puffs off its outer layers, forming a glowing shell called a planetary nebula, despite having nothing to do with planets. The name came from early astronomers whose telescopes showed these round shapes looking like planets. Much more massive stars explode as supernovae, leaving expanding clouds of debris."],["The Orion Nebula","The Orion Nebula, about 1,350 light-years away, is the closest large region of star formation to Earth. It can be seen with the naked eye as a fuzzy patch in Orion’s sword. Inside, telescopes have found hundreds of young stars, many surrounded by disks where planets may be forming."]],
k:[["Made of","Mostly hydrogen and helium gas, with dust"],["Size","Up to hundreds of light-years across"],["Nearest large star nursery","Orion Nebula, about 1,350 light-years away"],["Kinds","Emission, reflection, dark, planetary, supernova remnant"],["Famous image","Pillars of Creation in the Eagle Nebula"]],
d:[["964","Al-Sufi records the Andromeda Galaxy as a small cloud"],["1610","Nicolas-Claude Fabri de Peiresc discovers the Orion Nebula with a telescope"],["1774","Charles Messier begins publishing his catalog of nebulae"],["1864","William Huggins shows that some nebulae are made of glowing gas"],["1995","Hubble photographs the Pillars of Creation"],["2022","Webb photographs the Carina Nebula and the Pillars again"]],
s2:[["The Pillars of Creation","In 1995 the Hubble Space Telescope photographed three towering columns of gas and dust in the Eagle Nebula, about 6,500 light-years away. Called the Pillars of Creation, they are several light-years tall, and new stars are forming inside them. In 2022 the James Webb Space Telescope photographed them again in infrared light, revealing young stars hidden in the dust."],["Seeing through dust","Dust blocks visible light, hiding newborn stars. Infrared light, however, passes through dust more easily. That is why infrared telescopes, such as Spitzer and Webb, can peer inside star-forming clouds and see stars and planetary disks as they form."],["The Messier catalog","In the 1700s the French astronomer Charles Messier, who hunted comets, made a list of fuzzy objects that could be confused with comets. His catalog of 110 objects includes many famous nebulae, star clusters and galaxies: M1 is the Crab Nebula, M42 the Orion Nebula and M31 the Andromeda Galaxy."],["The Crab Nebula","The Crab Nebula is the remains of a star that exploded in 1054. Astronomers in China and elsewhere recorded a new star bright enough to see in daylight for weeks. At its center spins a pulsar, a neutron star rotating about 30 times a second."],["Stars are born in groups","Stars usually form in clusters from the same cloud. Over time the clusters drift apart. The Sun was probably born in such a cluster about 4.6 billion years ago, and its siblings are now scattered across the galaxy. The Pleiades is a young cluster about 100 million years old, still with some leftover dust."],["Recycling the universe","Stars make heavier elements, such as carbon, oxygen and iron, in their cores and in explosions. When they die, they return these elements to the clouds between stars, where new stars and planets form. The atoms in our bodies were made in earlier generations of stars."]],
sp:["We are made of star stuff","Almost all of the hydrogen in the universe was made in the Big Bang, but most heavier elements were made in stars. The calcium in our bones, the iron in our blood and the oxygen we breathe were forged in stars that lived and died before the Sun was born. As the astronomer Carl Sagan put it, we are made of star stuff."],
g:[["Nebula","A cloud of gas and dust in space (plural: nebulae)"],["Protostar","A young star that is still forming"],["Planetary nebula","A glowing shell of gas thrown off by a dying Sun-like star"],["Infrared","Light with longer wavelengths than red light, invisible to our eyes"]],
f:["The Orion Nebula can be seen with the naked eye.","Planetary nebulae have nothing to do with planets.","The Crab Nebula comes from a star that exploded in 1054."],
fun:"The iron in your blood was made inside stars that died before the Sun was born.",
tl:"Ang nebula ay malaking ulap ng gas at alikabok sa kalawakan. Dito isinisilang ang mga bagong bituin at planeta. May mga nebula ring nabubuo mula sa mga namamatay na bituin."});

ADDART({id:"supernova",t:"Supernovae",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"💥",w:"Supernova",r:["stars","bh","pulsar","nebula"],
p:[["Exploding stars","A supernova is the explosion of a star, one of the most powerful events in the universe."],["Brighter than a galaxy","For a few weeks, a single supernova can outshine an entire galaxy of billions of stars."],["Makers of elements","Supernovae scatter heavy elements into space, which later become part of new stars, planets and living things."]],
l:"A supernova is a gigantic explosion that occurs at the end of some stars’ lives. It can release as much energy in a few weeks as the Sun will give off in its entire lifetime of about 10 billion years. Supernovae happen in two main ways: when a massive star’s core collapses, or when a white dwarf star gains too much material and blows apart. They create and spread heavy elements, trigger new star formation, and can leave behind neutron stars or black holes.",
s:[["Massive stars collapse","Stars more than about eight times the Sun’s mass fuse heavier and heavier elements in their cores, until they build up iron. Iron cannot release energy by fusion, so the core suddenly collapses under its own gravity in less than a second. The outer layers rebound in a huge explosion, leaving a neutron star or a black hole."],["White dwarfs explode","A white dwarf is the dense, Earth-sized remnant of a Sun-like star. If it orbits a companion star and pulls gas from it, or merges with another white dwarf, it can reach a critical mass and explode completely. These Type Ia supernovae shine with a similar peak brightness, making them useful for measuring distances in space."],["Historic supernovae","Astronomers have recorded supernovae in our galaxy for about 2,000 years. Chinese records describe a guest star in 185 CE. The supernova of 1006 was the brightest ever recorded, and the 1054 explosion created the Crab Nebula. Tycho Brahe observed one in 1572 and Johannes Kepler in 1604, the last seen in the Milky Way with the naked eye."],["SN 1987A","In February 1987 a star exploded in the Large Magellanic Cloud, a small galaxy near the Milky Way. SN 1987A was the closest supernova seen in nearly 400 years and was visible to the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere. Detectors in Japan and the United States caught a burst of neutrinos from it, confirming theories of how stars collapse."]],
k:[["Energy","Can outshine a whole galaxy for weeks"],["Main types","Core-collapse and white dwarf (Type Ia)"],["Last naked-eye supernova in the Milky Way","1604 (Kepler’s Supernova)"],["Closest recent","SN 1987A, Large Magellanic Cloud"],["Leaves behind","Neutron star, black hole, or nothing"]],
d:[["185 CE","Chinese astronomers record a guest star"],["1006","Brightest supernova ever recorded"],["1054","Supernova that created the Crab Nebula"],["1572","Tycho Brahe observes a supernova"],["1604","Kepler observes the last naked-eye supernova in the Milky Way"],["1987","SN 1987A seen in the Large Magellanic Cloud"]],
s2:[["Measuring the universe","Because Type Ia supernovae reach a similar peak brightness, astronomers use them as standard candles: comparing how bright they look with how bright they really are reveals their distance. In 1998 two teams used them to discover that the expansion of the universe is speeding up, work that won the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Forging elements","Elements heavier than iron, such as some gold, silver and uranium, are made in supernovae and in collisions of neutron stars. The explosions blast these elements into space, enriching clouds of gas. The Sun and Earth formed from such enriched material."],["Shock waves and new stars","The shock waves from supernovae race outward through space, compressing nearby clouds of gas and dust. This can trigger the birth of new stars. Some scientists think a nearby supernova may have helped spark the formation of the solar system."],["Is Earth in danger?","A supernova within about 30 to 50 light-years could damage Earth’s ozone layer, but no star that close is expected to explode for millions of years. Betelgeuse, a red supergiant in Orion about 550 light-years away, will explode someday, perhaps within the next 100,000 years. It would be bright enough to see in daylight, but too far away to harm us."],["Finding supernovae","Supernovae in our own galaxy are rare, perhaps a couple per century, and many are hidden by dust. But astronomers find thousands each year in other galaxies using automated sky surveys. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, which began operating in 2025, is expected to find millions over its ten-year survey of the sky."],["Hypernovae and gamma rays","The most powerful explosions, sometimes called hypernovae, come from very massive, fast-spinning stars. They can produce gamma-ray bursts, narrow beams of intense radiation that are the brightest explosions known, detectable across billions of light-years."]],
sp:["The guest star of 1054","On 4 July 1054, Chinese astronomers recorded a guest star in the constellation Taurus, bright enough to be seen in daylight for 23 days and at night for nearly two years. Today, at that spot, telescopes see the Crab Nebula, an expanding cloud of debris about 11 light-years across, with a rapidly spinning neutron star at its heart."],
g:[["Supernova","The explosion of a star"],["White dwarf","The small, dense remains of a Sun-like star"],["Neutron star","The extremely dense collapsed core of a massive star"],["Standard candle","An object of known brightness used to measure distances"]],
f:["A supernova can outshine a whole galaxy.","The last naked-eye supernova in the Milky Way was seen in 1604.","Some gold on Earth was made in exploding stars and colliding neutron stars."],
fun:"The supernova of 1006 was bright enough to cast shadows at night.",
tl:"Ang supernova ay pagsabog ng isang bituin, isa sa pinakamalakas na pangyayari sa sansinukob. Maaari nitong lampasan ang liwanag ng buong galaxy, at nagkakalat ito ng mabibigat na elemento sa kalawakan."});

ADDART({id:"galaxies",t:"Galaxies",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🌀",w:"Galaxy",r:["milky","stars","bh","darkmatter","hubble"],
p:[["Cities of stars","A galaxy is an enormous collection of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity."],["Billions of galaxies","The observable universe contains hundreds of billions, perhaps trillions, of galaxies."],["Our neighbor","The Andromeda Galaxy, about 2.5 million light-years away, is the nearest large galaxy to the Milky Way."]],
l:"A galaxy is a vast system of stars, gas, dust and dark matter bound together by gravity. Galaxies range from dwarfs with a few million stars to giants with over a trillion. Our own galaxy, the Milky Way, contains perhaps 100 to 400 billion stars. Until the 1920s, many astronomers thought the Milky Way was the whole universe; Edwin Hubble showed that it is just one of countless galaxies scattered across space.",
s:[["Spirals, ellipticals and irregulars","Edwin Hubble sorted galaxies by their shapes. Spiral galaxies, such as the Milky Way and Andromeda, have flat, rotating disks with curving arms of young stars and a central bulge. Elliptical galaxies are smooth, rounded collections of mostly old stars. Irregular galaxies have no clear shape."],["Island universes","In the 1700s, some thinkers, including the philosopher Immanuel Kant, suggested that fuzzy spiral nebulae might be distant island universes. In 1923–1924, Edwin Hubble used the Mount Wilson telescope to measure stars in the Andromeda nebula and showed it was far beyond the Milky Way, proving that other galaxies exist."],["Black holes at the center","Most large galaxies have a supermassive black hole at their center, millions or billions of times the Sun’s mass. The Milky Way’s, called Sagittarius A*, has about 4 million solar masses. In some galaxies, matter falling into the central black hole shines brilliantly as a quasar, outshining the whole galaxy."],["Groups and clusters","Galaxies gather in groups and clusters. The Milky Way, Andromeda and more than 50 smaller galaxies form the Local Group. Clusters can contain thousands of galaxies, and clusters link into superclusters and great filaments, forming a cosmic web with huge empty voids between them."]],
k:[["Galaxies in the observable universe","Hundreds of billions to trillions"],["Nearest large galaxy","Andromeda, about 2.5 million light-years"],["Main types","Spiral, elliptical, irregular"],["Our group","The Local Group, more than 50 galaxies"],["Most distant known","Seen as they were less than 300 million years after the Big Bang"]],
d:[["964","Al-Sufi records the Andromeda Galaxy"],["1755","Kant proposes island universes"],["1924","Hubble proves Andromeda is a separate galaxy"],["1929","Hubble shows galaxies are moving apart"],["1995","Hubble Deep Field image reveals thousands of galaxies"],["2022–2024","Webb finds galaxies from the early universe"]],
s2:[["The Hubble Deep Field","In 1995 astronomers pointed the Hubble Space Telescope at a tiny, seemingly empty patch of sky for about ten days. The resulting image, the Hubble Deep Field, revealed around 3,000 galaxies, some seen as they were billions of years ago. Later deep fields by Hubble and Webb have revealed many thousands more."],["Andromeda is coming","The Andromeda Galaxy is moving toward the Milky Way at about 110 kilometers per second. In roughly 4 to 5 billion years, the two galaxies may collide and merge into a giant elliptical galaxy. Because stars are so far apart, they would almost never crash into each other."],["Colliding galaxies","Galaxies often collide and merge. Gravity pulls them into strange shapes, with long tails of stars, and squeezes their gas, sparking bursts of star formation. Large galaxies such as the Milky Way grew partly by swallowing smaller ones; streams of stars from these eaten galaxies can still be traced."],["Looking back in time","Because light takes time to travel, we see distant galaxies as they were long ago. The James Webb Space Telescope has observed galaxies whose light left them less than 300 million years after the Big Bang, about 13.5 billion years ago, helping astronomers learn how the first galaxies formed."],["The Magellanic Clouds","The Large and Small Magellanic Clouds are two dwarf galaxies orbiting the Milky Way, visible to the naked eye from the Southern Hemisphere as faint patches of light. They are named after Ferdinand Magellan, whose crew described them during their voyage around the world, though peoples of the Southern Hemisphere had known them for thousands of years."],["Dark matter holds them together","Galaxies spin so fast that the gravity of their visible stars and gas could not hold them together. Astronomers think each galaxy sits within a much larger halo of invisible dark matter, whose gravity keeps it from flying apart. Studying galaxy rotation was key to discovering dark matter."]],
sp:["How many galaxies?","Counting galaxies in deep images and allowing for those too faint to see, astronomers estimate that the observable universe contains hundreds of billions to perhaps two trillion galaxies. If each has on average tens to hundreds of billions of stars, the universe may hold more stars than there are grains of sand on all of Earth’s beaches."],
g:[["Galaxy","A huge system of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity"],["Spiral galaxy","A galaxy with a flat disk and curving arms"],["Quasar","An extremely bright galaxy center powered by a supermassive black hole"],["Local Group","The group of galaxies that includes the Milky Way and Andromeda"]],
f:["The Andromeda Galaxy can be seen with the naked eye on dark nights.","Most large galaxies have a supermassive black hole at their center.","The Milky Way and Andromeda may merge in about 4 to 5 billion years."],
fun:"The Andromeda Galaxy is the most distant object most people can see with the naked eye.",
tl:"Ang galaxy ay napakalaking kumpol ng mga bituin, gas, alikabok at dark matter na pinagbubuklod ng grabidad. Bilyon-bilyon ang galaxy sa sansinukob, at ang Andromeda ang pinakamalapit na malaking kapitbahay ng Milky Way."});

;
/* data/space2-3.js */
// Space articles, part 2.3: dark matter and dark energy, the cosmic microwave background, and gravitational waves.
ADDART({id:"darkmatter",t:"Dark Matter and Dark Energy",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🕳️",w:"Dark matter",r:["galaxies","bigbang","grav","relat"],
p:[["The invisible universe","Ordinary matter, everything made of atoms, makes up only about 5 percent of the universe."],["Dark matter","About 27 percent is dark matter, an invisible substance known only from its gravity."],["Dark energy","About 68 percent is dark energy, a mysterious effect that is making the universe expand faster and faster."]],
l:"Dark matter and dark energy are two of the biggest mysteries in science. Dark matter is an unseen form of matter that does not give off, absorb or reflect light, but whose gravity holds galaxies and galaxy clusters together. Dark energy is the name given to whatever is causing the expansion of the universe to speed up. Together they appear to make up about 95 percent of the universe, yet no one knows exactly what either of them is.",
s:[["Clues from galaxies","In the 1930s the Swiss astronomer Fritz Zwicky found that galaxies in the Coma Cluster moved so fast that the cluster should fly apart unless it contained far more mass than could be seen. In the 1970s Vera Rubin and Kent Ford showed that stars at the edges of spiral galaxies orbit as fast as those near the center, another sign of hidden mass."],["What could dark matter be?","Dark matter is not ordinary dark objects such as dust or dead stars; measurements show there is not enough ordinary matter to explain it. Most physicists think it is a new kind of particle that barely interacts with ordinary matter. Experiments deep underground, in space and at particle colliders are searching for it, so far without success."],["Bending light","Gravity bends light, an effect predicted by Einstein. Massive galaxy clusters act like giant lenses, distorting the images of galaxies behind them. By mapping this gravitational lensing, astronomers can see where mass is, including dark matter. The Bullet Cluster, two colliding clusters, shows the dark matter separated from the hot gas, strong evidence that it is real."],["The accelerating universe","Since the Big Bang, the universe has been expanding. In 1998 two teams studying distant exploding stars discovered that the expansion is speeding up rather than slowing down. Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt and Adam Riess won the 2011 Nobel Prize in Physics for the discovery. The cause was named dark energy."]],
k:[["Ordinary matter","About 5% of the universe"],["Dark matter","About 27%"],["Dark energy","About 68%"],["Dark matter first proposed","1933, Fritz Zwicky"],["Accelerating expansion discovered","1998"]],
d:[["1933","Zwicky finds hidden mass in the Coma Cluster"],["1970s","Vera Rubin and Kent Ford measure galaxy rotation"],["1998","Expansion of the universe found to be accelerating"],["2006","Bullet Cluster provides evidence for dark matter"],["2011","Nobel Prize for the discovery of accelerating expansion"],["2023","Euclid space telescope launched to map dark matter and dark energy"]],
s2:[["Vera Rubin","Vera Rubin, an American astronomer, measured how fast stars orbit in dozens of spiral galaxies and found that the outer stars move far too fast to be held by visible matter alone. Her work convinced many scientists that dark matter exists. The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, which began operating in 2025, is named in her honor."],["The cosmic web","Computer simulations show that, after the Big Bang, dark matter clumped together first, forming a vast web of filaments. Ordinary gas then fell into these filaments, and galaxies formed where they crossed. Maps of millions of galaxies match this picture closely."],["What is dark energy?","One idea is that dark energy is a property of empty space itself, which Einstein included in his equations as the cosmological constant. Others suggest it could change over time. Results from the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument in 2024 and 2025 hinted that dark energy might be weakening, but more data are needed to be sure."],["Other explanations","A minority of scientists think the evidence for dark matter might instead mean that gravity behaves differently on very large scales than our current theories say. Ideas such as Modified Newtonian Dynamics explain some galaxy motions but struggle to explain observations of galaxy clusters and the early universe."],["Space telescopes","The European Space Agency’s Euclid telescope, launched in 2023, and other new survey telescopes are mapping billions of galaxies to measure how dark matter is distributed and how dark energy has affected the expansion of the universe over time."],["The fate of the universe","If dark energy keeps pushing the universe apart, galaxies will drift farther and farther away from one another. In the far future, astronomers in the Milky Way might see no other galaxies at all beyond their local group, as the rest would have moved out of sight."]],
sp:["A universe we cannot see","Everything we have ever seen with telescopes, all the stars, planets, gas and galaxies, makes up only about one-twentieth of the universe. The rest is dark matter and dark energy, which we know about only because of their effects on what we can see. Understanding them may require new laws of physics."],
g:[["Dark matter","Invisible matter detected only by its gravity"],["Dark energy","The unknown cause of the speeding-up expansion of the universe"],["Gravitational lensing","The bending of light by the gravity of massive objects"],["Cosmological constant","A term in Einstein’s equations representing the energy of empty space"]],
f:["Ordinary matter makes up only about 5 percent of the universe.","Vera Rubin’s measurements of galaxy rotation supported dark matter.","The universe’s expansion is speeding up."],
fun:"About 95 percent of the universe is made of something no one has ever seen directly.",
tl:"Ang dark matter at dark energy ay malalaking misteryo sa agham. Hindi ito nakikita, ngunit tinatayang bumubuo ng mga 95 porsiyento ng sansinukob. Pinagbubuklod ng dark matter ang mga galaxy, habang pinabibilis ng dark energy ang paglawak ng sansinukob."});

ADDART({id:"cmb",t:"The Cosmic Microwave Background",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"📡",w:"Cosmic microwave background",r:["bigbang","darkmatter","galaxies","light"],
p:[["The oldest light","The cosmic microwave background is light left over from the early universe, about 380,000 years after the Big Bang."],["Everywhere in the sky","It comes from every direction and fills the whole universe."],["A baby picture","Maps of it show tiny ripples that grew into galaxies and clusters of galaxies."]],
l:"The cosmic microwave background, or CMB, is faint radiation that fills the entire universe and is the oldest light we can see. It was released about 380,000 years after the Big Bang, when the universe had cooled enough for atoms to form and light to travel freely. Stretched by the expansion of space over 13.8 billion years, that light now arrives as microwaves, with a temperature just 2.7 degrees above absolute zero. Its discovery in 1965 provided strong evidence for the Big Bang.",
s:[["When light set free","In the early universe, matter was a hot, dense fog of charged particles that scattered light, like fog scatters car headlights. About 380,000 years after the Big Bang, the temperature dropped to around 3,000 degrees, electrons joined nuclei to form atoms, and the fog cleared. Light that was released then has been traveling ever since."],["An accidental discovery","In 1964 and 1965, Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson, working with a large horn-shaped antenna at Bell Labs in New Jersey, found a faint hiss of microwave noise coming from every direction. After ruling out pigeons nesting in the antenna, they realized it came from space. Physicists at nearby Princeton recognized it as the afterglow of the Big Bang, and Penzias and Wilson won the 1978 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Cooled by expansion","When it was released, the CMB glowed like hot metal. As the universe expanded, the light was stretched to longer and longer wavelengths. Today its temperature is about 2.725 kelvin, or minus 270.4 degrees Celsius, and it peaks in the microwave part of the spectrum."],["Ripples in the glow","The CMB is almost exactly the same temperature everywhere, but satellite maps show tiny variations of about 1 part in 100,000. These ripples mark slightly denser regions of the early universe, which gravity later pulled together to form galaxies and clusters. Their pattern reveals the age, shape and ingredients of the universe."]],
k:[["Released","About 380,000 years after the Big Bang"],["Temperature today","About 2.725 K (−270.4 °C)"],["Discovered","1965 by Arno Penzias and Robert Wilson"],["Variations","About 1 part in 100,000"],["Mapped by","COBE, WMAP and Planck satellites"]],
d:[["1948","Ralph Alpher and Robert Herman predict leftover radiation from the Big Bang"],["1965","Penzias and Wilson detect the CMB"],["1989","COBE satellite launched"],["2001","WMAP satellite launched"],["2009","Planck satellite launched"],["2013","Planck releases detailed maps of the CMB"]],
s2:[["COBE","NASA’s Cosmic Background Explorer, launched in 1989, showed that the CMB has exactly the spectrum expected from the Big Bang and detected its tiny ripples for the first time. John Mather and George Smoot won the 2006 Nobel Prize in Physics for this work. Smoot said that seeing the map was like seeing the face of God, a remark that became famous."],["WMAP and Planck","NASA’s WMAP satellite, launched in 2001, and the European Space Agency’s Planck, launched in 2009, mapped the CMB in ever greater detail. Planck’s measurements helped fix the age of the universe at about 13.8 billion years and showed that it is about 5 percent ordinary matter, 27 percent dark matter and 68 percent dark energy."],["Seeing it at home","Before digital broadcasting, a small part of the static or snow on an untuned analog television was caused by the CMB. Some of the flickering dots people saw were photons that had been traveling for more than 13 billion years."],["A flat universe","The size of the ripples in the CMB shows that space on the largest scales is very nearly flat, meaning that parallel lines stay parallel. This matches the predictions of the theory of cosmic inflation, which proposes that the universe expanded incredibly fast in a tiny fraction of a second after the Big Bang."],["The cold spot","Maps of the CMB show a few puzzling features, such as an unusually large cold spot in the southern sky. Most scientists think these are chance fluctuations, but some have suggested more exotic explanations. Studying such details helps test theories of the early universe."],["Ground telescopes","Telescopes in very dry, high places, such as the South Pole and the Atacama Desert in Chile, study the CMB from the ground. They search for faint swirling patterns in its polarization that could be signs of gravitational waves from the earliest moments after the Big Bang."]],
sp:["The pigeons of Holmdel","When Penzias and Wilson first heard the mysterious hiss in their antenna, they suspected the pigeons nesting inside it, and the white droppings the birds left behind, which they called a white dielectric material. They removed the pigeons and cleaned the antenna, but the hiss remained. It was not caused by birds at all, but by the afterglow of the birth of the universe."],
g:[["Microwave","A type of radio wave with a short wavelength"],["Kelvin","A temperature scale starting at absolute zero"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature, about −273.15 °C"],["Cosmic inflation","A proposed burst of extremely rapid expansion just after the Big Bang"]],
f:["The CMB is the oldest light in the universe.","It was discovered by accident in 1965.","Its temperature is less than 3 degrees above absolute zero."],
fun:"Part of the snow on old analog televisions came from the afterglow of the Big Bang.",
tl:"Ang cosmic microwave background ay pinakalumang liwanag sa sansinukob, inilabas mga 380,000 taon matapos ang Big Bang. Nasa lahat ng direksiyon ito at malakas na ebidensiya ng Big Bang."});

ADDART({id:"gravwaves",t:"Gravitational Waves",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"〰️",w:"Gravitational wave",r:["relat","bh","pulsar","grav"],
p:[["Ripples in spacetime","Gravitational waves are ripples in the fabric of space and time caused by massive objects accelerating."],["Einstein’s prediction","Albert Einstein predicted them in 1916 as part of his general theory of relativity."],["First detected in 2015","Scientists first detected them directly in 2015, from two black holes colliding over a billion light-years away."]],
l:"Gravitational waves are ripples in spacetime that travel at the speed of light, produced when massive objects such as black holes or neutron stars move violently, for example when they orbit and collide. Albert Einstein predicted them in 1916, but they are so faint that he doubted they could ever be measured. In September 2015 the LIGO observatories in the United States detected waves from two merging black holes, opening a new way to observe the universe.",
s:[["Stretching space","According to general relativity, mass and energy curve spacetime, and accelerating masses send out waves in it, like ripples spreading on a pond. As a gravitational wave passes, it stretches space in one direction and squeezes it in another, by an incredibly tiny amount."],["How LIGO works","LIGO, the Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory, has two detectors, in Washington and Louisiana. Each has two arms 4 kilometers long at right angles. Laser light bounces back and forth along the arms; a passing wave changes their lengths by less than a thousandth of the width of a proton, which shows up in how the light beams combine."],["The first detection","On 14 September 2015, both LIGO detectors recorded a signal lasting a fraction of a second, a rising chirp. It came from two black holes, about 36 and 29 times the Sun’s mass, merging about 1.3 billion light-years away. In that instant, energy equal to about three Suns was released as gravitational waves."],["A Nobel Prize","Rainer Weiss, Kip Thorne and Barry Barish, who led the development of LIGO, won the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics. Thousands of scientists from many countries worked on the discovery. Since then, LIGO, the Virgo detector in Italy and the KAGRA detector in Japan have detected hundreds of mergers."]],
k:[["Predicted","1916 by Albert Einstein"],["First direct detection","14 September 2015"],["Speed","The speed of light"],["Detectors","LIGO (USA), Virgo (Italy), KAGRA (Japan)"],["Nobel Prize","2017 (Weiss, Thorne, Barish)"]],
d:[["1916","Einstein predicts gravitational waves"],["1974","Hulse and Taylor discover a binary pulsar"],["2015","LIGO makes the first direct detection"],["2017","Waves from colliding neutron stars detected along with light"],["2017","Nobel Prize in Physics awarded for the detection"],["2023","Pulsar timing arrays find evidence of a gravitational-wave background"]],
s2:[["Indirect evidence first","In 1974 Russell Hulse and Joseph Taylor discovered a pair of neutron stars orbiting each other. Over years, the orbit shrank exactly as expected if the stars were losing energy by giving off gravitational waves. This indirect evidence won them the 1993 Nobel Prize in Physics."],["Colliding neutron stars","On 17 August 2017, detectors caught gravitational waves from two neutron stars merging about 130 million light-years away. Seconds later, telescopes detected a burst of gamma rays, and over the next days, observatories around the world watched the glow of the explosion. It showed that such collisions create heavy elements, such as gold and platinum."],["Listening to the universe","Because gravitational wave signals can be converted into sound, scientists often describe detectors as listening to the universe. Merging black holes produce a rising chirp. Each signal reveals the masses and spins of the objects and how far away they were."],["Pulsar timing arrays","Pulsars are spinning neutron stars that send out radio pulses with clocklike regularity. In 2023, teams that had timed dozens of pulsars for years in North America, Europe, China, India and Australia reported evidence of a gentle background of very long gravitational waves, probably from pairs of supermassive black holes across the universe."],["Future detectors","The European Space Agency is planning LISA, the Laser Interferometer Space Antenna, three spacecraft flying millions of kilometers apart, due to launch in the 2030s. It will detect waves from supermassive black holes. New ground observatories, such as the Einstein Telescope in Europe and Cosmic Explorer in the United States, are also being planned."],["Why it matters","Gravitational waves let astronomers observe events that give off little or no light, such as black holes colliding. Combining gravitational waves with light, a field called multi-messenger astronomy, gives a fuller picture of the universe’s most violent events and tests Einstein’s theory in extreme conditions."]],
sp:["A tiny wobble","The waves detected in 2015 had traveled for 1.3 billion years, since before complex life appeared on Earth. By the time they reached LIGO, they changed the 4-kilometer arms by less than one ten-thousandth of the width of a proton. Measuring such a tiny change required shielding the detectors from vibrations, including passing trucks, ocean waves and even earthquakes on the other side of the world."],
g:[["Spacetime","Space and time combined into a single fabric in Einstein’s theory"],["Interferometer","An instrument that combines light waves to measure tiny distances"],["Binary","A pair of objects orbiting each other"],["Multi-messenger astronomy","Studying cosmic events using different signals, such as light and gravitational waves"]],
f:["Einstein predicted gravitational waves in 1916.","The first direct detection was in September 2015.","Gravitational waves travel at the speed of light."],
fun:"The gravitational waves first detected in 2015 had been traveling for 1.3 billion years.",
tl:"Ang gravitational wave ay alon sa espasyo at panahon na nililikha ng malalaking bagay gaya ng nagbabanggaang black hole. Hinulaan ito ni Einstein noong 1916 at unang natuklasan noong 2015."});

;
/* data/space2-4.js */
// Space articles, part 2.4: the Voyager probes, living in space, and the search for life beyond Earth.
ADDART({id:"voyager",t:"The Voyager Probes",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"🛰️",w:"Voyager program",r:["jupiter","saturn","uranep","space","kuiper"],
p:[["Grand tour","Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, launched in 1977, flew past the giant planets and sent back the first close-up views of many of them."],["Into interstellar space","Both probes have left the Sun’s bubble of solar wind and are now in interstellar space."],["A message for the stars","Each carries a Golden Record of sounds and images from Earth."]],
l:"The Voyager probes are two NASA spacecraft launched in 1977 to study the outer planets. Taking advantage of a rare alignment of Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, Voyager 2 became the only spacecraft to visit all four giant planets, while Voyager 1 explored Jupiter and Saturn and its large moon Titan. Nearly 50 years later, both are still sending data from beyond the edge of the Sun’s influence, making them the most distant human-made objects.",
s:[["A rare alignment","In the late 1970s, the outer planets lined up in a way that happens only about once every 175 years. A spacecraft could use each planet’s gravity to swing on to the next, a technique called a gravity assist, saving years of travel. NASA built two identical probes to take advantage of it."],["Jupiter and Saturn","The Voyagers reached Jupiter in 1979. They discovered active volcanoes on its moon Io, the first found beyond Earth, and faint rings around Jupiter. At Saturn, in 1980 and 1981, they revealed intricate detail in its rings and studied Titan’s thick, hazy atmosphere."],["Uranus and Neptune","Voyager 2 flew past Uranus in 1986 and Neptune in 1989, the only spacecraft ever to visit them. It discovered new moons and rings at both, found Neptune’s Great Dark Spot, a giant storm, and spotted geysers on Neptune’s moon Triton. Its images are still our best close-up views of these ice giants."],["Leaving the solar system","The Sun’s solar wind blows a huge bubble around the solar system called the heliosphere. Voyager 1 crossed its edge, the heliopause, in August 2012, becoming the first spacecraft to reach interstellar space. Voyager 2 followed in November 2018. They are still within the Sun’s gravitational reach, and will be for tens of thousands of years."]],
k:[["Launched","1977 (Voyager 2 on 20 August, Voyager 1 on 5 September)"],["Planets visited","Voyager 1: Jupiter, Saturn; Voyager 2: Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune"],["Entered interstellar space","Voyager 1 in 2012, Voyager 2 in 2018"],["Power","Radioisotope generators using plutonium"],["Distance of Voyager 1","Over 25 billion km from the Sun"]],
d:[["1977","Both Voyagers are launched"],["1979","Voyagers fly past Jupiter"],["1980–1981","Voyagers fly past Saturn"],["1986","Voyager 2 flies past Uranus"],["1989","Voyager 2 flies past Neptune"],["2012","Voyager 1 enters interstellar space"]],
s2:[["The Golden Record","Each Voyager carries a gold-plated copper record, a message to any intelligent beings who might find it. It contains greetings in 55 languages, music from many cultures, from Bach to Javanese gamelan and Peruvian panpipes, natural sounds such as rain and whales, and 115 images of life on Earth. A committee led by Carl Sagan chose the contents."],["Powered by plutonium","Sunlight is too weak in the outer solar system for solar panels, so each Voyager is powered by radioisotope thermoelectric generators, which turn heat from decaying plutonium into electricity. Their power falls by about 4 watts a year, so engineers have gradually switched off heaters and instruments to keep them running."],["Talking across billions of kilometers","Voyager 1’s radio signal, about as powerful as a refrigerator light bulb, takes more than 22 hours to reach Earth. NASA’s Deep Space Network of giant antennas in California, Spain and Australia listens for it. In 2023 and 2024, engineers fixed computer and thruster problems from Earth, using programming techniques from the 1970s."],["The Pale Blue Dot","On 14 February 1990, before its cameras were switched off, Voyager 1 took a family portrait of the solar system, including a picture of Earth as a tiny speck from about 6 billion kilometers away. Carl Sagan called it the Pale Blue Dot, and it became one of the most famous images in history."],["The fastest and farthest","Voyager 1 travels at about 17 kilometers per second relative to the Sun, covering roughly 540 million kilometers a year. In about 40,000 years, it will pass within about 1.6 light-years of a star called Gliese 445. Voyager 2 is slightly slower and heading in a different direction."],["The end of the mission","As their power runs down, the Voyagers will be able to operate fewer and fewer instruments. NASA expects that sometime in the late 2020s or 2030s, they will no longer have enough power to send data. They will then continue silently through the galaxy, carrying their Golden Records, for billions of years."]],
sp:["Sounds of Earth","The Golden Record’s greetings range from ancient Sumerian and Akkadian to Amoy, Bengali, Swahili and Zulu. Its music includes a Navajo night chant, a Senegalese percussion piece, Chuck Berry’s Johnny B. Goode, Beethoven and a song from Azerbaijan. On the cover, diagrams explain how to play the record and where Earth is, using the positions of pulsars as a map."],
g:[["Gravity assist","Using a planet’s gravity to change a spacecraft’s speed and direction"],["Heliosphere","The bubble around the Sun formed by the solar wind"],["Interstellar space","The space between the stars, beyond the heliosphere"],["Radioisotope generator","A power source that makes electricity from the heat of radioactive decay"]],
f:["Voyager 2 is the only spacecraft to have visited Uranus and Neptune.","Voyager 1 is the most distant human-made object.","Each Voyager carries a Golden Record with greetings in 55 languages."],
fun:"A radio signal from Voyager 1 takes more than 22 hours to reach Earth.",
tl:"Ang Voyager 1 at 2 ay mga sasakyang pangkalawakan ng NASA na inilunsad noong 1977. Binisita nila ang malalaking planeta at ngayon ay nasa interstellar space na, dala ang Golden Record na may mga tunog at larawan mula sa Daigdig."});

ADDART({id:"astronauts",t:"Living in Space",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👩‍🚀",w:"Astronaut",r:["iss","space","apollo","tereshkova","armstrong"],
p:[["Astronauts","Astronauts, also called cosmonauts in Russia and taikonauts in China, are people trained to travel and work in space."],["Life without weight","In orbit, astronauts float in microgravity, which changes how they eat, sleep and even how their bodies work."],["Hundreds have flown","Since Yuri Gagarin’s flight in 1961, more than 600 people from over 40 countries have been to space."]],
l:"Living in space means surviving in an environment with no air, extreme temperatures, harmful radiation and almost no felt gravity. Since Yuri Gagarin became the first human in space in 1961, astronauts have orbited Earth, walked on the Moon and lived for months aboard space stations. The International Space Station has been continuously occupied since November 2000, and China’s Tiangong station since 2021. Spaceflight is changing too, as private companies now fly crews into orbit.",
s:[["Becoming an astronaut","Astronauts are usually scientists, engineers, doctors or pilots. Training takes years and includes learning spacecraft systems, robotics, foreign languages and survival skills. They practice spacewalks underwater in giant pools, which mimic weightlessness, and fly in aircraft that dive to create about 20 seconds of free fall at a time."],["Why astronauts float","Astronauts on the space station are not beyond Earth’s gravity; it is still about 90 percent as strong there. They float because the station and everyone in it are falling around Earth together, at about 28,000 kilometers per hour, in a constant free fall called orbit. This condition is called microgravity."],["Daily life","Astronauts eat packaged and dried food, often with sauces to help it stick to spoons. Water is recycled from sweat, breath and urine. They sleep in sleeping bags tied to walls, and use toilets that work with suction. Because there is no up or down, they can work on the ceiling as easily as the floor."],["Effects on the body","Without gravity pulling on them, muscles and bones weaken: astronauts can lose about 1 to 2 percent of bone mass in some bones each month. Fluids shift toward the head, faces look puffy, and some astronauts’ eyesight changes. To stay healthy they exercise about two hours a day on treadmills, bikes and resistance machines."]],
k:[["First human in space","Yuri Gagarin, 12 April 1961"],["First woman in space","Valentina Tereshkova, 1963"],["Longest single flight","Valeri Polyakov, about 437 days (1994–1995)"],["People who have flown to space","More than 600"],["Space stations in use","International Space Station, Tiangong"]],
d:[["1961","Yuri Gagarin orbits Earth"],["1963","Valentina Tereshkova becomes the first woman in space"],["1965","Alexei Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1969","Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walk on the Moon"],["2000","The ISS becomes permanently occupied"],["2021","China begins crewed operations on Tiangong"]],
s2:[["Spacewalks","Outside the spacecraft, astronauts wear spacesuits that are like small personal spacecraft, supplying oxygen, controlling temperature and protecting against tiny meteoroids. Alexei Leonov made the first spacewalk in 1965, and his suit swelled so much that he struggled to get back inside. Today spacewalks to repair and upgrade stations can last more than seven hours."],["Radiation","Earth’s atmosphere and magnetic field shield us from much of the radiation in space. Astronauts on the space station receive in a day roughly what people on the ground receive in several months to a year from natural sources. On trips to the Moon or Mars, radiation from the Sun and from deep space would be one of the greatest dangers."],["Space stations","The Soviet Union launched the first space station, Salyut 1, in 1971, followed by the long-lasting Mir. The International Space Station, built by the United States, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada, has hosted astronauts from more than 20 countries. China’s Tiangong station is built and operated by China, which plans to host astronauts from other countries."],["Private spaceflight","In 2020, SpaceX’s Crew Dragon became the first privately built spacecraft to carry astronauts to the space station. Companies such as Blue Origin and Virgin Galactic now fly tourists on short trips to the edge of space, and private crews have flown missions in orbit, including the first spacewalk by non-professional astronauts in 2024."],["Growing plants","Astronauts have grown lettuce, radishes, chili peppers and flowers in space. Experiments show how plants sense direction without gravity, using light instead. Growing food will be important for long journeys, such as missions to Mars, which could take two to three years there and back."],["Twins in space","In 2015 and 2016, NASA astronaut Scott Kelly spent nearly a year on the space station, while his identical twin brother, Mark, a retired astronaut, stayed on Earth. Comparing them helped scientists learn how long spaceflight affects the body, from genes to the immune system. Most changes returned to normal after Scott came home."]],
sp:["Overview effect","Many astronauts describe a powerful feeling when they see Earth from space: the planet looks small, beautiful and fragile, with no borders visible between countries. This change in outlook is called the overview effect. Some astronauts say it made them feel a deep sense of connection to all people and a strong wish to protect the planet."],
g:[["Microgravity","The condition of near weightlessness experienced in orbit"],["Orbit","The curved path of an object around a planet or star"],["Spacewalk","Activity by an astronaut outside a spacecraft"],["Cosmonaut","A Russian astronaut"]],
f:["Astronauts on the space station see about 16 sunrises a day.","The ISS has been continuously occupied since November 2000.","Astronauts can grow a few centimeters taller in space because their spines stretch."],
fun:"Astronauts on the space station see about 16 sunrises and sunsets every day.",
tl:"Ang mga astronaut ay sinanay upang maglakbay at magtrabaho sa kalawakan. Sa orbit, lumulutang sila dahil sa microgravity, at kailangan nilang mag-ehersisyo araw-araw upang mapanatiling malakas ang buto at kalamnan."});

ADDART({id:"astrobio",t:"The Search for Life Beyond Earth",c:"Space and Astronomy",i:"👽",w:"Astrobiology",r:["exoplanet","mars","jupiter","saturn","jwst"],
p:[["Are we alone?","Astrobiology is the science that asks whether life exists anywhere else in the universe."],["Where to look","Scientists search on Mars, on icy moons with hidden oceans and on planets around other stars."],["No life found yet","So far, no life has been found beyond Earth, but the search has never been more active."]],
l:"The search for life beyond Earth is one of the oldest questions people have asked: are we alone in the universe? Astrobiology combines astronomy, biology, chemistry and geology to study how life began on Earth and where else it might exist. Scientists look for signs of past or present microbes on Mars, explore moons such as Europa and Enceladus that hide oceans beneath their ice, study the atmospheres of planets around other stars and listen for radio signals from possible civilizations. No evidence of extraterrestrial life has yet been confirmed.",
s:[["What life needs","As far as we know, life needs liquid water, a source of energy and chemical building blocks such as carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen and oxygen. On Earth, microbes called extremophiles thrive in boiling hot springs, acidic lakes, deep ice and rocks kilometers underground, showing that life can survive in harsh places."],["Mars","Mars once had rivers, lakes and perhaps an ocean. Rovers such as Curiosity and Perseverance have found ancient lakebeds and organic molecules, the carbon-based chemicals of life. In 2024 Perseverance found a rock nicknamed Cheyava Falls with patterns that could have been made by ancient microbes, though they could also have formed without life."],["Ocean moons","Jupiter’s moon Europa and Saturn’s moon Enceladus have salty oceans of liquid water beneath icy crusts. The Cassini spacecraft flew through plumes of water spraying from Enceladus and found salts and organic molecules. NASA’s Europa Clipper and Europe’s JUICE are on their way to study Jupiter’s icy moons in the 2030s."],["Planets around other stars","Astronomers have found more than 6,000 exoplanets, some rocky and in the habitable zone where liquid water could exist. Telescopes like the James Webb Space Telescope can study their atmospheres, looking for gases such as oxygen, methane or water vapor that might hint at life, called biosignatures."]],
k:[["Life found beyond Earth","None confirmed"],["Main targets","Mars, Europa, Enceladus, Titan, exoplanets"],["Known exoplanets","More than 6,000"],["Famous equation","Drake equation (1961)"],["Radio search","SETI, since 1960"]],
d:[["1960","Frank Drake begins the first radio search, Project Ozma"],["1976","Viking landers test Martian soil for life"],["1977","The Wow! signal is detected"],["1995","First exoplanet around a Sun-like star is found"],["2005","Cassini discovers water plumes on Enceladus"],["2024","Europa Clipper is launched"]],
s2:[["The Drake equation","In 1961 the astronomer Frank Drake wrote an equation to estimate how many civilizations in our galaxy might be able to communicate with us. It multiplies factors such as the number of stars, how many have planets, how many planets could support life and how long civilizations last. Many factors are unknown, so estimates range from almost none to millions."],["Listening for signals","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence, or SETI, uses radio telescopes to listen for signals that could only come from technology. In 1977 a telescope in Ohio recorded a strong, brief signal, and the astronomer Jerry Ehman wrote Wow! beside it. It was never detected again, and its origin remains unknown."],["The Fermi paradox","The physicist Enrico Fermi is said to have asked, Where is everybody? If the galaxy is billions of years old and has many planets, why have we seen no sign of other civilizations? Possible answers include that life is rare, that civilizations are far apart or short-lived, or that we have not looked carefully enough."],["Titan","Saturn’s largest moon, Titan, has a thick atmosphere and lakes and seas of liquid methane and ethane. It may also hide a water ocean deep underground. NASA’s Dragonfly mission, a nuclear-powered drone, is planned to launch in 2028 to fly between sites on Titan studying its chemistry."],["Life as we don’t know it","Some scientists wonder whether life elsewhere could be very different, perhaps using liquids other than water, such as methane on Titan. Recognizing such life would be hard, so astrobiologists try to think about what signs any living system might leave behind, such as complex chemistry out of balance with its surroundings."],["Protecting other worlds","Spacecraft sent to places where life might exist are carefully cleaned, so that microbes from Earth do not contaminate them and confuse the search. Cassini and Galileo were deliberately crashed into Saturn and Jupiter at the ends of their missions, so they could never hit and contaminate Enceladus or Europa."]],
sp:["Extremophiles","Some of Earth’s toughest creatures are tardigrades, or water bears, microscopic animals about half a millimeter long. They can survive drying out, freezing, extreme heat and pressure, and even, for short periods, the vacuum and radiation of space, as experiments in orbit in 2007 showed. Extremophiles like these give hope that life could exist in harsh places beyond Earth."],
g:[["Astrobiology","The study of life in the universe"],["Extremophile","A living thing that thrives in extreme conditions"],["Biosignature","A sign, such as a gas, that could indicate life"],["SETI","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence"]],
f:["No life beyond Earth has yet been confirmed.","Europa and Enceladus have oceans beneath their ice.","Tardigrades have survived exposure to space."],
fun:"Tiny animals called tardigrades have survived being exposed to outer space.",
tl:"Ang astrobiology ay agham na nagtatanong kung may buhay sa ibang bahagi ng sansinukob. Naghahanap ang mga siyentipiko sa Mars, sa mga buwang may karagatan sa ilalim ng yelo, at sa mga planeta sa ibang bituin, ngunit wala pang natatagpuang buhay."});

;
/* data/physics2-1.js */
// Physics articles, part 2.1: energy, heat and thermodynamics, and waves.
ADDART({id:"energyphys",t:"Energy",c:"Science",i:"⚡",w:"Energy",r:["newton","grav","elec","thermo","relat"],
p:[["The ability to do work","In physics, energy is the capacity to do work, such as moving an object, heating it or making light."],["Never created or destroyed","Energy can change from one form to another, but the total amount always stays the same."],["Measured in joules","Scientists measure energy in joules, named after the English physicist James Prescott Joule."]],
l:"Energy is one of the most important ideas in physics. It is the ability to cause change: to move things, warm them, light them up or make them grow. Energy comes in many forms, including kinetic energy of motion, potential energy stored by position, chemical energy in food and fuel, thermal energy, electrical energy, light and nuclear energy. According to the law of conservation of energy, energy can be transformed from one form into another but never created or destroyed.",
s:[["Kinetic and potential","A moving object has kinetic energy: the faster and heavier it is, the more it has. Potential energy is stored energy, such as that of a book on a high shelf, a stretched spring or water behind a dam. When the book falls, its potential energy turns into kinetic energy."],["Conservation of energy","In the 1840s scientists including Julius Robert von Mayer, James Joule and Hermann von Helmholtz showed that energy is conserved. Joule measured how much work was needed to warm water by stirring it, proving that mechanical work and heat are forms of the same thing. This law is one of the most thoroughly tested principles in science."],["Energy changes","Every day we see energy changing form. A car engine turns chemical energy in fuel into motion and heat. A solar panel turns light into electricity. Plants turn sunlight into chemical energy through photosynthesis, and our bodies turn the chemical energy in food into movement and warmth."],["Measuring energy","The unit of energy is the joule. Lifting an apple about one meter takes roughly one joule. Food energy is often given in kilocalories, where one kilocalorie is about 4,184 joules. Electricity is usually sold in kilowatt-hours; one kilowatt-hour is 3.6 million joules, enough to run a 100-watt lamp for 10 hours."]],
k:[["Unit","Joule (J)"],["Main law","Conservation of energy"],["Common forms","Kinetic, potential, thermal, chemical, electrical, nuclear, light"],["Power","Energy per second, measured in watts"],["Famous equation","E = mc² (mass–energy equivalence)"]],
d:[["1687","Newton’s laws of motion lay the foundations of mechanics"],["1807","Thomas Young uses the word energy in its modern sense"],["1840s","Joule, Mayer and Helmholtz establish conservation of energy"],["1850","Clausius states the laws of thermodynamics"],["1905","Einstein shows that mass and energy are equivalent"],["1938","Nuclear fission is discovered"]],
s2:[["E = mc²","In 1905 Albert Einstein showed that mass is a form of energy. His equation E = mc², where c is the speed of light, means a tiny amount of mass contains an enormous amount of energy. This is the source of the energy released in nuclear reactors, nuclear weapons and the Sun, where a small fraction of mass turns into energy during nuclear reactions."],["Power","Power is how fast energy is used or transferred, measured in watts, where one watt is one joule per second. A light-emitting diode bulb may use about 10 watts, a microwave oven around 1,000 watts, and a large power station several billion watts."],["Efficiency","No machine turns all the energy it uses into the useful form we want; some is always lost, usually as heat. Efficiency measures the useful share. LED bulbs turn a much larger share of electricity into light than old incandescent bulbs, which mostly made heat."],["Energy in living things","Almost all energy for life on Earth comes from the Sun, captured by plants and other organisms through photosynthesis. Animals get energy by eating plants or other animals. Inside cells, energy from food is stored in a molecule called ATP, which powers activities from muscle movement to thinking."],["Energy for society","Humans use energy for heating, cooling, transport, industry and electricity. Most of the world’s energy still comes from fossil fuels: coal, oil and natural gas. Burning them releases carbon dioxide, which warms the climate, so many countries are expanding renewable sources such as solar, wind and hydropower."],["Work and energy","In physics, work is done when a force moves something. Pushing a heavy box across a floor does work and uses energy; holding it still, though tiring, does no work in the physics sense. The work done equals the force multiplied by the distance moved in the direction of the force."]],
sp:["A joule of energy","A joule is a small amount of energy. A person climbing one flight of stairs uses about 2,000 joules of energy against gravity. A banana contains about 400,000 joules of food energy. The Sun gives off about 380 trillion trillion joules every second, of which Earth receives only a tiny fraction, still far more than all of humanity uses."],
g:[["Energy","The ability to do work or cause change"],["Kinetic energy","The energy of motion"],["Potential energy","Stored energy due to position or condition"],["Joule","The unit of energy"]],
f:["Energy can change form but cannot be created or destroyed.","One kilowatt-hour equals 3.6 million joules.","Almost all of the energy for life on Earth comes from the Sun."],
fun:"Lifting an apple about one meter takes roughly one joule of energy.",
tl:"Ang enerhiya ay kakayahang gumawa ng trabaho o magdulot ng pagbabago. Maaari itong magbago ng anyo ngunit hindi nalilikha o nawawala. Sinusukat ito sa joule."});

ADDART({id:"thermo",t:"Heat and Thermodynamics",c:"Science",i:"🌡️",w:"Thermodynamics",r:["energyphys","states","steam","atom"],
p:[["Heat on the move","Heat is energy that flows from hotter things to colder things."],["Temperature","Temperature measures how fast the particles in a substance are moving."],["The laws of thermodynamics","Four laws describe how heat and energy behave, from engines to stars."]],
l:"Thermodynamics is the branch of physics that studies heat, temperature and energy and how they change. It explains why ice melts, how engines and refrigerators work and why heat always flows from warm objects to cool ones. It grew in the 1800s from efforts to improve steam engines, and its laws are among the most universal in science, applying to everything from living cells to black holes.",
s:[["Heat and temperature","All matter is made of particles that are always moving. Temperature measures their average energy of motion: in hot things they move faster. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference. A bathtub of warm water holds more heat energy than a cup of boiling water, even though its temperature is lower."],["How heat travels","Heat moves in three ways. In conduction, it passes through materials as particles bump into each other, as when a metal spoon warms in hot soup. In convection, warm fluids rise and cool ones sink, carrying heat, as in boiling water or the atmosphere. In radiation, heat travels as infrared light, even through empty space, as from the Sun."],["Temperature scales","The Celsius scale sets water’s freezing point at 0 degrees and boiling point at 100 degrees at sea level. The Fahrenheit scale, used mainly in the United States, puts them at 32 and 212. Scientists use the kelvin scale, which starts at absolute zero, minus 273.15 degrees Celsius, the coldest possible temperature."],["The laws","The first law says energy is conserved: heat added to a system goes into raising its energy or doing work. The second law says that heat flows naturally from hot to cold and that disorder, called entropy, tends to increase. The third law says absolute zero can never quite be reached. A zeroth law states that objects at the same temperature are in balance."]],
k:[["Absolute zero","0 kelvin, −273.15 °C"],["Water boils","100 °C at sea level"],["Ways heat travels","Conduction, convection, radiation"],["Key concept","Entropy"],["Founders","Carnot, Clausius, Kelvin, Boltzmann"]],
d:[["1712","Thomas Newcomen builds a working steam engine"],["1824","Sadi Carnot publishes his study of heat engines"],["1848","Lord Kelvin proposes an absolute temperature scale"],["1850","Rudolf Clausius states the second law"],["1877","Ludwig Boltzmann links entropy to probability"],["1995","Scientists create a Bose–Einstein condensate near absolute zero"]],
s2:[["Entropy and the arrow of time","Entropy is a measure of disorder, or of how many ways the particles in a system can be arranged. The second law says that in an isolated system entropy never decreases. That is why a broken egg never reassembles itself and why time seems to flow in one direction. Ludwig Boltzmann connected entropy to the statistics of particles."],["Engines","A heat engine, such as a car engine or power station, turns heat into work by letting heat flow from a hot source to a cold one. Sadi Carnot showed in 1824 that no engine can be perfectly efficient: some heat must always be released to the cold side. The bigger the temperature difference, the more efficient the engine can be."],["Refrigerators and heat pumps","A refrigerator moves heat from its cold inside to the warmer room, which needs energy, supplied by electricity. It works by compressing and expanding a special fluid that absorbs heat when it evaporates. Heat pumps use the same idea to warm homes by drawing heat from outdoor air or the ground, even in cold weather."],["Expansion","Most materials expand when heated and contract when cooled, because their particles move more and take up more space. Bridges and railway tracks have gaps to allow for this. Water is unusual: it expands when it freezes, which is why ice floats and pipes can burst in winter."],["Near absolute zero","At very low temperatures, matter behaves strangely. Helium becomes a superfluid that can flow uphill and through tiny cracks. In 1995 physicists cooled atoms to billionths of a degree above absolute zero, creating a Bose–Einstein condensate, a new state of matter in which atoms act together as one."],["Heat and climate","Thermodynamics helps explain Earth’s climate. The Sun warms the surface, which gives off infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases, such as carbon dioxide and water vapor, absorb some of it and keep the planet warm. Adding more greenhouse gases traps more heat, raising global temperatures."]],
sp:["Why a metal bench feels colder","On a cold morning, a metal bench feels much colder than a wooden one, even though both are the same temperature. Metal is a good conductor, so it draws heat away from your hand quickly, while wood is a poor conductor and does not. Our skin senses how fast heat leaves it, not the actual temperature of the object."],
g:[["Temperature","A measure of how hot or cold something is"],["Entropy","A measure of disorder in a system"],["Conduction","Heat transfer through direct contact"],["Absolute zero","The lowest possible temperature, 0 kelvin"]],
f:["Absolute zero is −273.15 °C.","Water expands when it freezes.","Heat always flows naturally from hot to cold."],
fun:"Near absolute zero, liquid helium can creep up the walls of its container.",
tl:"Ang thermodynamics ay sangay ng pisika na nag-aaral ng init, temperatura at enerhiya. Ipinaliliwanag nito kung bakit dumadaloy ang init mula sa mainit patungo sa malamig at kung paano gumagana ang mga makina at refrigerator."});

ADDART({id:"waves",t:"Waves",c:"Science",i:"🌊",w:"Wave",r:["sound","light","tsunami","quantum","gravwaves"],
p:[["Energy on the move","A wave is a disturbance that carries energy from one place to another without carrying matter along with it."],["Many kinds","Sound, light, ocean waves, earthquake waves and radio are all waves."],["Measured by wavelength","Waves are described by their wavelength, frequency and amplitude."]],
l:"A wave is a repeating disturbance that transfers energy through a material or through space. When a wave passes, the material it travels through moves back and forth or up and down, but does not travel along with the wave. Waves are everywhere: ripples on a pond, sound in the air, light from the Sun, radio signals, the shaking of earthquakes. Understanding waves is key to music, communication, medicine and much of modern technology.",
s:[["Parts of a wave","The wavelength is the distance between two crests, or high points. The frequency is how many waves pass a point each second, measured in hertz. The amplitude is the height of the wave, which tells how much energy it carries. The speed of a wave equals its wavelength multiplied by its frequency."],["Transverse and longitudinal","In transverse waves, such as waves on a string or light, the disturbance moves at right angles to the direction the wave travels. In longitudinal waves, such as sound, particles move back and forth in the same direction as the wave, forming compressions and stretched-out regions."],["Mechanical waves","Mechanical waves need a material to travel through. Sound travels through air, water and solids, but not through empty space. Earthquakes send seismic waves through the rock of the Earth; by studying how they travel, scientists have learned about Earth’s layers deep inside."],["Electromagnetic waves","Light, radio waves, microwaves, infrared, ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays are electromagnetic waves, made of changing electric and magnetic fields. They need no material and can travel through space at the speed of light, about 300,000 kilometers per second. They differ only in wavelength."]],
k:[["Wavelength","Distance between two crests"],["Frequency","Waves per second, in hertz (Hz)"],["Speed of sound in air","About 343 m/s"],["Speed of light","About 300,000 km/s"],["Main types","Mechanical and electromagnetic"]],
d:[["c. 500 BCE","Pythagoras studies the vibrations of strings"],["1678","Christiaan Huygens proposes the wave theory of light"],["1801","Thomas Young’s double-slit experiment shows light behaves as a wave"],["1865","James Clerk Maxwell predicts electromagnetic waves"],["1887","Heinrich Hertz produces radio waves"],["1924","Louis de Broglie proposes that particles behave like waves"]],
s2:[["Interference","When two waves meet, they combine. If crests line up with crests, the waves add up and grow larger; if crests meet troughs, they cancel out. In 1801 Thomas Young shone light through two narrow slits and saw stripes of light and dark, proving that light behaves as a wave. Noise-canceling headphones use interference to cancel out unwanted sound."],["Reflection, refraction and diffraction","Waves bounce off surfaces, which is reflection, as in an echo or a mirror. They bend when they pass from one material to another at an angle, which is refraction, which is why a straw looks bent in water. They spread out around obstacles and through gaps, which is diffraction, which lets us hear sounds around corners."],["The Doppler effect","When a source of waves moves toward you, the waves bunch up and their frequency rises; when it moves away, they spread out and the frequency falls. That is why an ambulance siren sounds higher as it approaches and lower as it passes. Astronomers use the same effect with light to measure how fast stars and galaxies move."],["Resonance","Every object has natural frequencies at which it vibrates easily. If you push it at that frequency, the vibrations grow large; this is resonance. It lets a singer shatter a glass and makes musical instruments ring. Engineers design bridges and buildings to avoid dangerous resonance with wind or earthquakes."],["Waves in technology","Radio, television, mobile phones and Wi-Fi send information using electromagnetic waves. Ultrasound scanners use high-frequency sound to make images inside the body. Microwave ovens use microwaves to heat food, and sonar uses sound waves to find objects underwater."],["Waves and particles","In the early 1900s, physicists discovered that light also behaves like a stream of particles called photons, and that particles such as electrons also behave like waves. This wave–particle duality is a central idea of quantum mechanics and is used in electron microscopes."]],
sp:["Ocean waves","Most ocean waves are made by wind blowing over the water. Although the waves travel across oceans, the water itself mostly moves in circles, rising and falling as the wave passes. Near the shore, the bottom slows the wave, it grows taller and topples over as a breaker. Tsunamis are different: they are caused by earthquakes or landslides and move the whole depth of the ocean."],
g:[["Wavelength","The distance between two wave crests"],["Frequency","The number of waves passing a point each second"],["Amplitude","The height of a wave, related to its energy"],["Hertz","The unit of frequency: one wave per second"]],
f:["Sound cannot travel through empty space.","Light travels about a million times faster than sound in air.","The Doppler effect makes a siren’s pitch change as it passes."],
fun:"Sound travels about four times faster in water than in air.",
tl:"Ang alon ay paggalaw na nagdadala ng enerhiya mula sa isang lugar patungo sa iba. Alon ang tunog, liwanag, radyo at alon sa dagat. Sinusukat ito sa wavelength, frequency at amplitude."});

;
/* data/physics2-2.js */
// Physics articles, part 2.2: fluids and pressure, and time and clocks.
ADDART({id:"fluids",t:"Pressure and Fluids",c:"Science",i:"💧",w:"Fluid mechanics",r:["states","flight","ships","grav"],
p:[["Liquids and gases","A fluid is any substance that can flow, including liquids such as water and gases such as air."],["Pressure","Pressure is force spread over an area, and fluids push in every direction."],["Floating and flying","Fluids explain why ships float, why balloons rise and how airplanes stay in the air."]],
l:"Fluid mechanics is the study of how liquids and gases behave when they are still and when they move. Fluids push on everything they touch, creating pressure that increases with depth. Their behavior explains why ships float, how hydraulic machines lift heavy loads, how airplanes fly, how blood flows through our bodies and how weather systems move across the planet. Key ideas come from scientists such as Archimedes, Blaise Pascal and Daniel Bernoulli.",
s:[["What is pressure?","Pressure is the force pushing on a surface divided by the area of that surface. A sharp knife cuts well because its force is concentrated on a tiny area, while snowshoes spread a person’s weight so they do not sink. Pressure is measured in pascals; air pressure at sea level is about 101,000 pascals."],["Air pressure","The air above us has weight and presses down on everything, about 10 metric tons on every square meter at sea level. We do not feel it because the pressure inside our bodies pushes outward equally. Air pressure falls with height, which is why ears pop in airplanes and climbers on high mountains need extra oxygen."],["Pressure in liquids","In a liquid, pressure increases with depth because of the weight of liquid above. At the bottom of the deepest ocean trench, the pressure is more than 1,000 times that at the surface. Divers must rise slowly from deep water to let their bodies adjust, and deep-sea submersibles need very thick walls."],["Archimedes and buoyancy","According to legend, the Greek scientist Archimedes realized while stepping into a bath that an object in a fluid is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of fluid it displaces. This upward push is called buoyancy. An object floats if it displaces its own weight of fluid before it is fully submerged."]],
k:[["Unit of pressure","Pascal (Pa)"],["Sea-level air pressure","About 101,000 Pa (1 atmosphere)"],["Key principles","Archimedes, Pascal, Bernoulli"],["Fluids include","Liquids and gases"],["Deepest ocean pressure","Over 1,000 times surface pressure"]],
d:[["c. 250 BCE","Archimedes describes buoyancy"],["1643","Evangelista Torricelli invents the barometer"],["1648","Pascal shows air pressure falls with height"],["1738","Daniel Bernoulli publishes Hydrodynamica"],["1783","First hot-air balloon flights with people"],["1903","The Wright brothers make the first powered airplane flight"]],
s2:[["How ships float","A steel ship floats even though steel is denser than water, because its hull encloses a large volume of air and displaces a great deal of water. If enough water rushes into the hull, the ship becomes heavier than the water it displaces and sinks. Submarines dive and rise by filling tanks with water or air."],["Pascal’s principle","Blaise Pascal discovered that pressure applied to a fluid in a closed container is passed equally to every part of it. Hydraulic machines, such as car brakes, car lifts and excavators, use this: a small push on a narrow piston creates a large force on a wide one, letting a person lift a car with ease."],["Bernoulli’s principle","Daniel Bernoulli showed in 1738 that when a fluid speeds up, its pressure drops. This helps explain how airplane wings produce lift, why a shower curtain is pulled inward when the water runs and how spray bottles work. Lift on a wing comes from it turning the airflow downward and from the pressure differences this creates."],["Measuring air pressure","In 1643 Evangelista Torricelli, a student of Galileo, filled a glass tube with mercury and turned it upside down in a dish. The mercury fell to a height of about 76 centimeters, held up by the pressure of the air. This was the first barometer. Falling pressure often warns of storms, and very low pressure marks the center of typhoons and hurricanes."],["Viscosity","Viscosity is a fluid’s resistance to flowing. Honey and lava are very viscous, while water and air are not. Engine oil is designed to stay slippery enough when cold and thick enough when hot to protect engine parts. Some fluids, such as a mix of cornstarch and water, become thicker when squeezed quickly."],["Fluids in the body","The heart pumps blood through blood vessels under pressure. Blood pressure is measured with two numbers: the higher pressure when the heart beats and the lower pressure between beats. Our lungs work by changing air pressure inside the chest, so air flows in and out."]],
sp:["Eureka!","According to a story told by the Roman writer Vitruvius, King Hiero II of Syracuse asked Archimedes to find out whether his new crown was pure gold. While bathing, Archimedes noticed the water level rise and realized he could measure the crown’s volume by the water it displaced, and so compare its density with gold. He supposedly ran through the streets shouting Eureka, I have found it."],
g:[["Fluid","A substance that can flow, such as a liquid or gas"],["Pressure","Force divided by the area it acts on"],["Buoyancy","The upward force a fluid exerts on an object in it"],["Viscosity","A fluid’s resistance to flowing"]],
f:["Air presses down with about 10 metric tons on every square meter at sea level.","Ships float because they displace their own weight of water.","Hydraulic machines can lift cars with a small push."],
fun:"Air presses on every square meter of the ground with a force about equal to the weight of 10 metric tons.",
tl:"Ang fluid ay anumang bagay na dumadaloy, gaya ng tubig at hangin. Ang presyon ay puwersang nakakalat sa isang lugar. Ipinaliliwanag ng fluid mechanics kung bakit lumulutang ang barko at lumilipad ang eroplano."});

ADDART({id:"timephys",t:"Time and Clocks",c:"Science",i:"⏱️",w:"Time",r:["relat","earthplanet","gps","atom","clocks"],
p:[["Measuring time","People have measured time for thousands of years using the Sun, the Moon, water, sand, pendulums and atoms."],["The second","Today the second is defined using vibrations of cesium atoms."],["Time is relative","Einstein showed that time passes at different rates depending on speed and gravity."]],
l:"Time is the ongoing sequence of events from the past through the present to the future. Measuring it accurately has shaped farming, navigation, science and modern life. Early people tracked days, months and years using the Sun, Moon and stars; later came sundials, water clocks, mechanical clocks and pendulums. Today atomic clocks keep time so precisely that they would lose less than a second in millions or even billions of years, and they make technologies such as GPS possible.",
s:[["Natural clocks","The day comes from Earth’s spin, the month roughly from the Moon’s cycle of phases, about 29.5 days, and the year from Earth’s orbit around the Sun. Ancient Egyptians divided day and night into 12 hours each, and Babylonian astronomers used a counting system based on 60, which gives us 60 minutes in an hour and 60 seconds in a minute."],["Early clocks","Sundials told time by the shadow of a stick or blade. Water clocks, used in Egypt, Babylon, China and Greece, measured time by water flowing steadily into or out of a vessel. In 1088 Su Song in China built a huge astronomical clock tower driven by water. Sandglasses and candles marked with lines were also used."],["Mechanical clocks","Mechanical clocks with weights and gears appeared in European towers in the late 1200s. In 1656 Christiaan Huygens built the first pendulum clock, which was far more accurate. Spring-driven watches followed. In the 1700s John Harrison built marine chronometers accurate enough at sea for sailors to work out their longitude."],["Atomic clocks","Atoms of each element absorb and give off light at exact frequencies. Since 1967, the second has been defined as 9,192,631,770 vibrations of radiation from cesium-133 atoms. Atomic clocks based on this idea are the world’s timekeepers, and newer optical clocks using other atoms are even more precise."]],
k:[["Unit","The second (s)"],["Definition","9,192,631,770 oscillations of cesium-133 radiation"],["Most accurate clocks","Optical atomic clocks"],["Time standard","Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)"],["Time zones","Based on 24 zones, with many local variations"]],
d:[["c. 1500 BCE","Egyptians use sundials and water clocks"],["1088","Su Song builds his astronomical clock tower in China"],["1656","Christiaan Huygens builds the first pendulum clock"],["1761","John Harrison’s chronometer proves accurate at sea"],["1884","Greenwich is chosen as the prime meridian"],["1967","The second is defined using cesium atoms"]],
s2:[["Time zones","When railways and telegraphs linked distant cities in the 1800s, local times based on the Sun caused confusion. In 1884 an international conference chose Greenwich, in London, as the prime meridian, the starting line for longitude and world time. The world was later divided into time zones, though many countries set their own, and some use half-hour or quarter-hour offsets."],["Time and relativity","Einstein’s theory of relativity showed that time is not the same for everyone. A moving clock ticks more slowly than one at rest, and a clock in stronger gravity ticks more slowly than one in weaker gravity. These effects are tiny in daily life but have been measured with atomic clocks on airplanes and even on clocks a few centimeters higher than others."],["GPS needs relativity","GPS satellites carry atomic clocks. Because they move fast, their clocks run slow by about 7 microseconds a day; because they are in weaker gravity, they run fast by about 45 microseconds a day. Without correcting for the combined difference of about 38 microseconds a day, GPS positions would drift by kilometers each day."],["Leap seconds","Earth’s spin is slowly and irregularly changing, so atomic time and time based on Earth’s rotation drift apart. Since 1972, leap seconds have been added from time to time to keep them together, which can disrupt computers. In 2022 international scientists voted to stop adding leap seconds by 2035."],["Calendars","Calendars organize days into years. The Julian calendar, introduced by Julius Caesar in 46 BCE, had a leap year every four years. The Gregorian calendar of 1582 fixed a small error by skipping some leap years. Many cultures also keep other calendars, such as the Islamic lunar calendar and the Chinese lunisolar calendar."],["What is time?","Physicists and philosophers still debate what time really is. Why does it seem to flow only forward? Many link the arrow of time to the increase of entropy, or disorder, described by thermodynamics. In relativity, time and space are joined into a single four-dimensional spacetime."]],
sp:["The longitude prize","For centuries, sailors could find their latitude from the Sun and stars, but not their longitude, causing many shipwrecks. In 1714 the British government offered a prize for a solution. The clockmaker John Harrison spent decades building marine clocks; his H4, a large pocket watch, kept time so well on a voyage to Jamaica in 1761–1762 that it allowed longitude to be found within a few kilometers."],
g:[["Second","The basic unit of time"],["Atomic clock","A clock that keeps time using the vibrations of atoms"],["Time zone","A region that uses the same standard time"],["Leap second","An extra second added to keep clocks in step with Earth’s rotation"]],
f:["The second is defined by the vibrations of cesium atoms.","GPS satellites must correct for relativity.","Greenwich was chosen as the prime meridian in 1884."],
fun:"A clock on a high shelf ticks very slightly faster than one on the floor, because gravity is a little weaker there.",
tl:"Ang panahon ay sinusukat ng tao gamit ang Araw, Buwan, tubig, pendulum at ngayon ay atomo. Ang segundo ay batay sa pag-vibrate ng mga atomo ng cesium, at ipinakita ni Einstein na ang takbo ng panahon ay nag-iiba ayon sa bilis at grabidad."});

